      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>cittttas to thr</head>
        <p>cittttas to thr</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. THE EDITOR of "The Examiner" invites corr.. LETTERS must in all cases be accompanied by the epondence on all subjects of public interest, name and address of the writer. LETTERS should be brief and pointed. TheirCommuslcAnoes received early in the week— object must be to impart or seek information. except urgent matters—can always be given not abuse. the best attention.</p>
        <p>THE DAYLIGHT SAYING BILL.</p>
        <p>you kindly permit me to draw attention to the fact that if this Bill had been passed, its operation would commence on Sunday next, at 2 a.m.? Sunset would not take place, before 8 o'clock on Monday, and workers would have an additional hour of sunlight for reerea-1 tion after work every evening until the third Sunday in September.</p>
        <p>There would be no difficulty in the morning, because there would be as much daylight at six by the clock as there was on March 24th at six o'clock.</p>
        <p>The Bill is supported by 720 City Corporations and Town and District Councils, as well as by hundreds of well-known societies, associations, d/c. In the counties of Bedford, Berks, Bucking- ham, Dorset, Oxford, Sussex, Westmorland and Wilts, every town having a population of 10,000 or upwards has passed a resolution in favour of the Bill, and in eight counties every such town but one has passed such a resolution. .</p>
        <p>WM. WILLETT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>FLETCHER, RUSSELL'S STRIKE.</head>
        <p>FLETCHER, RUSSELL'S STRIKE.</p>
        <p>Sir,--In reply to "Regular Reader's" letter of Saturday last, I should like to know what he calls a silly dispute. Does he for one moment think that, being banded together through our trade unions, we are always going to stand on one side and be the dictated to? Who are the people that are responsible for the profits of the firm which have not been so great this last six weeks? Any sane, levelheaded man who thinks' for himself knows that it is the employed. As we have only our labour to sell, we must see we get the best value possible for it. Well, we being clear-thinking men, know that the best course to obtain this is to join a trade union, and furthermore to see we are'recognised. Why should we be told who to have for our Leaders? If we think these tub-thumping agita- tors, who have been telling us ever since they started tub-thumping of the sufferings of the women and children, are the best leaders we can have, aro we not right in asking them to lead us? Or does "Regular Reader" think it would simplify matters if they chose a director of the firm? Why do the firm refuse to see these tub-thumpers? Is it because they are not capable of meeting them, or because they have not got a clear case to put before us? What I would the firm say if we were to start to tell them who should serve on—the board of direc- tors? They would think we had gone insane. Who is "Regular Reader" that he thinks we are foolish enough to let two or three men tell us when to finish work and lead us as lambs to the slaughter? Is he trying to throw dust in the people's eyes, or has he just awakened to find that women and children are suffering? Let me tell him we don't intend to return to work until the firm are prepared to take us as we are and recognise Us. Then we will put an end to one of the most solid strikes that Warrington has witnessed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>NO BUNKUM.</head>
        <p>NO BUNKUM.</p>
        <p>GREENGROCERS, WHY NOT ORGANISE 9</p>
        <p>-; 'I, •</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>. ' ; ' 1 - ,</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>`t,</p>
        <p>,</p>
        <p>Sir,—Nearly all trades or professions, with a few exceptions, are effectively organised. In these days of keen competition (very often un- justly so) it has not only become fashionable, but absolutely necessary, to organise. The ever- increasing lack of confidence in each other is the cause of this onward rush which is gathering up every tributary in its mad career, until now we see a mighty, rushing torrent of public opinion, with its ever-increasing power, spurred onwards by the word " Organise!" " Organise !" But, unfortunately, for the greengrocer and fruiterer, this mighty torrent has, so far, failed to arouse these traders from their lethargic con- dition. So far, the suction of this mighty tor- rent has failed to draw our sympathy. For what reason it is difficult to say, seeing that there is not a more easily affected business than ours. It has so much to depend upon, both at home and abroad. We depend much upon fav- ourable weather conditions. If there is a boun- tiful harvest we cannot give our goods away, so to speak; if it is otherwise we cannot obtain a supply at a fair price. We are not like the ironmonger, able to rub oil over his goods, or be able to stock a safe line which does not deteriorate by keeping. Therefore, instead of talking about organising we are just 50 years behind the time doing it. Because we have been so long in doing it, is, however, no reason why woo should continue as we are any longer. to; the time bag arrived when every one of us, must. combine for our individual protection again q unfair competition in our local market, j the toning up of our business, and for an assur-, ance of reasonable profits for the goods we sell. Otherwise we shall be swamped; then we shall be laughed at for our folly. Even now our retail trade is held up to ridicule by strangers coming into our midst, who tell us that we are " simply feeding the public," and we are just in that position that,' we cannot very well deny the statement. •</p>
        <p>My sole object is to get all retail greengrocers and fruiterers either to form a Retail Traders' Society, or else become affiliated whit some other organisation which meets our special re- quirements. • WALTER BYFIELD, Fruiterer and Greengrocer, 2, Gorsey-lane.</p>
        <p>SAVE MONEY, TIME AND TROUBLE By wearing our</p>
        <p>VERY LATEST TIES.</p>
