        <head>COMPULSORY SERVICE IN AUSTRALIA,</head>
        <p>COMPULSORY SERVICE IN AUSTRALIA,</p>
        <p>Sir,—Captain Crosfield evidently desires to be in the fashion—politically—hence his challenge to Mr. Barry to prove his case against Conscrip- tion in Australia before a committee of three Warrington citizens. With all due respect to Captain Crosfield and the tribunal he wishes to set up, and to those who have adjudicated for us in other matters, it is about time that those who object to having their politics expressed for them by proxy, should make a protest against such tactics. The average man would, I venture to think, express himself in this way when asked to give an opinion upon the correspond- ence: "Well, Mr. Barry has lived all his life in Australia and has first-hand knowledge of the working of compulsory military training in that country, and he ought to know more than a man who has, for all I know, never seen Australia, and has only second-hand knowledge to work on. As for Mr. Barry coming to this country to mislead us, I don't see what he would gain by doing that." And, to say the least, it is hardly playing the game for Captain Crosfield to sug- gest that Mr. Barry is going up and down the country misleaditig the people. I hold nci brief for Mr. Barry. The only knowledge I have of him is his manly account of workings of conscription in Australia at the ' Peace Meeting in the Co-operative Hall, but I venture to think that when he his to give An account of his mission in this country) and he tells our cousins "down under" that a promin- ent member of the National Service League in Warrington suggested that he was not, telling the truth, they will have a very poor opinion of our controversial methods. Captain Crosfield's desire to contribute• ,R5O to the Works Bowling Association, that is, if the committee decides in Mr. Barry's favour, is remarkably like what the Americans call an appeal to the Almighty Dollar in a game of poker. When an American is playing poker, which is, by the way, a game of bluff, he will, so as to frighten his opponent, stake a large sum of money upon a poor hand of cards and thereby win when his cards are inferior to his opponent's. That is known as an appeal to the Almighty Dollar, and can only be played suc- cessfully by the man who has a big pile of dollars. Captain Crosfield is evidently playing poker' with us. The spirit of Captain Crosfield's letter is worthy of some of the officers of the Curragh Camp.</p>
        <p>GEORGE TOULIIIN.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Captain Crosfield makes an offer of .250, to be given to the Warrington Works Bowling Association, if I can prove the three points placed before your readers. May I state that I am a member of the Society of Friends, and as such will have nothing to do with sporting offers. 1. The Responsibility of the Labour party.</p>
        <p>My contention is that though the -Labour party passed the Defence Act, they were not the authors of the measure, nor did the Labour movement know what the Act contained. If the Labour party knew what the Act contained, why is there such widespread discontent? This week's mail brings me a report from Sydney to the effect that the New South Wales Labour party has demanded the appointment of a Royal I Commission to investigate the whole question of the Defence Act—its interference with citizen rights and conscience, the age of training, the expenditure and harsh. conditions. The West Australian Labour party has passed 'a' resolution to the effect that if the Defence Act be allowed to remain on the Statute Book in its present form, it will mean a most serious menace to the whole working class movement. The fact that a large number of powerful trade unions have also condemned compulsory military training, all since the Act was adopted, proves my point that the Labour movement did not know the mean- ing of the Defence Act. Apparently Captain Crosfield has little knowledge of the inner work- ing of the Australian Labour party. I have worked in conjunction with it, and served on its committees, so I know what I am talking about! The compulsory clauses of the Defence Act came into force in 1911. Lord Kitchener visited Australia in 1909. A scheme was propounded,</p>
        <p>its principles being in conforn4i&amp; with the Defence Acts, 1903-9. With the exception of the vital point of compulsion, these Acts were based on the voluntary principle. The adoption of Lord Kitchener's recommendations necessitated further amending the Acts. These Acts trans- formed the voluntary system into one of com- pulsion. Let me prove Lord Kitchener's associa- tion with the Defence Act by reference to the "Melbourne Age." This newspaper was the leading, organ asking for compulsory military training. It now loudly demands modifications. Alt these ,quotations are front the "Age" - lieteniber 16th, 1913 "The public • feeling aroused over our grossly excessive expenditure on military defence has enforced upon the Gov- ernment the necessity of keeping well within the measure of the Kitchener scheme, instead of ex- ceeding by at least ,21,000,000 in the second year of its operation."</p>
        <p>"The Age," December 17th (Report of Parlia- mentary debate): " Mr. Manifold, M.H.R.; charged the Opposition (the Labour party) with having cordially supported the naval and de- fence schemes of Admiral Henderson and Lord Kitchener, and with now opposing them. He thought the Kitchener scheme meant getting too many into the citizen forces, and some steps would have to be taken to limit the number of cadets. The military and naval colleges were tsoqing too much."</p>
        <p>"The Age," December 24th: "Lord Kitchener estimated that when his schema was in full operation, in the seventh year of its existence, the annual cost to the country would be £1,884,000. We should not over-state the posi- tion in the smallest degree if we maintained that the military system this year will cost the country ,t 2,750,000 or nearly £1,000.000 more than the estimate framed by Lord Kitchener for the completed plan." "The Age," November 28th: "Acting on the counsel of two British experts, Admiral Hender- son and Lord Kitchener, we have laid the foundations of two great defence establishments. We accepted the experts' schemes in 17,10h0. Both</p>
        <p>senemes largely exceeded in cost and in dimen- sions the modest plans ,which. we believe would meet our national requirements. • We were rather staggered, indeed, when the Henderson and Kitchener schemes were first laid before us, because we had never aspired to launch out in the ambitious manner suggested." "The Age" is one of the most important and powerful papers in Australia. Let me now call in an independent critic, Mr. Henry (son of the late W. T.) Stead, editor of the Australian "Review of Reviews." In the September issue of last year he says: "Actually, therefore, Australians as a whole have had no opportunity whatever of either approving or disapproving the Defence Act, and the policy which produced it. One cannot blind oneself to the fact that whilst the Liberal party claims the introduction of the measure, and the Labour party passed it, the Act is not the work of either. It resembles the Act in force in New Zealand and the proposals of the National .Ser- vice League at home too closely for anyone to believe that it was evolved locally!' 2. The Verdict of the Last General Election. Captain Crosfield demands of me the impos- sible! I rather suspect that he knew it when he made the request. I stated that 20 candi- dates at the last election were opposed to com- pulsory military training. In reply, Captain Crosfield asks me to produce at least ten of their election addresses ! Australia is a few thousand miles away, and I don't usually travel about with political candidates' election addresses. Besides this is not the point. I did not say 20 candidates stated in their election addresses thetlia that lastthey e election no pttoesed to },r e edemmil military Leaguetla jai:ih gd. tAh et candidates of all parties—Liberal, Labour, Inde- pendent and Socialist—their attitude towards the compulsory clauses of ' the Defence Act. Twenty candidates replied that they were op- posed to compulSory military training. I have written to Australia fully on this point, and I will send the reply direct to your paper. 3. The Accuracy of My Figures. Captain Crosfield challenges me to prove that the figures 122,000 represent the number of lads in training in Junior Cadets, Senior Cadets and Citizen Forces. My figures were issued by the Defence Department, and I published them in the way I received them. These figures. cover Senior Cadets and Citizen Forces. It would be superfluous and ridiculous to include the Junior Cadets, simply/ because the Junior Cadets are +riot under military law, nor are they liable to prosecution in connection with the Defence Department.. So •in 'dealing With the prosecutions, it was only fair to give the figures of the prosecutions and the number actually liable. However, the figures were issued by the Defence Department, and the re- sponsibility rests with them. I did not say that there had been 21,300 prosecutions of different people; what I said was that 122,000 are in train- ing, and the prosecutions to date amount to 21,300, and 3,400 have been sent to places of mili- tary detention.</p>
        <p>JOHN W. BARRY. 21, Beech-road, Horfield, Bristol.</p>
        <p>graphs by Parkinson. MISS GLADYS ADAMS, - As Beatrice in "Much ,Ado. About Nothing."</p>
