      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>CATHOLIC LECTURES.</head>
        <p>THE CATHOLIC LECTURES.</p>
        <p>fully sympathise with the desire of "Anxious" to see a course of lectures given on the Protestant point of view of Christianity, and woulcl.like to see Mr. Ogden, or someone else, take the Matter up. For my own part I have no fear of Protestantism losing ground or going under, and I may point out that there is at least one place in the town where good, sound Protestantism is preached on evangelical. lines. I mean Emmanuel Episcopal Reformed Church. Perhaps the Pastor, Mr. Jaye, might be pre- vailed upon to take up the question of lectures, as I know he is pretty well furnished with in- formation on the subject; and, further, has a very near and dear relative on active duty in the cause of Protestantism in Spain. ' PHIL SQUOD.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The work of Easter Week interfered with my intention to reply last week to your corre- spondent "J.D." That must be my sole excuse. Regarding your correspondent's identity, he need fear no malice were his name revealed. There is no excommunication he need fear, nor do I think my "parishioners" would ostracise him. Concerning any freedom of thought on the part of the clergy of the Anglican Church, I would merely reiterate what I stated in my pre- vious letter, namely: It is more heroic to have a difference of opinion in any community as the, result of 'honest intellectual 'effort than the' dead, dull, monotonous uniformity that is born of a blind mental allegiance to any Pope, whether at Rome, or, as it transpired in history, in France, when one Pope cursed what the other Pope blessed. A man was granted his brains for use, whether in the realm of science or religion. I am to discuss ."The Real Presence." " Confession glad Your correspondent finally decided to God alone" is a Protestant maxim, and one would welcome a discussion upon Scripture warrant, but claim your space for it. Just now I have no intention to Your correspondent has not disposed of my argument concerning the method of administer- ing the Communion in the Early Church. The New Testament Practice, long ere Augustine began his ministrations, was the selection of any piece of bread, the choice of any bottle of wine. There was never any assumption that the bread was the body, or the wine was the actual blood of Jesus. If Transubstantiation was credited even by the Early Church, how is it that we had to wait, until the ninth century, when a monk of Corbey, one Paschasius Radbert, expressed it in a treatise? How is it that Pope Hildebrand was very loth, even in the eleventh century, to adopt the view, if Transubstantiation .was a settled belief in the Early Church? The name by which it is now known in the Roman Catholic Church was not found or applied to the doctrine until even two centuries later, and since then have arisen such terms and conceptions as "The Adoration of the Host," "The Reservation of the Host," for local purposes, &amp;c. Why should it take eleven centuries to discover that Christ's words, "This is My body," were to be taken literally, and in the corporeal sense, and even the combined intel- ligence of the Church two centuries more to give a name to it? The Church of the Acts of the Apostles and of Paul's epistles was a Church that treated the Lord's Supper as simply as the Free Churches treat it to-day. Where, in the whole of the New Testament, is authority given to an individual who answers in kind to the Roman Catholic priests to utter "hoc est corpus meum," and immediately what was bread becomes the body of the Lord Jesus 'Christ? There was no corps in the Early Church akin to the Roman priesthood. "Where two or three are gathered together in My name, there am I in the midst of them" (Matt. xviii. 20). There is no warrant for a priesthood in that text anything akin to the Roman priest- hood, or the possession of such awful power as turning bread into corporeal body. - Concerning the words used, no one disputes . that the words spoken'were "This is My body, this is My blood." But were all Christ's words spoken literally? Luke xvi. gives us a very graphic description of hell; but none save an obsolete theology would describe it as a literal fire. Yet the utterance was the utterance of Christ, and surly it must suggest itself to your correspondent "J.D." that it is not thecOrporeal Christ which gives life and power to the spirit of man. "'What is of the flesh is flesh; ‘what is of the spirit is spirit," and spiritual sutten- ance is given to men by the spiritual indwelling of Christ. In conclusion, allow me to remind "J.D." that he does his Church no service; neither does he convince any Warrington public of the Catholicity of his Church when he suggests there is no spiritual benefit to be derived from a Baptist Communion service. I have not scouted "the Communion" he loves. I believe he gains spiritual benefit from it. I take his word for it. I would suggest, Mr. Editor, that " J.D." read 1 Corinthians xiii., Revised Version, as always, when he quotes Scripture.</p>
        <p>H. OGDEN. Baptist Church, Golbarne-street.</p>
        <p>In "Much Ado About Nothing" one of the leading ladies is Miss Gladys Adams, who has had considerable experience in Shakespearean plays, but this is the first time she has partici- pated in comedy, but she has distinct gifts in that direction. Miss Adams made success in "The :Verdi ant of Venice," and later as Lady</p>
        <p>he will be Dr. Caius. In these two characters he should be seen at his best, as there is humour for him to handle.</p>
        <p>Mr. Charles Law takes his usual active part in the production. He is Benedick in "Much Ado," and Page in the other play. He is well known as a Shakespearean actor and has already been in "Mach!zdh;" "Richard the Third," and other plays;•lmit•now changes into a fresh type of character. Mr. Leslie L. Langford will be a success as Ford in "Merry Wives." • Falstaff will be in the particularly Safe hands of Mr. Ryder Boys, who has earned almost national reputation in regard to this character.</p>
        <p>EFFECTIVE FEATURES. No efforts have been spared to make the features crisp and effective and the smallest details have been given attention. The music in the church scene in "Much Ado" will be rendered by the members of the Carlton Choral Society. Twenty of their members will be dressed as monks and will render the Latin chant.</p>
        <p>At the end of the last act in "Merry Wives" twenty young girls from Messrs. Crosfield's will represent the fairies who punish Falstaff. The music in this scene has been selected from the works of Mendelssohn. Other music has been specially written by Mr. Tom Boothroyd. The scenery for both plays has been specially designed by Mr. T. Robinson, of Liverpool, who was scenic artist to Sir George Alexander when he produced "Much Ado" at St. James' Theatre, London. Every effort has been made to secure historical accuracy in the costumes, which have been provided by May's, of London.</p>
        <p>PROSPECTS BRIGHT. , "'Much Ado About Nothing" will be given lon Monday, Wednesday and Friday. "The Merry Wives of Windsor" will figure on the pro- gramme on Tuesday, Thursday and Saturday "The prospects are exceedingly bright," said Mr. W. Mason, the genial and most energetic business manager, to "The Examiner" repre- sentative, "and never had we so many patrons and honorary officials who are seeking to mak(i the show, a success.</p>
        <p>"We hope to have record opening nights an Monday and Tuesday, because of the facilities that have been given to the school children. W. offer them admission to the gallery at 2d. and the pit at 4d..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>Local Amateurs to Commemorate the Bard of Avon.</head>
        <p>The Local Amateurs to Commemorate the Bard of Avon.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION OF TWO COMEDIES.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Shakespearean Players com- memorate the Bard of Avon next week, when they will hold the boards of the Royal Court Theatre with two of the best known comedies, "Much Ado About Nothing" and "The Merry Wives of Windsor." These should be well worth seeing, as our local amateurs have made strenuous efforts to present the comedies in the best possible manner.</p>
        <p>It looks a heavy undertaking for amateurs, but the Warrington players are much superior to the usual grade of histrionic amateurs. They do • the work which they take in hand a new record. well, and this season they are likely to establish</p>
        <p>The great enthusiasm which prevails amongst the players is reflected in the energy they throw into the rehearsals, and no body of players could be found who give more attention to detail and the securing of the effects aimed at by Shakespeare. Mr. Charles Law, who is one of the keenest amongst this energetic body of players, informed " The Thraminer " represen- tative that the rehearsals had been most success- ful, and the plays would be presented on a</p>
        <p>Ann in "Richard the Third." She takes the part of Beatrice in this comedy, and in "Merry Wives" makes an ideal Mrs. Ford. ,</p>
        <p>Miss Milly Truslove, who formerly played in "Richard the Third;" is playing the very im- portant part of Hero in "Much Ado"; Miss Ellison will have the part of Annie Page in "The Merry Wives," and Miss Bessie Forshaw will enact the part of Mrs. Quickly, a part for which she is admirably suited.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <p>lavish scale. The company is a most representa- tive one, and all sections of the community , have contributed to the different casts. The casts remain as have been published pre- viously in "The Examiner," with the exception that the part of Mrs. Page in "The Merry Wives of Windsor" will be played by Miss ,Winifred Corney.</p>
        <p>SOME OF THE PERSONALITIES. The personnel is remarkably strong, and it is rare to find an amateur dramatic company able to build such a fine array of talent as that seen in either play on this occasion, and with such talented and experienced actors and actresses the representation of either comedy should be such as will Satisfy the most fastidious critic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>FEW OF THE ACTORS.</head>
        <p>A FEW OF THE ACTORS.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. W. H. Bfciwn, who is ,Don Pedro in "Much Ado," was very successful as Antony in "The Merchant of Venice," and also as .Hastings iii " Richard the Third." . Mr. John Lowe is one of the most popular members of the company, and he figures as Claudio in "Much Ado." He is excellently suited for the part. Mr. C. Daintith is Leonato in "Much Ado" and the, Host in "Merry Wives."</p>
        <p>To Mr. H. M. Ashton, the well-known local comedian, has been assigned the part of Dog- berry in "Much Ado," and in "Merry Wives"</p>
        <p>POULTRY PEN.</p>
        <p>Hints and Interesting Facts and Chat for Amateurs.- ' 1+4444,10144014•1444.14b14•19,14444•144.44.14</p>
        <p>"EXAMINER" SPECIAL.</p>
        <p>The tendency on the part of even compara- tively small amateur poultry-keepers to-day is to make a special feature Of some phase of the work. This has much to commend it.. The time was when the ordinary specialist was he who went in for showing. This is all very well as a hobby, but it proves most expensive, and the returns are not equal to the outlay, and thii places it beyond the means of the ordinary j amateur.</p>
        <p>Egg production is one of the strongest cards any amateur can play. The demand for eggs is steadily increasing. It does not necessarily mean that we Britishers are eating more. .America is now becoming one of the greatest of egg countries, and her supplies are now required for home markets and few come to this country as they did in past years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <p>"'Noe, • , ' '4444; 417" 4,4 „ 14,0, • • • .</p>
        <p>MR. CHARLES D. LAW, As Page in The Merry Wives'of Windsor."</p>
        <p>4</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>LIN EIS COUNCIL SCENE.</head>
        <p>LIN EIS COUNCIL SCENE.</p>
        <p>A ‘Vranule for Hours at GoThorne.</p>
        <p>The selection Of a chairman for the Golborne District Council led to ,a prblonged wrangle on Thursday ev ; —"mg, and the meeting adjourned in the early hours of yesterday without having made an appointment. The Liberals pressed the claims of, their nominee, Mr. A. Yates, while the Conservatives insisted that Mr. T. Robinson, the retiring Chair- man, should be re-elected to the•position. Mr. J. Naylor, on behalf of the Liberals, submitted' a proposal that if the Conservatives would agree to a principle of alternating chairmen they would ,consent to the election of Mr. Robinson this year. This, however, the Conervatives declined to do.</p>
        <p>Mr. Elliott -(Conservative) said he would sit there until next Friday before he would give way. The .discussion continued at intervals, and after the meeting had been in progress about two hours Mr. Whelan jocularly asked the Clerk what the closing hour might be. 'The Clerk: There is no. closing hour in the .orders.</p>
        <p>One 'member said he would have been setting</p>
        <p>potatoes had he known what was going to hap- pen. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>- Neither side would give way, and members indulged in smoking during the intervals of dis- cassion.</p>
        <p>The meeting began at 5.30, and at eight o'clock an adjournment took place for three-quarters of an hour, with a view to an arrangement. When, the Council re-assembled both sides were still obdurate. At 9.30 Mr. Yates said every member on that side was prepared to stay all night before he would give way.</p>
        <p>_ • Mr. Rutter (Conservative) : I will say The same for those on this side.</p>
        <p>Subsequently Mr. Prescott suggested they should go home and return at six o'clock in the morning and bring their dinners with them. Another Member: A very good idea. r Mr. Prescott: Yes, I think so. What do you say, Mr. Rutter?</p>
        <p>Mr. flutter: This a wait-and-see game-. At 1.30 on Friday morning the Council was still sitting, and there did not appear to be any sign of either side giving way. . As there did not seem to be any prospect of either side gaining a victory', and as one or two members had fallen asleep, it was apparent that the meeting would terminate sooner than was at one time expected.. At five minutes past two a proposal that the meeting sh6uld be adjourned until Thursday was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>EGGS TO BE DEARER.</head>
        <p>EGGS TO BE DEARER.</p>
        <p>With the growth of more luxurious living on 'the Continent a fresh market has been created there for eggs, and this ,in turn is reflected on the British market. Denmark is not likely to supply England with so many as in the past, and Germany is laying tribute to the Siberian eggs which only a few years ago came to glut the British market. '</p>
        <p>It is becoming evident that we Will have to depend more and more in the future upon our home producers, and prices are sure to advance through winter. As much as twopence was paid for a guaranteed new-laid egg last winter, and there are signs that prices may in the future advance to 3s. per dozen. Then the home poultry keeper will find that his hobby pays him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>WINTER LAYERS.</head>
        <p>WINTER LAYERS.</p>
        <p>If he is going to cater for this promising market it will be well to secure the- best type of winter layers. These were enumerated in the articles about the different breeds published during recent weeks, and it is still time enough to set sittings.</p>
        <p>IThe late i7dlickens, which can be.hatched up to the end of May, will be laying by November I or December, and should lay right up to the • spring. Thus they will be egg-producing at the period of the year when the market prices are at top level. To keep them up to laying pitch, the birds must be kept dry and warm. Some people line the inside of the hen houses with felt, and thus .afford warmth and Protection. Others install stoves and, hot water pipes to maintain an even 'temperature day and night. The results in the latter ease invariably prove excellent, but, of course, it is an expensive method, though those who have used it said it paid splendidly.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>SEPARATING CHICKENS FROM ADULTS.</head>
        <p>SEPARATING CHICKENS FROM ADULTS.</p>
        <p>The reason why' the dangers of overcrowding (says a writer. in " Farm.and Home ") are more acute at the present time is that, accommodation for more birds has to be provided. The breed- ing pens are still intact, and the old hens are in full lay, and consequently it would be unwise to,dispose of them. Ducklings for stock purposes and those or sale (hiring the nextlevi, weeks. are numerous. Geese and turkeys for consumption next Christmas are making their appearance, while in addition to these chickens are arriving thick and fast—in fact, at more rapid rate than accommodation can easly be provided. Very often the reason that they are all jumbled up together is not owing to insufficient land being available, but to the fact that the owner is afraid to scatter them about in case of severe I weather. _ .</p>
        <p>If sufficient shelter is provided this danger is very slight; as a matter of fact, almost any kind of exposure is preferable to warmth and protec- tion procured at the expense of undue crowding • together under insanitary conditions. The first and most important separation is that of the young from the old stock. The system of at- tempting to rear chickens in the midst of adults is one that cannot be too strongly condemned. The chickens live in a continual state of thral- dom; .they neither get sufficient food nor food: of the right kind,-since the hens obtain the more expensive .foods supplied to the chickens, who can consequently only get a small amount, and even that little by stealth.</p>
        <p>Frequently they have to be content with some grain snatched from the hens, which is too large for their digestive organs properly to assimilate. Therefore, to do justice to the young ones it is absolutely imperative that they be kept apart until they are old and strong enough to hold their own with the adult stock. The next division is to remove the small from the big chickens. They should be sorted into</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>ADULT SCHOOLS.</head>
        <p>ADULT SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>Faith, Optimism and Character.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH MEN'S.</p>
        <p>" A Holiday in County Down" was the sub- ject of the lecturette given by Mr. Frank Lynch on Sunday morning.- The beautiful scenery around the Mourne Mountains, the piquant talk of the drivers of the jaunting cars, the quaint customs, especially a real Irish country wedding. and the mode of living, and the primitive homes of many of the natives Were described in ii pleasing homely way.</p>
        <p>_ The Bible lesson on "The Testing of Faith " —especially in immortality, the message of Easter—was oinmed by Mr. H. Sherburn ina. few thoughtful sentences. ,The directions in which faith is necessary in life were discussed. Many points of Interest were brought out.. The message of Easter seems more and more to em 7 phasise the fact that resurrection is a natural process of life, ever present and operative.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>SANKEY BRIDGES WOMEN.</head>
        <p>SANKEY BRIDGES WOMEN.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening , the Bible lesson on "The Test of Faith" was introduced by Mrs. Alock, presidet of the Kilburn Women's School. She said fear and lack of faith go hand in band. The one is born of the other. Fear is a most expensive guest to entertain, the same as worry is. Alwiws remember that to fear about a thing is to Mira@ some other evil by lowering oui power of resistance. The mind dominated hY fear opens the door for the entrance of the very thing we (head. Fear can paralyse every muscle of the body; it affects the flow of the blood; fear can make the body rigid, motionless and power- less to move.</p>
        <p>It is thus in proportion as we are able to keep fear and worry out of our lives that we are able</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <p>similar sizes, as soon as the more aggr begin to assert their superiority. It is olnYill constant observation that the owner can out the many little points that require resdin! ment, and this is most certainly one of If it is noticed that some of the youngest rael berg of ;the batch are not making the longl that they should, something is- doubtless icr°' Very often this is the explanation. the,</p>
        <p>LAYING COMPETITIONS.</p>
        <p>The fifth report of the 'Utility Poultry Ch Twelve Months' Laying Competition, at frar Adams Agricultural College, which is under management of Mr. F. W. Rhodes, has instil, issued, and covers the period from Februal ' to March 13th. The figures show once 111°1 considerable increase in the average nunibt eggs laid by each of the 300 competing hi All the birds entered are pUllets of difiel strains of pure breeds. The average nuulheA: eggs laid per bird during the twenty-eight is almost twenty, being three eggs more per ' than last month. A point worthy of note is large percentage of large eggs laid, over 94 cent. of the whole of the eggs laid weighing and over.</p>
        <p>The White Wyandotte still hold.the first,' plac,es, but are closely followed up by the W' Legliorns, ewhich have made excellent Pr°gl and have stood the adverse weather conditi admirably. One death has occurred, but ett wise the health of the birds has been goed'</p>
        <p>The area of grass run allowed for each six birds is 400 square yards, and is I</p>
        <p>ample for the purpose, in spite of the Prel twelve monthe competition having been ducted on the same site.</p>
