      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>MOTOR NOTES.</head>
        <p>MOTOR NOTES.</p>
        <p>THROUGH TUNIS BY CAR.</p>
        <p>Between Enfidaville and Sousse we find more traffic. Everywhere we meet with arabats, great carriages fantastically painted with all sorts of talismanic signs, which look, with their mas- sive carved wheels, like ancient British chariots. Or sometimes it is a nomadic tribe, with tents, herds, and kabyle dogs; and then what a helter- skelter when we sound our siren!</p>
        <p>But at length the roads begin to wind; the earth becomes richer; crocuses ad asphodels glow beneath the olive trees; little donkeys laden with mad-apples and tomatoes trot by; and small boys appear on the roadside, their eyes shaded by the hoods of their Mohammedan burnous, and give us a military salute as we pass. A brilliantly white house shines from among the almond trees; soon comes Hamman Soussa,• a village seemingly peopled only by shadows and palm trees; then Sousse itself, ris- ing in transplendent battlements, whose very name seems to have the flavour of the gorgeous South.</p>
        <p>Sousse is well worth a stay of at least a day or two. There are the catacombs to visit, a little museum containing relics of ancient Hadrume- turn, the oil wells, the abrupt old streets leading up to the ramparts, whence one gets a view, over a dull green ocean of olive trees, of the other ocean of turquoise blue, lined with white-sailed carvels. The Grand Hotel at Sousse is one of the best in all Tunis; from the verandah of the dining-room and from the balcony of one's bed- room there is a Magnificent outlook over the old town, not so long ago a stronghold of pirates and famous for its slave-markets.</p>
        <p>The environs are exquisite; there is Monastir, with its maritime cemetery; Djemmal, where camel fights are held; Madia, where a sunken galley, filled with a cargo of antique statues and columns, has recently been discovered. But chiefest of all, there is Kairouan, formerly a holy city, but now profaned, the only town in Tunis where the unbeliever is allowed to enter the mosques. And what mosques they are! But Guy de Maupassant has written such mag- nificent pages on the subject that he has left nothing more to be said about this capital of white cupolas, springing up from the desert wildness as if the legend were true which relates that the stones came by themselves from the North and commenced to build the city without the aid of man. From Sousse to Sfax is a journey of 160 miles, during which not a single village is met with. There are few caravans, vegetation is scarce, and of the usual sources of water supply there are none. If you deem it worth while (and it is) to treat your Michelins to a refreshing wash, an oued must be sought for, from which a little brackish water may be drawn. The road continues to be excellent and rims straight on in an unbroken line, so that you can see it an incredible distance ahead. As one proceeds it, develops into a switchback of tiny hillocks, very amusing to travel over. Anti so to Sfax. BIBENDLIM.</p>
