      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>EXAMTNER, SATURDAY, APRIL 18, 1914. FARM 14. ND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMTNER, SATURDAY, APRIL 18, 1914. FARM 14. ND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>ENCOURAGING NOTES RELATING</p>
        <p>TO CHESHIRE.</p>
        <p>Good Season Predicted.</p>
        <p>Our Hatton correspondent writes At the time of writing farmers are busy with their potato planting. Generally speaking, they are later than usual owing-to the heavy rains, stiff farms being especially backward. Labourers are very scarce, and as a consequence one sees many fields with perhaps one man, the staff being made up of women, girls and boys. Wheat looks well on the whole; oats are begin- ning to make their appearance above the soil. Everywhere there seems an abundance of beau- tiful green grass ready for the cattle when they are turned out to graze, which will not be long now.</p>
        <p>In the garden spring is making itself felt, and such things as broad beans, early sown peas, onions, shallots, &amp;c., are beginning to make a brave show. In some sheltered borders potatoes are showing nicely. It is some years since there was such a show for store fruit, for both plum and damson trees in most parts of Cheshire are now one mass of white bloom. If late frosts are absent we shall have a good season of these popular fruits. It remains to be seen yet what ,apples and pears will blossom like, but for these a good season is predicted.</p>
        <p>The cuckoo has not yet been heard, but his advent is anxiously awaited, as his arrival generally betokens that mild spring weather that is so favourable to vegetation.</p>
        <p>FARMING TROUBLES. The position of the arable farmer, says " The Times," is becoming serious. The first ten days of the present month have been an improvement upon March, but the change was not pronounced enough to let field operations proceed as rapidly as the occasion required. The drill has been at work in most districts, but interruptions have been frequent and the land in indifferent condi- tion for the reception of the seed. The land dries so much more rapidly in April than in March that the traditional April showers often help rather than hinder the progress of work. Last week, however, the downpours were heavier and more frequent than was desirable. With the soil already sodden underneath, it is not an easy matter to prepare anything like a good seed bed—a fine free, open tilth in which the seed germinates quickly and the plant develops strong spreading roots. The April sowing record resembles that of last year too closely to inspire hopeful expectations of the late Lenten corn. Stockowners are beginning to study the pros- pects of the grazing season. The continued ex- clusion of Irish animals is a vital .factor in the situation. The seller of good dairy cows or graz- ing bullocks in thriving condition can command his own terms, short of a prohibitive limit. The dairy farmer and grazier are proportion ately affected to their detriment. They are buyers in a closed market, in which supplies are hopelessly inadequate. They count upon having to pay high prices, but the more dis- criminating of them will decline to give the ex- orbitant rates that an unsatisfied demand will doubtless create. On the other hand, the alter- native of abandoning milk production or the grazing of bullocks does not commend itself..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>VERY IMPORTANT-IF TRUE.</head>
        <p>VERY IMPORTANT-IF TRUE.</p>
        <p>A correspondent of the " Morning Post" sends news which will be read by cattle owners all over the world with a' feeling that it is almost " too good to be true." _ _ _</p>
        <p>It comes from Switzerland, and is to the effect that a professor of natural science in that coun- try (Dr. Stauffacher), already known for his re- searches into the nature of phylloxera, has dis- covered the cause of foot-and-mouth disease. It is not. a bacterium at all, such as scientists have been searching for without success, but a para- site of infinitesimal dimensions of the protozoa order, -which the doctor has found in every part of diseased animals whether alive or dead, and that, too, in immense numbers. In his view, the failure to find a means for exterminating foot-and-mouth disease has arisen from the fact that investigations have been on the wrong tack; in other words, our scientists have been looking for a bacterium instead of a parasite. If this Swiss professor is right, the door may be opened for finding an efficient means of pre- vention by inoculation. It must be remembered that we are not looking for a cure. That would be useless, as we should not be allowed to use it. The disease in itself is by no means a very fatal one, but causes so much loss of flesh and milk that we have to submit to severe restric- tions to prevent it from spreading. Thus our only hope lies in the direction of prevention, and we shall all be watching with anxiety for fur- ther and fuller information from Switzerland. It really does seem that if we are ever to find an effective means of dealing with foot-and- mouth and swine fever we must look for it out- side the magic circle of officialdom.—" Old Breeder" in "Farm and Home."</p>
        <p>AMERICAN GOOSEBERRY MILDEW. The Board of Agriculture and Fisheries have received information that the summer stage of American gooseberry mildew (Sphaerotheca more uvse) was discovered in a Cambridgeshire garden on the 6th inst. The dreaded disease has also appeared in Kent. The Board impress upon growers the importance of examining their 1 gooseberry bushes carefully and, in the event of the disease showing itself, spraying with a solu- tion of liver of sulphur, lib. to 32 gallons of water. Growers are also reminded that this is a notifiable disease and that a leaflet giving directions for the treatment of affected crops can be obtained free of cost from the 'Secretary of the Board, 4, Whitehall-place, S.W. Neglect to notify is a punishable offence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>STACK FIRING MANIA.</head>
        <p>STACK FIRING MANIA.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. E—mbleton Fox, chairman of the Lind- sey (Lincolnshire) Quarter Sessions, passed sen- tence of three years' penal servitude on Bernard Smith (3.3), joiner, for attempting to set fire to a straw stack at Fotherley. In doing so he said that was the third time the man had been con- victed of stack firing or attempted stack firing. It was not often that a Court of Quarter Sessions got a chance of dealing with a'man like the pris- oner, as the law in its wisdom said that Courts of Quarter Sessions must not deal with cases of arson, hut they could deal with attempted arson. In his opinion the Assize Courts were not nearly so competent to deal with that class of offence as Quarter Sessions, because the judges did not see the evil and the mischief of stack fires as did the magistrates who lived in the country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>SHOULD MILKMEN HAVE BEARDS?</head>
        <p>SHOULD MILKMEN HAVE BEARDS?</p>
        <p>We are getting on with our restrictions, says "Farm and Home." A clergyman with a moustache is a rarity, but a milkman with a beard will soon be unknown. The New York milkmen, we hear, have received " orders " from the Miik Commissioners to shave 0 their beards on the ground that these are prolific agents for the conveyance of germs. We hope they won't be compelled by-and-bye to discard their clothes also, but we should think the average " suit " is more thickly populated with germs than the beard, which does get a wash sometimes.</p>
        <p>ARTIFICIAL MILK. A Bean with a History like a Romance.</p>
        <p>Synthetic milk, that is milk produced by it process of building up or putting together the various elements, is announced once more, but this time it is milk with the lactic and bacillus, milk that can be converted into "excellent cheese and butter," according to an article in "The Times." The basis of the new production is casein from the soya bean. to which are added ju eNac!- ornnorti6ns fsttv aid. gs rs and salts. Emuleitk tion IP carried mit. and this difficulty has been completely overcome, the uev• fluid</p>
        <p>satisfying every test, even to the extent of re- fusing to cream.</p>
        <p>It is added that the taste of the new milk is slightly different from that of ordinary milk, though the difference is not apparent to all people, and even the slight " taste " can be re- moved at will. It is said that a dairyman, asked to sample two fluids, praised the artificial production and gave very modified appreciation of the other, which was a sample from his own supply of natural milk. The distinctive flavour of the milk is that of ' the soya bean, concerning which "The Times" adds: The history of the soya bean is like a romance. While the first consignment of soya beans was sent to Europe so recently as 1906, to- day Western requirements are something like a million tons a year. .The beans are grown in China, Japan, Korea and Manchuria, where they have long been valued for their oil and for the waste products after the oil has been extracted, which are used as fertilisers in the rice and sugar-cane fields. Vermicelli, biscuits, and other foodstuffs are also manufactured from the beans. In this country the soya oil has now a very ready and extensive market. It is used instead of the cotton seed variety on account of cheap- ness. Soap manufacturers are also coming to depend upon it. The chief use, however, would seem to be as cattle cakes for winter feeding. That the article which has fed so many ranch cows during the past two years should itself be artificial milk is undoubt- edlysomethinga coincidence. Most of the soya beans in at nh se entering makingoof this country pass through Hull, which, with its great oil and seed mills, is the natural centre for such a commodity. This import trade is already a very extensive one, the freight on soya beans having mounted up to a figure somewhere in the neighbourhood of £1,000,000 per annum. In addition, a consider- able export trade has recently sprung up owing to the demand of Continental dairy farmers for soya meal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>Hoe round roses to ensure a more uniform assimilation of manurial matter. A heavily manured rose border should be given a dressing of lime to sweeten the soil. Sow seeds of schizanthus under glass. Begin to harden off plants wintered in cold frames. . _</p>
        <p>As seedlings get large enough prick off into other boxes, and gradually harden. Give water to plants in cold frames if the soil is dry. Take cuttings of outdoor chrysanthemums and insert in sandy soil in a cold frame or house.</p>
        <p>•The following seeds may be sown in boxes placed in a frame: Asters, clarkias, stocks, scabious, sweet Sultans, marigolds, and godetias. S rich manure over the Tootspr Spread a raspberrieslayer g and ° loganberries. Protect early peas from birds with pea guards or cotton.</p>
        <p>Plant potatoes as soon as the soil is in good working order.</p>
        <p>After hardening border carnations plant firmly in the soil. Mix 'some finely broken old mortar with the soil. Sow seed of Brussels sprouts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>DIVIDING HARDY FERNS.</head>
        <p>DIVIDING HARDY FERNS.</p>
        <p>Now that the period of rest for hardy ferns is passing and the roots are getting active for the new spring growth, it is time to take in hand any transplanting or dividing that may be required. The active state of the roots and the absence of tender and easily damaged fronds enable the plants to speedily establish themselves after re- moval. A good mulching of decayed manure and leaves after planting is done will be very bene- Scial.—" The Garden."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>SOWING VEGETABLE SEEDS.</head>
        <p>SOWING VEGETABLE SEEDS.</p>
        <p>- i i When the soil s n a good condition, neither, too wet nor too dry, vegetable seeds germinate speedily. A fine surface soil is essential for the tiny rootlets to take hold of the small particles of soil. It is impossible for seeds to grow pro- perly if buried under lumps of soil. Sow the seed in drills; it is more economical than sowing broadcast, and any thinning out that is required will be accomplished easier. Seeds will not require as much covering soil at this part of the year as in the summer. Make the drills in a straight line, using the hoe. The rake can be utilised for filling in the drills, after which make the soil firm by gently patting it with the back of the spade.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>FUTURE MANURE SUPPLIES.</head>
        <p>THE FUTURE MANURE SUPPLIES.</p>
        <p>oefne expected,coblui course, oei,t ma y b e probably assumed With the rapid rise in the number of motor. driven vehicles for merchandise and passenger traffic, the manure profobrlemfewoefr tehoettfloutouorde pigs isigsa puzzling one. The farmers' supply i f -8, to some extent, assured from his own stock, although in decreasing quantities, are bred to-day than formerly. The market and gownd,hnoaigtnagr)den i gardener finds it more difficult as time goes on to get a sufficiency of manure, lie brings his produce to market, man- ure he can get from the stables iu the cities and towns. Then there is the grow( A home, and fhrasomtotl:vesorrtoadtos Life trheefrueseisfraLgeyartohnliiwiisnbe unless he is prepared to gathe... what he can he good crops. I raised by and use chemicals. Artificial manures are stimulating only, and do not supply humns to the soil, without which liii osi Fairlynsoil f ecr goodat in, sebr be crops,a that the soil does not become richer, but poorer, by the constant use of artificials. , easily-handled, I lies l`hien stohluetitorne aotfmtehnet znofanthuwr en problemsewa ge. Oldham . The sludge is has been experimenting for some years now with a new process of treating the town sludge and eawpithfeerftfieclitseivrse first of all dried, then the grease is extracted ,providing farmers faairidinecrhs and the rest comes out a fine, dark powder, con- taining nitrogen, pbesPhoric acid, potash, and about 10 per cent. of organic matter.</p>
        <p>MANURES FOR VEGETABLES. Some vegetables require more manure than others. Land which has been growing crops for some years, with annual manurings, will grow excellent crops of some vegetables without fur- ther 'manuring. sprinkle soot or burnt refuse i over the site, forking it n. The following crops are suitable: Beet, Carrots, Artichokes, Parsnips, Chicory, Salsify: A moderate dressing of animal manure, in addition to soot, will grow Cabbages, Broccoli, Savoys, Turnips, Spinach, Potatoes. The following require heavy manur- ings : Lettuce, Onions, Celery, Cauliflowers, Beans, Peas, Leeks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>. DECIDE YOURSELF. The OpportunitY is Here, Backed by Local Testimony.</head>
        <p>. DECIDE YOURSELF. The OpportunitY is Here, Backed by Local Testimony.</p>
        <p>Don't take our word for it. Read the statements of your neighbours. And decide for yourself. Here is one case of it : Mr. T. Fellows. of 44, Bewsey-road. near St Paul's Church, Warrington, says: "I worked eaerkolomwdinmg to an atack of lum- y back nAul loins were t Lnagaog. c nrry be forpaai Ns- fearful. I could not stoop, and in order to get out of bed I had to roll out. All the strength seemed to go out of my baek,and at last I had to leave work. "I was at home for a week, and my back was terribly stiff and sore. Every time I moved the pains attacked me. _ _ . _</p>
        <p>"At last I made up my mind to try Doan's Backache Kidney Pills, and I was soon thankful to find that they were doing me good. After taking two boxes I was quite cured. The pills cleared away a lot of gravel, and the kidney ex- cretions were put in good order again. I shall ,always he grateful for the good I derived from Doan's Pills, and you can rely UT)011 my recom- mending them to others. (Signed) T. Fellows." Prier 2/9 a box. El boxoq 13/9; of nil dealers, or rosteelfrclelltri Co • 8. Wrlls-st,•tiet. Ox- ford-street. London. W. Don't ask for Backache air Id-finer tiill.9 --ask DisTmcny fair IlcAN's MIA:- ache Kidney Pills. the same as Mr icflns had</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>CYCLING AND MOTORING.</head>
        <p>CYCLING AND MOTORING.</p>
        <p>Can the Skidding Danger be Avoided?</p>
        <p>A SADDLE HINT.</p>
        <p>One acknowlalged defect of the cheap machine is that the manufacturers always seem to save money by fitting a very inferior saddle. From the point of view of actual reliability' of the cycle, the action is hardly open to question, as there must be economy somewhere, and far better for it to be in the saddle than in the frame, but, unfortunately, a poor saddle often makes cycling a mild form of torture. It is very little use recommending a particular variety, as individual tastes vary so much, but most cyclists will probably agree with the dictum, "Wien you get a good saddle stick to it." I have been riding the same saddle fo a matter of eight years or so, and like it better every time, and not the most expensive example of saddle- maker's art would tempt me to exchange. A year or two ago I sent it off to the makers, who fitted a new seat on the old frame in a manner thoroughly satisfactory, and at about one-third the cost of a new one. The same rejuvenated saddle now accompanies a new machine, and when it again shows signs of wear, back it goes to the maker, and the most persuasive cycle dealer or attractive catalogue will never induce me to purchase a new one. Warning Signals. Just at present the Cyclists' Touring Club is devoting considerable attention to the question of standardising warning sounds for the various road users. Before the advent of the motor car, cyclists- were quite content with the ordinary bell or familiar whistle; the latter has now prac- tically disappeared, and some riders have even forsaken the bell for a small edition of the motor horn. Theire is nothing more annoying to cyclist or pedestrian than to be startled by an ear-splitting blast from what sounds like a powerful ear, at least, only to be passed by a " scorcher " on a rakish-looking machine with an equally rakish horn. Surely there is very little difficulty about suitable warning notes. The deep-toned horns should be limited to the car and used as seldom as possible, not with idle caprice and a fondness for startling other people. Motor cyclists might use a less noisy horn, particularly as the average motor Cycle requires no warning note at all, its approach being sufficiently apparent from the noisy bent of the engine. The humble cyclist will do well to adhere to the time-honoured bell and to use it only when absolutely necessary; the novice rings far too often and uSually makes himself a nuisance in this respect.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>Photograph by Burrell. RECORD EASTER MEET OF THE WARRINGTON WESLEY.</head>
        <p>Photograph by Burrell. RECORD EASTER MEET OF THE WARRINGTON WESLEY.</p>
        <p>The Accuracy of the Cyclometer. Hardly one cyclist in a thousand who listens to the miles ticking off on his cyclometer realises that the measurement is, after all, only an ap- proximate one, and liable to variation. of course, for all practical purposes the cyclometer is satisfactory enough, but the underlying prin- ciple is quite interesting. In itself, the cyclo- meter doesn't measure distance, but the number of revolutions of the front wheel required to traverse a certain length of road, the revolutions</p>
        <p>being transformed into distance by the mechan- ism, which is constructed in accordance with accurate mathematical calculation. The makers allow for the difference between 26 and 28 inch wheels, but that does not ensure perfect accu- racy, as a front wheel well inflated has a bigger radius than one where the tyre is soft, and the circumference is consequently greater by, per- haps, several inches. Though slight in one revo- lution the variation would make an appreciable total when the figures of the cyclometer mount up into the thousands, were it not for the fact that the tyre has not always the same degree of inflation. Of course, the state of the back tyre is of no importance, as the two wheels run free of each other, and the cyclometer is only affected by the one to which it is actually connected. A New Foot Warmer..</p>
        <p>Motor car equipment is everywhere moving in the direction of luxury, and affords an inter- esting comparison with the conditions prevail- ing a few years ago. Electric lighting sets, im- proved coachwork, hoods, windscreens, &amp;c., are only a few examples of progress which occur to one at the moment, but a glance at any of the catalogues will show how widespread the move- ment is. The latest device which has been placed in the French market is a new foot warmer heated by petrol. Weighing only 1514)., the apparatus comprises a wooden frame lined with asbestos, and containing a brass tray, the whole fitting into the floor of the car. A brass box which holds a burner and asbestos wicks rests on the tray. The container, which is slotted at the. top, can be opened as required; the lamp itself is filled with petrol, the wicks lighted and the lid closed. When the slots are opened to their widest capacity the maximum quantity of heat is given, and when they are totally closed the wicks are automatically ex- tinguished. As some slight risk of fire is be- lieved to be attached to the device, probably at- tributable to the use of an open flame, the ap- paratus has not yet been placed on the English market, but is becoming quite popular on the Continent. A Recent Non-skid Wheel. , Has the skidding problem been solved at last? Various devices, all guaranteed to reduce side-</p>
        <p>:nal thread the anus anti buckles a the carrier atrapk; through. Duckling them inside. If the button) or the bag is too large for the enrrier, a cigar box tin will be necessary to lengthen the earlier, or the bag will fall out of shape in a or two.</p>
