      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>EAST ERT I DE.</head>
        <p>EAST ERT I DE.</p>
        <p>Now the holidays are over, we have the plea- sure of looking back on a week-end of more than usual good luck in weather. The Wesleyan Cycle Club, 19 strong, ran on Friday to Pick- mere for lunch, and were excellently served at the Smithy House, where we always resort at meal times when in that district. No sooner were the cycles safely stowed away under cover than the rain came down and lasted till lunch was over. Then it was fine,till Peover Church was reached, and shelter was again at hand. Mounting again after a heavy shower we had time to reach the "Pines," where Mrs. Hoyle provided the tea, and we enjoyed it all the more listening to the swish of the wind and rain out- side. The ride home was made easier by that same wind, and the rain held off the whole time. - On Saturday 15 of the same club ran to Corn- bill Farm, on the Chester-road. Instead of going, forward to Chester as per programme; a pleasant ramble to Plemstall Church and back took up the afternoon, and the return was made through Afouldsworth and Norley. Monday was not so fortunate. Thirty-two—a record—set off for Castle Mill, so as to take ad- vantage of the gale. A ramble down to the Bailin, and lunch at Mrs. Overentre, Chapel House Farm, took till one o'clock, when a start was made for Styal. The rain started also. We sheltered in a Dutch barn till after four o'clock, when it was decided to make for Mrs. Overend's again for tea. The home journey, through Bagnley and Altrincham, was heavy though fine, and a constant procession of cars and auto.. cycles made it lively until Bollingten was reached.</p>
        <p>CLUB RUNS TO-DAY. Warrington Wesleyan—lrelsb',. 2.3 e. Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Guild—Dunham Massey, 2. Lamp Time: To-night, 8.10 ; next Saturday, 8.25.</p>
