      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 18, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 18, 1914.</p>
        <p>" THE LAND." Third Article THE BOYS' FIELD DAY.</p>
        <p>Systems under which Land &amp; Buildings are Owned &amp; Occupied.</p>
        <p>TENURE.</p>
        <p>BY E. RICHARD CROSS,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>Member of the Land Inquiry Committee.</head>
        <p>A Member of the Land Inquiry Committee.</p>
        <p>The Tenure chapters of the Urban Report deal with the various tenures under which land and buildings are owned and occupied in England and Wales. At the outset an attempt is made to _ indicate their extent and. distrileatiom and--the - Report proceeds to deal with the ...grievances, which exist under each and to sanest remedies for them-</p>
        <p>The distribution of the various tenures is gone into in some detail and illustrated by a map. The -Committee do not commit themselves to any does estimate of the extent to which the various tenures obtain; but they consider that substantially more than half the urban popula- tion of England and Wales is living under ordinary rreehold 'tenure, about one-twentieth under freehold subject to some form of perpetual annual payment, somewhere about one-tenth under long leasehold over 99 years, and rather under one-third on short leasehold (99 years and under), including leases for lives. Copyhold , exists in small "pockets" all over the country. LORDS' SPORTING AND MINERAL "RIGHTS." Capyhold is held to be an inconvenient and</p>
        <p>irritating form of tenure, which now has no defenders. The existing machinery for compul- ., , - gory enfranchisement is cumbrous, slow and</p>
        <p>expensive to the tenant. The proposals of the Lord Chancellor for immediate and universal enfranchisement are approved; but several modifications are suggested. It is considered that over half the compensa- tion awarded to the steward of the Manor should be paid by the lord, that the lorcnyright .of escheat on failure of heirs should be abolished, that the sporting rights of the lord should be extinguished, and that compensation for the buying out of mineral rights should be assessed by the new Land Commissioners. A large amount of this part of the book is devoted to consideration of the leasehold system n its various forms. Its origin is discussed and is. short account of its history is given. It ap- pears to have grown up either through legal limitations on the powers of private owners and ecclesiastical and other corporations, or through their desire to keep their lands in the hands of their families or successors, and to obtain the large increment which they anticipated. HARDSHIPS OF SHORT LEASES.</p>
        <p>The short or London leasehold system is dis-I cussed in considerable detail. Many illustra- tion- at the hardships and evil it creates are quoted from the evidence collected by the Cord- mittee's Investigatoie. The opinions of experts and of previous investigators are also quoted, and in their conclusions the Committee say that 'the system in its present form gives rise to in- tolerable grievances on the part of leaseholders, leads to unjust confiscation and unwarrantable exactions, discourages business energy and enterprise, and has a highly prejudicial effect on urban development. It puts serious obstacles in the way of the abolition of slums, and tends' to increase the rents paid by the actual occu- piers.</p>
        <p>• Various remedies for these evils are discussed, in particular that of compulsory enfranchise- ment, which, however, the Committee reject, partly on the ground that it is an unsatisfactory remedy for the great majority of leaseholders who wish to employ their capital in a more • svcrfitable way, and also because they regard the multiplication of small owners as undesirable on public grounds. It is thought it would re- tard desirable improvement's and ,increase the number of possible resisters to the legitimate appropriation by the municipality or the State of that portion of site value which is of com- munal origin. Enfranchisement is, however, recommended in the case of places of worship and schools, and land and buildings leased to local authorities. - Mere compensation for improvements on the lines of the Irish Town Tenants Act is consid- • ored an inadequate remedy, under normal cis. cumstanees. The ordinary remedy should, it is contended, be security of tenure, conditioned only by the paramount consideration that pub- lic and private improvements should not there- by be prevented or checked. Where, in the public interest, continuity of teraire is undesirable, it is -recommended that the lessee should be compensated for the un- • exhausted value of suitable buildings erected and other suitable improvements made by him, and, in the case of business premises, also for , loss of goodwill, depreciation of fixtures, and the costs of enforced removal. , The power to veto improvements, alterations in user and assignments or sub-leases should no , longer be an absolute one, exerniseable in the ,lessor's sole and uncontrolled discretion, but ,should be subject to review by an impartial 'tribunal. ; The existing legal right of lessors to a rever- ,aionary interest in buildings erected before the ; suggested legislation comes into force must be respected. A TRIBUNAL TO SETTLE DISPUTES. It is thought that a Judicial Tribunal to settle disputes between owners and lessees is a , vital and essential part of the national organ- isation, however the question of land ownership is ultimately settled. The Committee had, in an interim report pre- sented to the Chancellor of the Exchequer last year, recommended a Land Court on the same , lines as that proposed in their rural volume, as ; the best tribunal for settling questions between lessors and lessees. They now, howeyer, endorse the Government's proposal to create Judicial -Land Commissioners, and approve of the pre- "; liminary intervention of conciliators approved by the Ministry of Land. They set out to con- siderable detail what they consider should be r the powers of the Land Commissioners in deal- ing with security of tenure, compensation and other matters arising between landlord and tenant. It is not proposed that the lessee should have to w,ait until the end of the term ap- proaches before making his application. Though ;!• the reversionary value of buildings erected before the new legislation takes effect is to be aviarded to the lessor, he is to get it, not in the ,way of a premium or fine which would absorb the lessee's capital, but by way of increased rent spread over,. the extended term. Extensions are •'not to be granted if the landlord or the muni- cipality can show that the property is requir• ed for , public purposes, for carrying out by the eiground landlord of a desirable improvement, or for; a higher use." ' LIFE LEASE EVILS. In dealing with the question of the evils of 41eases for lives, a form of tenure mainly found in Devon and Cornwall, and now rapidly dying rout, the report quotes some pertinent extracts from Mr. Thomas Hardy's " Woodlanders," .-stioviing the tragic events of this tenure on the I ; fortunes and lives of individuals. I must refer my readers to the E.eport itself for the various reforms that are proposed in the 'law of landlord and tenant. They include pro- visions with regard to structural alterations, dilapidations, the avoidance of unreasonable onditions, the enactment of scales of casts on assignments, consents to alterations and licences, and for the enlargement of the powers of limited owners. BUSINESS PREMISES.</p>
        <p>The case of business premises under tenancies for less than 21 years is dealt with at consider- able length. The grievances of tenants of such premises are stated to be similar in nature to those of the short leaseholder, and their effect. oa enterprise and on urban dcvrizpatant to be</p>
        <p>the same also: In other words, security of tenure or compensation for improvements is to be given to factory owners and shopkeepers, even though their tenancies may be for short teems. The concluding chapters deal with hardships existing under the present system under all forms of tenure. It is shovin that the rigidity of the present law with regard to restrictive covenants not only occasions loss to individuals, but prevents proper urban development, and it is proposed that the power to rescind or vary co4pants as to the use of property shall be rested in the Land Commissioners. It is laid down as a principle that the use to which land and buildings should be put is one which concerns the local coinmunity as a whole, and that local authorities should be given a locus standi before the Commissioners on all applications with regard to matters of this kind.</p>
        <p>Other matters in respect of which reforms are preposed are rights of light and .other easements, the ascertainment of sums payable to vendors and lessors for street making, and the grievances of builders' merchants and creditors of builders through the existence of clauses in building agreements and leases providing that all build- ing materials and plant brought on to land shall immediately become the property of the lessor or the vendor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>SYSTEM TO BLAME.</head>
        <p>SYSTEM TO BLAME.</p>
        <p>• —. This part— of the book winds up with a recom- mendation for the abolition of the law of primo- geniture,'under which, when a man dies without having made a will, his real property passes to his heir-at-law, to the exclusion of his other children or next of kin. It is generally recog- nised that the law as it stands has no foundation in natural justice, and often causes great hard- ships and imports much bitterness into family relations. Many illustrations of all the grievances I have referred to in this article are given; but the Committee, as they say in their preface, while endeavouring faithfully to portray the evils arising from the existing system, have studiously refrained from any attempt to pass judgment on the motives of individuals, or to fix them with personal responsibility for the hardships which that system permits, or it may be com- pels, them to inflict on others.</p>
        <p>It may, I think, fairly be claimed for the reforms they propose that while they necessarily restrict somewhat the freedom of owners of land, they will greatly increase the freedom and the opportunities of those who live and work upon it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>PROVERBS X. 15.</head>
        <p>PROVERBS X. 15.</p>
        <p>"The Destruction of the Poor is Their Poverty."</p>
        <p>'BY ARTHUR HEBOEN.</p>
        <p>One Sunday evening I heard an address on poverty, by a preacher whose sympathy with the victims of social injustice is beyond question. Ile took for his text a verse'from Proverbs, "The rich man's wealth is his strong city; the destruc- , tion of the poor is their poverty," and he based his remarks on "Round About a Pound a Week," a book that describes with great wealth of detail the conditions under which respectable, sober people live ,in Lambeth (London), whose incomes average the sum of 225. a week. The dreary streets were portrayed—the awful monotony of which is rarely relieved even by a drunken brawl—and the daily tragedies of poverty within the wretched, cramped, in- sanitary houses, where the most rigid economy cannot save the children from the inevitable penalties of insufficient food, air, clothing and warmth. We were told how many of the chil- dren per thousand die during infancy; at what rate these little victims of injustice (nearly all of whom are born healthy and normal) become defective; how the murderously inadequate in- come, supporting sometimes eight or more per- sons, works out at less than is. 6d. per person per week for food (except in the case of the father, whose wage-earning capacity depends upon a certain degree of physical efficiency); and many other ghastly details.</p>
        <p>STATISTICS OF HUMAN AGONY. How I hate these statistics of human agony! They remind me of nothing so much as the horrors of the vivisection chamber, where all the pangs of the wretched victims of human cruelty are carefully classified and tabulated for the nice regulation of future torture. But I bore the text in mind and waited hopefully. Here is a poverty that is not officially recog- nised—the fathers are in regular employment and the weekly bills are duly met—and that is not in any sense picturesque. Everything is sor- did, dreary and terrible, and it was told as the book was written—a dispassionate statement of facts which does the investigators of these hor- rors much credit. People around me were visibly impressed. The premature ageing of young men and women and the wholesale slaugh- ter of the innocents that were described, per- haps, touched the hearts and awakened the sym- pathies of many of the hearers. As for me, my blood boiled with indignation, partly, perhaps, because I know Lambeth, its people and their lives, and I know the story to be no exaggeration of the terrible truth, and partly because I also know such poverty to be unnatural and un- necessary, and therefore damnable rather than pitiable. There was only one gleam of bright- ness in the sad picture, the love of these people for their children. They remain human under inhuman conditions. And that love is tested as never in the experience of parents in better cir- cumstances, and as human love never should be tested—by daily sacrifice and cruel self-denial, ungrudgingly endured, in the vain attempt to supply the things that love prompts and that nature demands as part of the price of health. After these harrowing details, told with a self-suppression and calmness that I envied, the preacher proceeded to suggest some means of dealing with the problem. In passing he alluded appreciatively to the boon these poor women are deriving from the Maternity benefit of the Insurance Act, and then he showed what might be done by establishing school clinics, the pro- vision of meals for the children, and a scheme of subsidising the wages of these deserving raeu. , This would strengthen their independence, we were told, and would enable them to make a bold, combined fi,glit for better conditions, which rt seky ecvaennn otth edo t e true -day y becauseloss o f they e amr ee a ro et iTinpnehaco Codp mel se et aasb t.vyea pv rano? d. )uvi i(dti f be egyr ablehssoul coshuet slodpwdu ohbr pee not ssbleeads tn.ut land 3rA, hntahoddo thoseub s thatee been was all!</p>
        <p>THE HELL OF RESPECTABLE POVERTY. My discomfiture was complete. I had listened with what patience I could command to a heart- rending account of the hopeless struggles of the poor soula who live in the hell of respectable Poverty, hoping to the end that the preacher would express my own burning anger in a scathing exposure and denunciation of the causes of poverty. But I had hoped in vain. Perhaps he did not know; perhaps he thought that was not the time or place. The service closed with the hymn, " When wilt Thou help Thy people, Lord?! a most fitting conclusion, I</p>
        <p>(Continua is mixt coltope.)</p>
        <p>4`-;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>Young Brigadiers Take Part in Sham Fight.</head>
        <p>The Young Brigadiers Take Part in Sham Fight.</p>
        <p>"BATTLE" AT HIGH LEGH.</p>
        <p>In common with Boys' Brigades all over the country, the Warrington Battalion C.L.B. held a field day on Easter Monday, when the scene of operations was in the High Legh district. The day was ideal. with the exception of one heavy shower, which was unable to extinguish the glow of enthusiasm which pervaded all ranks.</p>
        <p>There were approximately 150 on parade, in- cluding cyclists., signallers, ambulance and band, all under the command of Lieut.-ColoneßApple- ton and his staff officers, Major Brooks, Adju- tant Sankey, and Sergt.-Major Goodwin. The rendezvous was Arpley Cannon. from whereAhey proceeded at nine o'clock via Latchford and Grappenball, to High Leg,h.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>RAID ON HIGH LEGH.</head>
        <p>RAID ON HIGH LEGH.</p>
        <p>The general idea of the operations was that Lancashire and Cheshire were two neighbour- ing states opposed in warfare. Lancashire (red), had successfully evaded Cheshire (blue) at War- rington, and had advanced into Cheshire, leav- ing an advanced depot of stores and ammuni- tion at High Legh Hall under the command of Colonel X., with a small detachment of infan- try. The special idea of the day's movements was that early on the Monday morning the general officer commanding the Lancashire force at Warrington had received reliable informa- tion from Colonel X. that a small raiding party of Blues which had evaded Reds' troops, left Macclesfield the , previous evening and were marching in the direction of, Knutsford. It was believed .that that party Contemplated a raid on'High Legh in order to destroy the stores, so the general officer commanding the Reds at , Warrington therefore decided to strengthen the garrison at High Legh, and Ordered the 2nd Liverpool Cadet Battalion C.L.B. to advance. This battalion, under the command of Lieut.- Colonel F. M. Appleton, proceeded from Arpley Cannon early on Monday morning, preceded by the advance guard, in charge of Lieut. Bottom- ley, and protected in the rear by a guard. ,Each body had signallers and buglers attached, whilst a well-equipped ambulance squad was in at- tendance upon the main body. The transport, under escort, marched independently. Each boy was provided with ten rounds of "blank" am- munition.</p>
        <p>"ARMIES" IN CONTACT. The cyclist scouts of the 2nd Liverpool Batt. C.L.B. came- into contact with the advance guard a the enemy (represented by the High Leg!' Troop of Boy Scouts), and after a skirmish ' captured one of their c umber and drove the ' remainder back on Higt Legh. On arrival at High Legh Hall it was assumed that the enemy, ' Without guns, were holding an entrenched posi- tion. The enemy were represented not by a number of men, but according to the effect they would have on the attacking infantry with reference to the attackers' use,nf,ground to de- velop their fire and to protect themselves. For this purpose three flags were utilised to repre- sent a certain degree of fire power. A red flag indicated that the fire of the defenders was the superior, a blue one that the fire of the attack and defence was equal, and a white one that the fire of the attack was superior. The umpire and judge (Capt. 11. C. Cotton, 4th South Lanca- shire Regiment) was in the defensive position, and the three flags were used acicording to his decision. There were many lessons derived from the scheme which proved very realistic, -espe- cially- in the • closing stages, when the attack made excellent use of cover and became vie- torions. SERVICE AFTER THE " BATTLE.", Tea was provided in the park, following' which , a short service was held in Colonel Legh's private chapel, when the Rev. Edwards spoke to the boys upon true manhood. Mr. H. Atkin- son, a member of the High Legh Boy Scouts,' Presided at the organ. The battalion again assembled in the park Prior to their departure, and Lieut.-Colonel Appleton took the opportunity of thanking Col. Legh and the Hon. J. E. Cross for their kind- ness in putting the park and chapel at their disposal. The Hon. J. E. Cross replied, saying that Col. Legh was a long Way from home, but had he been there lie would certainly have been Un-' pressed with their excellent behaviour. If they wished to Rome at any other time he was sure they would be welcomed. . The captain and subaltern for the day were respectively Captain S. L. Henshall and Lieut. R. Isherwood. Lieut.-Colonel Appleton, his staff officers, and the company officers, along with the new sergeant-major, worked hard to make the day enjoyable.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>PROVERBS X. 15-,-continued.</head>
        <p>PROVERBS X. 15-,-continued.</p>
        <p>thought, to a discourse that could not fail to impress one with a sense of human helplessness in the face of man-made disaster. When will good people recognise the fact that poverty is the result of human maladjustments? That God has provided for all His children a globe itehlY stored with everything that i s necessa y to the sustenance and comfort of every living soul, but that some have taken possession of these free gifts (land) and therefore some are unwholesomely rich while many are degradingly poor? If "the destruction of the poor is their poverty," it seems to me that the only way to end the destruction is by removing the poverty. To'rnake the lot of the defrauded poor a little less intolerable by feeding 'and medically at- tending the children, and by doing the many other, ,things these people would do for their oivn children if they were not poor, is but to •Perpetuate their poverty, because such measures can only make the good people who advocate them feel a comfortable consciousness of having discharged their obligations to the unfortunate, and also leSsen the unrest of those whose mur- murings are disturbing respectable society and making people think.</p>
        <p>! "PROVIDENCE,, NOT TO BLAME. Who can doubt that the poverty of the poor is their destruction, physical, mental and moral? , Who in these days dare attribute that poverty to "a mysterious dispensation of Provi- (tenor? Involuntary poverty is the result of robbery. It,is robbery that is producing poverty and disease amongst the submerged millions, that is slaughtering the thousands of little chil- dren who die in Lambeth and elsewhere, and that blasts those who do not find release in early death. Involuntary poverty in all its horrible features ,and effects—unemployment, low wages, slums, sickness, hunger, vice, degradation and crime—is the result of the great social injustice of land monopoly,. And that evil thing, enor- mous and powerful though it be', can be destroyed .by the progressive taxation of land 'values.</p>
        <p>Wages are low in Lambeth because men fire cheap. In Christian England to-day labour is a marketable commodity which varies in price as .the supply is great or small, in proportion to the demand. There are many mere men than jobs, and that is why men are clieap. The first thing to, do is, therefore, so to increase the de- mand for men as to absorb the surplus labour, or to reach the same end by'expediting the mur- derous elimination of the "unfit." Until that is done no real improvement can be secured, whatever may be attempted by Trade Union action or Parliamentary palliatives. The taxa- tion of land values will force millions of acres of land in town and country into good use, and thereby create an enormously increased demand for labour of every kind that will soon make men dear. Their independence will grow with their value. Does it sound too good to be true? Certainly none who have learned the inspiring truth taught by irenry,Cieorge in "Progress and Poverty" can be tormented by such doubts.</p>
        <p>„ Who, believing that "the destruction of the poor is their poverty,” and knowing poverty to he caused by robbery, dare rest until justice id done?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>LIVELY SOCIALISTS.</head>
        <p>LIVELY SOCIALISTS.</p>
        <p>Incidents at the I.L P. and B.S.P.'s Conferences.</p>
        <p>THE QUESTION OF FUSION.</p>
        <p>The two wings of the Socialist party—the Independed Labour party and the British Socialists' party—held their conferences over Easter, and, judging from the feelipg expressed at one conference. it is unlikely (hat the two wings will come together with the view of united action. Some lively incidents were witnessed at both conf(Tences.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>B.S.P. AND LABOUR PARTY.</head>
        <p>B.S.P. AND LABOUR PARTY.</p>
        <p>At the B.S.P. conference in london,.in an offi- cial report on the subject of Socialist unity, a question which had been discnised in priyate meeting, it was stated that : _ _ _ -</p>
        <p>said that the movement for the consolidation of the Labour and Socialist forces of Great Britain SPeaking for the Executive, Mr. Dan Irving, had been going on for a long time past, and those opposed to the Closer union of Socialists here were now in an insignificant minority op- posed to the other Socialist organisation of the whole world.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pearce, who opposed, said that the pro- position was not only for union with the Labour party in this country, but for affiliation with the Labour party in the Rouse of Commons. He believed in Socialist unity, but the Labour party were not Socialists. If the British Socialist party joined it, nothing but discord could result. Mk. R. M. Ryndman said that if the British Socialist party now went into the Labour party it would be to get into closer touch with the organised working class of this country, and thereby greatly increase its influence over them. They would go in with the redflag flying. In the course of a discussion in open confer- ence the Chairman said it was not intended to take a vote there on the question Of Socialist unity, as it was being considered in the branches.</p>
        <p>In a supplementary report on the Parliamen- tary Fund, the Executive Committee stated that as certain branches had refused to collect the levy and others had failed, in spite of repeated applications, to collect or remit the levy for 1913 without offering any reasonable explanation they recommended the conference to take dras- tic action.</p>
        <p>SUFFRAGETTES INTERRUPT I.L.P.—ERS. n made from various parts At the I.L.P. conference at Bradford, while nominations were being of the hall for appointment as conference tellers, an elderlylwoman in the gallery made an obser- vation which the Chairman of the conference, Mr. J. Keir Hardie, M.P., was unable to hear. "Delegates," Mr. Hardie called out, "should be sipneak from the b the odyoftghaellheary,ll .... We can't allow them t The woman who had previously spoken now cried out, "Why don't you give women the vote," and proceeded to throw over the balcony a handful of leaflets. To the woman's apparent surprise no further notice was taken of her con- duct. The Chairman ignored her observation, and the persons in her vicinity simply said nothing. The woman was ohviously disgusted, as she rose to leave the gallery, and to someone near her who made an observation she replied rather snappishly, "Shut up." Subsequently another woman repeatedly in- terrupted the proceedings from the gallery. The stewards refrained for a long time from inter- fering with her. At length, however, the cries Of " Throw her Out!" became sc.) insistett that some of the stewards took hold of her with the Object of turning her out. Mr. Keir Hardie appealed for them to refrain, and for a few minutes there was every indica- tion of an imminent struggle between the stew- ards and the woman's friends. Peace was re- stored by the President's persistence, however, and the woman remained in the gallery, having nodded acquiescence in a bargain with the veteran Chairman that she would desist from interruptions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>FINANCIAL BURDEN.</head>
        <p>THE FINANCIAL BURDEN.</p>
        <p>- - • ..... The conference Was engaged for a considerable time in discussing the report of the National Administrative Council, which stated that the least satisfactory feature of the financial state- ment was the Parliamentary Fund, which was at present in debt to the General Fund to the extent of .433. The Council therefore recom- mended,that a shilling levy be made on every member of the party. A resolution in favour of the levy having been, moved, Mr. T. D. Bensoa, of Manchester, the treasurer, expressed the hope that any levy 'imposed would be loyally supported. In that event £1,500 would be realised, whereas previous levies had only realised something over £4OO in one year, and something over X6OO in another Year. From Mr. Benson's further remarks, which could only be heard imperfectly, it ap- peared that the commitments of the party for the next election were heavier than ever, and the Council were therefore not able to provide the returning officers' fees from the central source. He suggested that those branches which were not running candidates should help those which had undertaken such obligations.</p>
        <p>The conference endorsed the proposal for a Shilling levy.</p>
        <p>When the A QUESTION OF TACTICS. period for lunch approached the Chairman called attention to a paragraph in the report of the WA.C., which read as follows: Our alliance with the trade unionists in the Labour party raised the question specially of tactics which we must face seriously. What we suggest is that resolutions dealing wtth Policy, the report of the seven I.L.P. Members of Parliament, and other cognate/ matters Should be grouped together so that the Con- ference may deal with the matter as a whole and in a manner worthy of its importance. The W.A.C. feel that greater freedom of speech would be possible were the Press excluded from ts special session, but that is a point for th e Conference itself to decide. The Chairman put the question of whether, or not the representatives of the general Press should be excluded, and in doing so intimated that. reporters for the Labour papers would in any case be allowed to remain, and an official note of the proceedings would be taken. The conference's reply was a decisive vote in favour of excluding those members of the Press who did not represent Labour publications. A delegate asked what would happen in regard to the gallery? . The Chairman: Members of the party in the gallery would be as welcome in the afternoon as they are now.</p>
        <p>d A Delegate: How shall we know they are members of the party. Another Delegate: HoW are we to exclude our enemies an try to get in? any member of the Press who may</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>AGAINST "CABINET RULE."</head>
        <p>AGAINST "CABINET RULE."</p>
        <p>- - - - - - By a majority of 233 votes to 78 the delegates on Tuesday passed a resolution requesting the Parliamenta Labour party in future to vote exactly in accordance with the principles of rY their party. on all issues, without troubling to consider what effect their votes may have upon the life of the Government. To quote a Portion of the resolution, the re- quest was made "with a view to the ultimate break-up of the system of Cabinet rule, which involve the suppression of the rights of the private member to any adequate voice in the policy of his party, and which implies the resignation of the Ministry and the dissolution of Parliament when proposals of the Cabinet are negatived, besides making almost impossible the free consideration of proposals which have not received the Cabinet ball-mark." .</p>
        <p>Jr W. reach. nrrptvin2 the reeoluti" or</p>
