        <p>(Reproduced by THE FIGHT FOR THE BANNER. JOHN BULL: "This tires me. Why can't you carry it between you? Neither of you can carry it alone."</p>
        <p>behalf of the Bradford branch, declared that the Labour party could not afford to stand by a system which compelled them to place their most sacred principles in pawn, and allow the pawn-ticket to be held by a small junta. (Hear, hear.) The Cabinet system was too idiotic to be accepted by municipalities, and yet it was con- sidered good enough for the controlling of national affairs.</p>
        <p>"LABOUR AND SOCIALIST" CANDIDATES. A long discussion took place on a recommenda- tion put forward by several branches that the I:L.P., the British Socialist party,, and the Fabian Society should join in the formation of a United Socialist Council. The subject was considered in December, by the executives of the three bodies at a meeting held unde4 the auspices of the Internationall Socialist Bureau, and it was agreed that the members should be consulted as to the advis- ability of putting forward a proposal at the I Labour party conference of 1915 that candidates who so desire may describe themselves as "Labour and Socialist" candidates. That meet- ing also urged that steps should be taken in the way of holding joint •meetings, &amp;c., with a view' to bringing about "a speedy and happy solution of the difficulties which have hitherto stood in the way of Socialist. unity." A vigorous protest was made by Mr. Egerton I Wake (Barrow-in-Furness), who declared that I ea-operation of the kind proposed would reopen the door to dissension. The work of the I.L.P. would be paralysed by the resurrection of all the old quarrels on matters of dogma and method.</p>
        <p>Mr. Fenner Brockway pointed out that if they ran "Labour and Socialist" candidates there would be nothing to prevent other parties from running "Labour and non-Socialist" or "Labour and \Anti-Socialist " candidates. The recommendation with regard to the classi- fication of candidates as " Labour and Socialist" was referred back to the committee for further consideration. The other recommendations were adopted.</p>
