      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>OUR DRINK BILL</head>
        <p>OUR DRINK BILL</p>
        <p>Trade Prosperity Leads to Higher</p>
        <p>Expenditure.</p>
        <p>OVER £lB PER FAMILY.</p>
        <p>Mr. George B. Wilson, of the United King- dom Alliance, writing to "The Times," deals with the official figures connected with "The Trade" last year, from which it appears that the national drink bill was .2166,681,000, an in- crease of £5,128,000 over that of 1912.</p>
        <p>Last year was one of great prosperity, says Mr. Wilson, and the weekly wage bill of the nation was probably higher than it had ever been before, so that it is not surprising, though regrettable, that a certain proportion of the in- creased earnings .was expended in the purchase of intoxicating liquors. "It is encouraging, however, to note that the consumption of alco- holic liquors, as measured by the figures of per capita consumption, is still much lower than it was during the periods of trade activity 'which culminated in 1874 and 1899, as appears from the following table : Per capita consumption in gallons. Beer. Spirits Wine. 1874 1899 / 32.53 • . 1.09 .41 1913</p>
        <p>" If the consumption in 1913 had been at the rate of 1874 the cost to the consumer would have amounted to .X 242,000,000 whereas I estimate the actual expenditure in 1913 at .6166,681,000. This is an increase of 085,128,000 over the figures for 1912. Spirit§ "show an increase of £1,997,000 on an increased consumption of 1,267,000 gallons, beer- .83,007,000 on an increase of 1,002,000 stan- dard barrels, and wine £llB,OOO on an increased consumption of 131,000 gallons." The following are the figures of the drink bill for 1913 for the United Kingdom:—</p>
        <p>United Kingdom (population 46,035,570) Quantities Quantities Liquors. consumed consumed Cost in in 1912. in 1913. 1913. British spirits, 31s. £ 6d.per Proof gallon 25.152,669 26,163,423 41,207,000 Other spirits, ditto.. 5,423,594 5,680,402 8,947,000 -</p>
        <p>Total spirits, ditto.. 30,576,263 31,843,825 50,154,000 Beer, 60s. per stan- dard barrel ......33,913,219 34,915,687 104,747,000 Wine, 185. per gal... 11,291,205 11,421,759 10,280,000 British wines, eider, etc., estimated 2s.</p>
        <p>per gal. ...... 15,000,000 15,000,000 1,500,000</p>
        <p>166,681,000</p>
        <p>£lB 2s. per Family of Five. In 1913 the average expenditure per head was £3 12s 5d., and per family of five £lB 2s. id., as compared with £3 10s. 9d. per head and £l7 13s. 10d. per family in 1912. Mr. Wilson goes on to point out, .however, that per capita figures throw no light on the question as to the distri- bution of liquor amongst the different classes of a nation, and do not allow for the fact that over 14,000,000 persons in the United Kingdom are children under 15, and take no account of the very large number of women and considerable number of men who are wholly or practically abstainers. It may be assumed, he says, that the average per capita consumption of those who take intoxicants is much above the general per capita figures; that the consumption of the men who drink is much larger than that of the women who drink; and that the consumption of dwellers in urban areas is much larger than that of the rural population. He estimates the drink bill for England and Wales at £137,041,000, being £3 14s. 2d. per head;' for Scotland, £15,815,000, being £3 es. 9d. per head; for Ireland, £13,823,000, being £3 3s. Id. per head. The per capita consumption of pure alcohol in the United Kingdom was approximately 1.91 gallons. Out of every 100 gallons of pure alcohol con- sumed in the United Kingdom 77.4 gallons are taken in the form of beer, 20.6 gallons as spirits, and 2.0 gallons as wine. A table giving the esti- mated per capita consumption of beer and spirits and wine for the three kingdoms puts the con- sumption of spirits in England and Wales at .59 proof gallons, Scotland 1.41, and Ireland .71, and that of beer at 30.11, 10.25, and 2049 stan- dard gallons respectively. . _ _</p>
        <p>During 1913, he points out, there was approxi- mately one licensed beer or spirit shop to every 330 persons, or one to every 200 of the popula- tion of twenty years and upwards. It is obvious, he declares, that such facilities for obtaining intoxicants are greatly in excess of any supposed requirements. In addition 9.295 clubs paid duty on .C 2.273,160 worth of intoxicants purchased by thew for distributicn to their members.wi</p>
      </div>
