      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>CROP OF EASTER VESTRIES.</head>
        <p>THE CROP OF EASTER VESTRIES.</p>
        <p>&amp;hely Scene at Stockton Heath; Short and Peaceful Meetings the General Rule,</p>
        <p>THE NEW VICAR ,OF PADGATE.</p>
        <p>week witnessed the annual crop of Easter vestries. At few of the churches were any burning questions raised, as a general rule the Meetings were brief and marked by no out- standing features.</p>
        <p>t the Parish Church the question of a sug- gested change in the ritual was discussed, but sideration tllO matter was adjourned for further con- .</p>
        <p>At Stockton Heath there was a bit of a scene v Or the question of freewill offerings, but the Subject was dropped on the recommendation et the Vicar.</p>
        <p>Ata Padoate Canon Willis announced the tiillll3intment of the Rev. H. F. S. Collier as „e. new incumbent. Tributes of respect were c7d.to the memory of the late Vicar, and a rchal welcome is promised the new Vicar.</p>
        <p>pleased to !innounce..that he ,had appointed the Rev.. H. F. S.-Collier Cie vicar of Padgate.</p>
        <p>Ritual Changes. .In: conclusion, the. ,Rector said: I told you once I should not make any further change in ritual without your thorough approval of it. There are two things I want very much, and .I hope I may be 'able to carry you with me. One is, that I should like to have lights at the mid- day 'celebration as well as at the early one. I like it because Christ is the light of the world, and it is 'just simply to mark it. I don't think people will be frightened now. The other thing is that we have a choral celebration of the Holy Eucharist on the third Sunday as well as the first. The choir don't object to the music, but the 'length of the. service. It distresses me that the choir should go out. It gives people the im- pression that it is with my sanction and ap- proval, and that is not so. I will shorten the service. I will begin with the Lord's Prayer and end with the third collect, and I will keep myself to ten minutes for the Sermon. If you see your way to these things I shall be delighted:</p>
        <p>Mr. Isherwood: We have been saying how well we have been going along; don't you think it would be wise to let well remain well. (Hear, hear.) - • Councillor Craik : The Rector simply suggested it; think over it. The Rector: Think over it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>PARISH CHURCH. The Rector and Ritual.</p>
        <p>t'Le Rector (the Rev. Canon Willis) presided L 14,71 'e Parish Church vestry, meeting in the new tili:l. vestry on Thursday evening. Amongst 1,!0-8e Present were the Rev. J. M. Bnckmaster, • Isher,.. Craik (Rector's warden), Mr. E. Whil.”%d (people's warden), and Mr. E `8 (vestry clerk). 4e0i,.. Isherwood submitted the statement of 16Llts• and said the balance in hand Of £25 would be given to defray the cost of trat°,:clie the old vestry. They were very 14 ;jut to the ladies and church workers, who (111de -°lle so much for the benefit of the church. "Ibear.) They began the year with a bal- iil hand Of .4.115 13s. Gd. A sum of £3lO foBl. ;'• was collected for church expenses, and • inen'Peoial objects .433 6s. 4d., and £.97 Its. sd. 18s-41.,,,e from other sources gave a total of A:656 Pens'. 'rhe total expenditure for church ex- as £342 168.. Among 4 the other items the'Peadilure, amounting to £2BB 6s. 4d., w.e2.e . eiluretollowing amounts: Clergy fund, 0 toti al I Soeiety, £4O 16s. 2d.; giving together 'a 0 eXPenditure of £631 2s. 4d. 13.11 the .1/ Proposition of Mr. Antwisi, seconded —l'. Taylor, the accounts were adopted.</p>
        <p>The The Officers. *am. 'lector appointed Councillor Craik as his and Mr' Watson proposed and Mr. e,conded that Mr. E. Isherwood be the 14 :8 warden. Councillor Craik said he was proud high office; and Mr. Isherwood said `'lnn.Wl'l do all he could for the welfare of the</p>
        <p>Lckit (Illor Craik reappointed Councillor his sidesman, and Mr. Isherwood re- lt Mr. S. Taylor as his sidesman. eetor said they all hoped Mr. Taylo.r. tN,k,or 'undition was serious, would soon he kr r Craik broposed, and Councillor 4.pi tided, health. that the following gentlemen ;:,'Ciled-41eetsd sidemen: Messrs. IL W. Aiitirtss, E. ff. Coates, IL T. Harding, Joseph llopkin, E. E. Knott, IL Lee, Henry said he itt-1,5141 J. T. Whittle. tN f6lthl 4,34rw00d could not say ord "w far the clrilielibffkers. They all worked t and there was no friction in any shape 'l' °le was taken, but there was some dissent. Ivo .sehti tirther names were Submitted, but the decli tied to stand. tor,, e gentlemen were then re-elected nem sulnner was re-elected sidesman at St. tl -4e'f4 Mistion and Mr. Houghton at St. "lents' 1, • • C. Price and Mr. J. T. Whittle were IletiCed auditors, and 'Mr. E. A. White vestry , 4z. ',white said that was the 30th meeting be he wa`tended. In 1884, in the old crypt vestry, Rim 44.,13 Present as assistant clerk, and he was ey could once more meet in the vestry, quiser Present one was an ornament to the</p>
        <p>deLay Representative. arid kae recommend that Mr. E. H. Coates .r, •J. op ii H k* be the lay representatives. ftok 4t f.bk'orie asked if they had had any report th --e lay representatives. It was important ere should be one. They wanted to know h 4; the l'ePresentatives had done, and it was them tka,, 1.5' fair, lie thought, to re-elect ! before *4' "eW what they had done in the past. It rhiial Ii other— ltite:Pactor : Mr. Coates and Mr. Hopkin have ''041.1-'ed the conferences very regularly, and we 11°t have two more earnest and willing Iktt. lioDkin said they bad no chancewhant: sad :f telling the people what was going o icat: considered it was not fair to the parish- ra or the representatives. They could not call a special meeting for the purpose._ doing 41! it for granted that they a•ere doi IMtivi 'lector • Can you suggest any means. h3'ale-1.47°?1 cou'ld make it known to the Par's'i_ - 110Pkin • I think they ought to be able to 11N041. tePort •at some meeting called by the 4 • ),• P. k! °ates said if it were the ; generalwishhe on! too pleased to preparereport. ;4:tifiocjan a i Conference there was an nter- -1 een.ssion on church observances and the i t •)e , is . proposed that the representatives a Iseilliaated, and this was carried. 4'tor announced that the new Chancel- teeith 043' d hold a visitation court new tile i. 4 Ile Purpose of admitting w ;at't°lalaktilll'r Craik proposed, and Mr. Isherwood. ' a hearty vote of thanks to the w Eeet.o.i "4 I)reNid. irig, and this was carried ltil 'qty. iiThe Buildings Worth £62,440. tao4itietetor, in reply, said they had spent all i they had n hand for the repair fund. tri,eBl)ent nearly ..t750 on that ( vestry, and at t sae amount had been raised and 4:.1.4ed%m "t• Clement's. The total amount re- ;4B -911:1 expended during the past year was lola*l 4 1-* 6(1 which included everything. That tileteold w lit)sa parish,, atnl le covereamount ifnosr lu r 41areased the insurance at Hie Rectory th to -t5,000, as i