      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>ST. PETER'S CHURCH.</head>
        <p>ST. PETER'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The Rev. B. Percival presided at the vestry meeting in connection with St. Peter's Church on Wednesday evening. The statement of accounts, which was adopted unanimously, showed that there was a balance in hand of £l4. The following were the church officers re- elected : Churchwardens, Messrs. T. S. Steel and T. Reddish; lay representatives, Mefsrs. Jos. Bamfoilli and J. W. Kay; organist and choir- master, Mr. E. A. Padmore; sidemen, Messrs. Joseph Bamforth, James Taylor, J. W: Kay, Robert Knight, J. .T. Hands, E. Clayton, G. E. Greaves, R. Brownbill, G. J. McQuillan, T. lace, W. Pinson, W. Dickens, D. Armitage and R. Bennett; auditors, Messrs. R. Bennett and E. Padmore. Hearty votes of thanks to the officers and the Vicar for presiding concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>ST. BARNABAS' CHURCH. The Wardens Re-elected.</head>
        <p>ST. BARNABAS' CHURCH. The Wardens Re-elected.</p>
        <p>The St. Barnabas' vestty meeting was held on Thursday evening, when the Vicar, the Rev. J. If. G. Hollingivorth, presided. Mr. Boyle, people's warden, submitted the ac- counts, which showed that £ll 18s. 6d. was brought forward from last year, and that the total receipts amounted to £l7l 15s. 5d., leav- ing a balance in hand of £8 148. Id. Church Officers.</p>
        <p>The Vicar again nominated Mr. J. E. Morgan sidesman, Mr. Boyle being re-elected people's warden. The following sidesmen were elected : Messrs. B. Dimelow, E. Wakefield, G. Wakefield, Hinde, Jones, Mayhew, T. Evans, Jun., Kimberley, Minards, Oxley, Turner and Houghton.</p>
        <p>The Vicar; in thanking the church officers and workers, said he was very pleased with the work ,which had been done during last year, for the relationship between the wardens and sidesmen had been most cordial.</p>
        <p>_ Mr.. Boyle proposed a vote of thanks to the Vicar for presiding. This was seconded by Mr. Siddall and carried, the Vicar replying. At a Church Council meeting held later, Mr. Morgan and Mr. Siddall were appointed lay representatives.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>ST. ANN'S CIIURCII.</head>
        <p>ST. ANN'S CIIURCII.</p>
        <p>The Rev. James Davies, vicar, presided at the vestry and communicants' meetings at St. Ann's Church on Tuesday, and said he was indebted to the church officers, choir and parochial workers, as well as those who were making the prepara- tions for the sale of work. lie could not state the total cost of the reredos and the renovations, but he was hoping all the expenses would be met by the parish. The designer and those who had carried out the work were to be complimented on the result, for everyone was full of praise in regard to the work. lie trusted the sale would. be a very successful function, and thanked those who were responsible for the success which at- tended last year's effort. • Officers Re-elected. The following were the•officers re-elected : Mr. T. 11. Corbett (Vicar's warden), Mr. T. S. Jolley (people's warden), Messrs. G. Armstrong, A. Bradshaw, C. Bradshaw, T. J. Boswell, J. Hall, J. E. Harrison, C. A. Lea, A. Owen, F. Taylor, U. Striver and W. Thomas (sidesmen). Mr. G. Armstrong and Mr. T. S. Jolley were the lay representatives who were re-appointed. The balance sheet, presented by Mr. Jolley, showed the receipts .1102 lie. 4id., the general offertories arhounting to ..t6l 18s. 6d. and the special offertories .05 7s. ld. There was a bal- ance in hand of .16 18s. 81d; Votes of thanks to the Vicar and vestry clerk closed the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>INLY TRINITY CHURCH.</head>
        <p>INLY TRINITY CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The Holy Trinity vestry meeting was held on Tuesday evening, when the Vicar, the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton, presided. Mr. W. Gill (the Vicar's Warden) said the wardens considered the balance sheet a very satisfactory one, and they were very pleased with the support which had been given to them. They commenced the year with a balance in hand of 18s. 11d. Special offertories amounted to £B2 125., which was ,t 6 16s. 10d, less than the previous year. The general offertories were responsible for £lBB 2s. 11d., which was an increase on last year of £lB 2s. 4d., which could easily be accounted for by the fact that the year just closed was a longer one. The total receipts amounted to £299 38. 9d., and after paying £35 to the Curacy Fund and £lO to the Repair Fund, in addition to several special ex- penses there was a balance in hand of .21 2s. 4d. The accounts were passed, on the proposition of Mr. T. Welsby. Mr. T. D. Amos seconded. The accounts of the distribution of various charities were given by the Vicar.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>Officers' Election.</head>
        <p>Officers' Election.</p>
        <p>The Vicar again nominated Mr. W. Gill as his warden, referring to him in eulogistic terms: Mr. John Hesketh was re-elected people's war- den, on the proposition of Mr. Welsby, Mr. T. Doraville seconding. The lay representatives, Messrs. W. D. Jeans and E. Rose, were re-elected, as also was the auditor, Mr. 11. W. Naylor. The sidesmen were elected as follows: Messrs. Joseph Frignett, Thomas Welsby, James Bow- cock, Ernest Dyke, Frederick Taylor, Thomas Domville, James Harding, William Gaudy, G. H. Gill, J. J. Hesketh, Hugh Ferguson, Thomas Hunt, Henry Milling, Josiah Pickles, Edward Rose. Abe Walsh, T. Emmett, L. R. Rowson, W. W. Leigh, W. H. Turner, T. D. Amos, J. W. Dyke and W. E. Worthington. The Vicar, in proposing the re-appointment of Mr. G. F. F. White as the vestry clerk, said that gentleman was continuing the long connection of his family with the church—a connection which went back long before his (the Vicar's) time. Mr. Gill seconded, and the resolution was carried. Votes of thanks were accorded the 'officers and choir, and also the Vicar for presiding, Mr. Amos saying that no yes* meeting could be more harmonious.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <p>11111.1 EXAMINERS SATURDAY, APRIL 18, 1914.</p>
        <p>repudiate any suggestion of the kind. I shall certainly not be dictated to by anyone as to how I shall do my chum' work. Ido not think that there will be any great harmony at evening meetings if statements are rashly made without any proof whatever, and I shall regret if any alteration is made, if such things will take, place. _</p>
        <p>would be advantageous, and later, when the opportunity came, he (the Rector) was only too glad to offer him the living of Padgate. It had been a delight to the speaker to hear the warm expressions of regret oft all sides at the Yi.ear's death. (Hear, hear.) He believed Aire Dickin - Soh was a very earliest and spiritually-minded man. (Hear, hear.) The Canon said he had been inundated with applications for the living, but the Rev. H. P. S. Collier, who had accepted, woulf.! Ao doubt be a very faithful incumbent, and lie wag attre they would give him a very warm welcome. -.(Hear, hear.) Mr. Gorton said they did indeed feel the loss of their late Vicar very much. They would have great difficulty in finding one who could speak to them so eloquently and so well as he did. Tiler., would always .; remember that merry twinkle in his eye on all occasions. They thanked the Rector for the efforts he had made in trying to fill Mr. Dickinson's place. He (Mr. Gorton), as one who knew Mr. Collier intimately, could assure them all that in him they would find a most brotherly man, and one who would appeal to all.</p>
        <p>-Mr. Mather endorsed all that had been said. The meeting decided that a faculty be approved for the memorial to the late Vicar, which is to take the form of a communion table.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>IN THE DISTRICTS</head>
        <p>IN THE DISTRICTS</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>A WARDEN FOR FOURTEEN YEARS.</p>
        <p>The Stockton Heath vest iy. meeting was held on Tuesday, when the Vicar (the Rev. James Collins) occupied the chair. Mr. T. J. Lee (people's warden) stated that the ,accounts showed the receipts to be £312 16s. 10d., .linetliat.there was a balance in. the bank of Is.. lid. The accounts were passed. The Vicar said it was with sorrow that he heard that Mr. Henry Greenall was going to leave Hill Cline for Daresbury, and he thought the vestry ought to place on record its apprecia- tion of Mr. Green°,ll's valuable services, which had been rendered to the parish for 14 years as Vicar's warden.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pearson, in seconding, said Mr. Greenall had fulfilled the splendid traditions of a. family who for nearly a century had been associated with the parish. The thanks of the parish and the vestry were due to Mr. Greenall, Who had shown great loyalty to the church.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lee associated himself with the remarks, for he had worked with Mr. Greenall as co- warden and found him ever ready to give his advice. They would also be sorry to lose Mrs. Greenalli who had worked hard for the parish. (Hear, hear.) The resolution was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Elections.</head>
        <p>The Elections.</p>
        <p>The Vicar announced that lie had asked Mr. Lee to act as his warden, and he was glad to say he had accepted, for he knew the appointment would commend itself to, the parishioners gener- ally.</p>
        <p>On the proposition of Mr. T. R. Westbrook, seconded by Mr. A. J. Willett, and supported by Mr. W. Mclntyre, Mr. James Richardson, jun., was elected people's warden, and in reply he said it was, with diffidence that he allowed his name to go forward, because he realised that worthy men had filled the position, and because he thought there were others who had a greater claim. His father took a deep interest in the church for many years, and he (the speaker) would endeavour to do what would meet with his father's approval. (Hear, hear.) The Vicar, in proposing that the excellent ser- vices rendered by the late Mr. William Richard- son be put on record, said the deceased gentle- man was a zealous ,worker and church official. The motion was carried in silence.</p>
        <p>The Sidesmen.</p>
        <p>The following were re-elected sidesmen : Messrs. A. Down, T. It. Westbrook, W. Mclntyre, A. J. Willett, J. Melbourne, M. H. Brady, Thomas Jackson, A. Worrall, J. Swin- ton, S. McDowell, Albert Broady, F. Gandy, F. Pearson and F. Warburton, with the addition of three new sidesmen, namely, Messrs. J. H. Fletcher, Fred Berry and John Hallows. Messrs. W. Mclntyre and F. Pearson were re- elected lay representatives, the Vicar announc- ing that the Archdeacon's visitation would take place at Frodsltam Parish Church on April 24.</p>
        <p>Votes of thanks were accorded the church offi- cials and those subscribing to the freewill offer- ing scheme, whilst the Vicar took the opportu- nity of thanking the congregation for the Easter offertory.</p>
        <p>The "any other-business" part of the'meet- ing was responsible for a little scene towards the close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Working Men and the Church.</head>
        <p>Working Men and the Church.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. R. Smith said he was representing a good many men in the parish, who were unable to attend the vestry meetings because of the awkward time arranged. They had a number of men—Working men—who would like to attend the meetings, because they were interested in church work. He also spoke for the majority of their Church of England Men's Society. lie made a proposition that the time of the meet- ings in future be eight o'clock in the evening, instead of at 12 noon. It could be seen that the trend of the district was to hold night meetings, and instanced the Parish, St. Paul's, Christ Church and Lymm Churches as parishes where it had been thought desirable to hold night meet- ings. He had a petition signed by 25 gentlemen in favour of the alteration, and he trusted they would see the advisability of making a change. Mr. Lee said that it had been tried when Mr. Pigot was there, and only three people turned up.</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith: That was a good many years ago, and has nothing to do with to-day. Mr. J. W. Crossley, in seconding, characterised the proposition as a "quite reasonable and fair one," for it would benefit the church if they gave the interested people an opportunity of attending.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pearson said it was a matter for recom- mendation to the Vicar and churchwardens. The Vicar: The meeting cannot go beyond that.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lee remarked that there was not the slightest reason why the meeting should not be held at night to give them an opportunity of seeing how the experiment worked. Mr. Richardson said he welcomed the idea. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>Mr. Rose said he had been approached a num- ber of times during the year about the same thing.</p>
        <p>It WAS ultimately decided that it be a recom- mendation to the Vicar and churchwardens to hold an evening meeting next year.</p>
        <p>A "Scene."</p>
        <p>The Vicar read a notice inviting the officers to a meeting of the freewill offering scheme. Mr. Smith said he thought all the parish- ioners who subscribed to the scheme should have an opportunity of attendirlg. When it came out lie understood that it was to be a freewill offer- ing, but he, as one of those who was not sub- scribing, was tackled about it. That was hardly a freewill offering. If those who paid had the privilege of attending the meeting, it would not look as if there was any suspicion of any hole- and-corner work.</p>
        <p>Mr. Willett denied that there was any hole- and,corner work, adding that it was a question of raising the extra money necessary to carry on the work of the parish. Mr. Smith: In other parishes it had been quite usual to call a meeting of the subscribers. At the Warrington Parish and St. Paul's Churches the thing was undertaken in a demo, cratic manner, for a meeting of parishioners was called and officers were elected. If Church people were not allowed to attend the meetings, you cannot help suspicion arising. Mr. T. R. Westbrook: Has not the statement of accounts for the freewill offering scheme been published in the Magazine? Mr. Lee: Yes, and audited by a chartered accountant. "Surprised."</p>
        <p>Mr. Crossley said he was surprised at Mr. Smith. He (Mr. Crossley) did not know there was anything underhanded or wrong, or that the money was being applied for purposes other than those for which it was initiated. Mr:Brady : After what has been said I beg to offer my resignation as secretary of the scheme. (Cries of "No, No.") Mr. Smith: I have no wish to east any slurs . at all, but I think subscribers should have the opportunity of attending the meeting, . Mr. Lee: There was a meeting called. Mr. Smith: For officials only. Mr. Lee: I believe u. meeting was held in the Parish Room. Mr. Smith: I don't think so.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pearson: It is not at all within Mr. Smith's right to throw out insinuations. He should be kept in his place, Mr. Chairman.</p>
        <p>The Vicai 1,•I think it is time we closed.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pearson: I should like to express my in- dignation. There is nobody serving on any committee but who is anxious to do- his best! not only in the interests of the church, but also in the interests of the parishioners. Under the veiled insinuation which is thrown out, it is almost demanded that a certain course should be taken, implying, I take it, that the church officers have not been straight in the matter. I</p>
        <p>ditch, E. Daniels and W. H. Houghton; people's sidesmen, Messrs. W. Jump, W. S. Howard, Sen.,W. Potter, Woolston sides- BGc.igHtroAugllleitnonan, dTF. Aj.ntwrorbiguhei. men : 'Messrs. R. Ryder and H. Stafford. Messrs. F. Gidman and R. Denham were re- elected auditors, whilst Mr. F. Blackwell was appointed vestry clerk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Time to e lose.</head>
        <p>Time to e lose.</p>
        <p>31r. Janeway said he it was merely A question of language. _</p>
        <p>- The Vicar : f think it is time we cloeed.</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith: Cannot I say a word in reply?</p>
        <p>The Vicar: Yes.</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith: I very much regret that the meet- ing should have taken what I said in the way they have done. I did not wish to cast any in- sinuation—l would sooner stop away than do so —but the meeting seemed to overlook the fact that some men, because of their opportunities, were able to express themselves clearer than other men could.</p>
        <p>- The Vicar: I think it is time we closed now. A vote of thanks was passed to the Vicar for presiding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>WALION.</head>
        <p>WALION.</p>
        <p>A FAVOURABLE BALANCE.</p>
        <p>The Rev.L. J.T. Darwall presided at the meeting held on Easter Monday, and was handed by Mr. J. Cooper, the people's warden, a cheque for £l6 13s. 7d., the result of the Easter offerings. Mr. Cooper also submitted the accounts, which showed the receipts at £134 9s. 11d., and pay- ments at £lO9 us., leaving £24 18s. 11d, in hand. The accounts were passed. The Vicar presented a statement showing that Sir Gilbert Greenell had paid salaries and accounts amounting to 4264 odd.</p>
        <p>The Rev. L. J. T. Darwell proposed Sir Gilbert Greenall as Vicar's warden, and Mr. John Cooper was re-elected people's warden. Messrs. William Weir and John Cooper were re-elected to the diocesan conferenee, and the sidesmen were continued in office.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>GRAPPENtiALL</head>
        <p>GRAPPENtiALL</p>
        <p>ACCORDINd TO OLD CUSTOM.</p>
        <p>An old custom which hak been maintained for mail), years, the vestry meting of the Grap- penhall Parish Church was held on Easter Monday morning. The Rev. H. V. Pigot (the rector) presided. Mr. Pigot proposed Mr. Henry Standish Layland as his warden, and Mr. Oswald Lofthouse was elected as the people's warden. The Rector nominated Mr. Reginald William Wilde as sidesman, and the meeting elected as sidesmen the following; Messrs. J. E. Ashcroft, T. Brierley, W. W. Dimelow, A. Evans, J. Leigh, W. Lomas, H. N. Morrey, H. T. Nicholls, W. Peacock, T. Sharrocks, N. Smith, H. A. Thomp- son, T. Whyman, J. Williams and W. Winstan- ley. Mr. A. Evans and Mr. T. Sharrocks were elected lay representatives for dioc&amp;an and ruri- decanal conferences. Mr. Arthur Down was given a vote of thanks for the services he had rendered as organist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>PIiDGAI E.</head>
        <p>PIiDGAI E.</p>
        <p>THE NEV VICAR'S COMING.</p>
        <p>The vestry meeting of Christ Church, Padgate, was held on Wednesday evening, when the Rec- tor of Warrington (Canon F. W. Willis) was elected the chairman. Many touching refer- ences were made to the late Vicar, and promises of loyal support to.the.new Vicar were tendered. In accordance with the decision at the previous vestry meeting, the accounts only represented the nine months ending December 31, 1913. The idea of this was that next year's vestry meet- ing reports would cover from January Ist to December 31st, and so overcome the uneven number of Sundays that are in the year if accounts are rendered from one Easter to an- other, owing to that day being a movable feast.</p>
        <p>The Accounts.</p>
        <p>The accounts, which were accepted unani- mously, showed that they commenced the nine months with a deficiency of £lB 13s. 6d., which by December 31st had been reduced to Bs. 10d., the total income amounting to £lO5 15s. The Vicar and churchwardens' special accounts showed that they commenced with a balance in hand of £26 ps. 11d.; the total receipts aggre- gated .i:35 ss. 10d., leaving a balance in hand of £29 4s. .10d. The Woolston accounts, also agreed to, showed that out of an income of £44 18s. Bd. there was a credit babince of £35 Bs. 3d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Election of Officers.</head>
        <p>Election of Officers.</p>
        <p>Owing to the peculiar position the meeting was placed in with regard to the election of Vicar's warden, it was agreed that Mr. H. T. Houghton, who had worked with the late Mr. Dickinson, should vain undertake the office. Mr. P. Royston was elected people's warden. These gentlemen were also elected lay repre- sentatives. The following sidesmen were appointed: Vicar's sidesmen, Messrs. R. Denham, G. Mather, E. Gorton, J. P. Critchley, H. O. Hil-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>ORFORD.</head>
        <p>ORFORD.</p>
        <p>THE VICAR'S TRIBUTE.</p>
        <p>The vestry meeting for the parish of Orford was held in the choir vestry of St. Margaret's Church, Orford, on Tuesday evening. The Vicar, Rev, G. Vale Owen, presided over a poor attendance.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the previous meeting were read by the vestry clerk, Mr. R. G. Brandreth, and adopted, on the proposition of Mr. W. Knowles, seconded by Mr. F. E. White.</p>
        <p>The accounts showed an increase in income, but also an increase in expenditure. The col- lections for church expenses amounted to £55 is. 9d., compared with £5l 4s. 7d. the previous year; sick and general fund, £lO ss. 3d.; Sunday school, £3 15e. id.; choir fund, £1 68. 2d.; col- lections for outside charitable objects, "..,6 Os. 4d.; vicarage fund, ...t6 9s. id; Easter offering to Vicar, £4 ss. 11d.; Lenten offerings for Waifs and Strays, 9s. 10d.; refunded subscription, 10s.; making a total income of ,tBB 3s. 5d., as against ..t,83 2s. sd. for the previous year. General church expenses amounted to .C4B 18e. 7d., com- pared with £44 Is. 7d. the previous year. Re- payment of loan, £4 3s. 2d., leaving a credit balance of se.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Election of Officers.</head>
        <p>Election of Officers.</p>
        <p>The Vicar said he was pleased to say 'that Mr. J. Swinton had again consented to be Vicar's warden. They had always worked very amicably together, and as long as Mr. Swinton would con- tinue to serve as his warden he would be pleased to have his help. Mr. Swinton thanked the Vicar for his kind words, and said it was a pleasure to work with him.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Povey was re-elected people's warden unanimously, on the proposition of Mr. E. Brownbill, seconded by Mr. J. Swinton. The sidesmen elected for the ensuing year were as follows: Messrs. It. G. Brandreth, It. Monks, 11. Williams, E. Brownbill, L. Cross and G. Simcock. The Vicar's warden appointed Mr. L. Cross as his sidesmen, and the people's warden appointed Mr. 11. Williams. Mr. W. Knowles proposed and Mr. J. Swinton seconded, that Mr. R. G. Brandreth be re-elected vestry clerk. This was carried unanimously. Messrs. F. E. White, G. Simcock and C. B. Woods were appointed auditors. Messrs. It. G. Brandreth and F. E. White were elected ruri- decanal representatives.</p>
        <p>Vicar's Thanks. In a short speech the Vicar said: First, I should like to thank all our very good workers, comprising sidesmen, Sunday school teachers, magazine distributors, and all Church workers. I am sure all have worked amicably together during the year, and have helped very loyally and well in the work, and I want to thank them on your behalf for helping to run this growing and developing parish. In regard to the church itself, we may feel thankful that we do not live in what are usually called "those good old times." I believe in the year 1800, in that big cathedral of St. Paul's. London, there were only six communicants. From that time things began to develop in the Church, and in the year 1900, not in St. Paul's, but in little Orford School, there were 41 communicants. That will show you what a difference there was between 1900 and those good old times, a century ago. In 1908 we built the church, and indeed it has justified its existence. One indication of the justification is the number of Easter communi- cants. This year, instead of 41, we had 140 com- municants on Easter Sunday morning. We can thank God and take courage, and it just shows us whether after all the church WAS wanted in Orford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>ONE WAY To Master Rheumatism.</head>
        <p>THE ONE WAY To Master Rheumatism.</p>
        <p>There are still some people who imagine,that Rheumatism can be cured by embrocations', and outward applications, overlooking the fact that the trouble is rooted in the blood. It follows, therefore, that it can be treated successfully only by cleansing and enriching the blood, by which method the poisonous acids of Rheuma- tism are driven out of the system. There is just this one way to really cure Rheu- matism—through the blood; and because Dr. Williams' Pink Pills act promptly by purifying impure, weak blood and making new, good blood, they have cured a great number of cases of Rheumatism in this country, including many that had defied all previous treatments. Every town and village contains people who know by experience the curative value of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills in disorders of a Rheumatic nature. Lumbago is a very painful form of muscular rheumatism affecting the muscles of the back and loins that has also yielded in countless cases to Dr. Williams' Pink Pills; and in Sciatica— that sudden, piercing agony that shoots down the thighs and legs—these Pills have been equally successful in giving freedom from tor- ture. • • You cannot afford to waste time and money on useless, external treatments. Start to-day on the blood-cure for Rheumatism, that is, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, and so drive the disease out of your system. All dealers sell them; or direct from Dr. Williams' Medicine Co.. 4G, Holborn-viaduct, London, 2s. 9d. a box, or 13s. 9d. for six, pOst free. FREE.—A Book on the Blood sent free to any reader sending postcard to Book Dept., 4G, llol- born-viaduct, London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Canon Willis's Tribute.</head>
        <p>Canon Willis's Tribute.</p>
        <p>The Rector said it was with deep grief that he took the place of their late Vicar. Mr. Dickin- son was more likep a son to him than anything else. He was working for him for six years, and for good reasons, he thought another sphere</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>FALSE PRETENCES.</p>
        <p>Man Sentenced to Three Months.</p>
        <p>"This is a case where one's feelings are in- clined to outrun one's discretion," declared Mr. A. 'Browne, who prosecuted in a case at the Police Court on Thursday, when Henry Ather- ton, 11, Bowe-street, Bolton, was charged on remand with obtaining a bed, value £2, by false pretences, from• Mr. Firth, late Police Court Missioner at Warrington. Prisoner said it was an accident. Mr. Browne said the actions of the man and the woman with whom' he-was living were dis- gusting. On the 22nd November, 1912, the man came to the town, with a woman and child. They applied for assistance and stayed at a lodging-house for two nights, obtaining the money by begging. They went to a cottage in Stony-yard. There was nothing in the house, and they were sleeping on the cold flags. There was straw on the floor and all the windows were broken. They told Mr. Firth and Mr. Wain- wright (N.S.P.C.C.) that they were married at Wigan Parish Church. Mr. Firth went out of his way more in this case than in any other, and he got a quantity of furniture and food, and also got friends to intervene. The woman was in a certain condition, and Mrs. Willis, the Rector's wife, got all the things ready. The man was found regular work, and Mr. Firth and Mr. Wainwright made his money up. Mr. Firth went down on Sundays to see if they had enough food. Believing they were man and wife, Mr. Firth got a bed. The child was born on Insur- ance Day, and Mrs. Harris and the charity organisation gave the woman help. Prisoner sold the furniture for 14s. Cd. and then disappeared. It was found out that he was not married to the woman. He was apprehended at Bolton. A considerable amount of money had been spent on the couple. "It is a right-down bad case," concluded Mr. Browne.</p>
        <p>Prisoner said he got two broken chairs, one little table, one picture, and two blankets.</p>
        <p>The Clerk: What have you to --say aout falsely representing you were married? Prisoner: We should have IDeen married only for— _</p>
        <p>Prisoner, who had been before the Wigan Court four times, was sentenced to three months, with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY. Before Mr. R. T. Fairclough..(in the chair) and</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. Jolley. Absent Four Years.—Charles Jones, French polisher, 52, Frances-street, Chester, was charged with deserting his wife, Sarah Jones, Smith's- Court, Winwick-road, in 1910.—The wife said she had not heard from her husband since he went way, and he had not let her have any money. She did not think he would send her anything if he could get out of it. She was earning 9s. per week at a tannery.—The Chief Constable said they could now find the man any- where.—Jones said he had only been doing odd jobs.—An order was made for 10s. per week, and prisoner had to pay the costs of the case. Girl's Plight.—Kate Fleming, a domestic ser- vant, of Wakefield-street, who had previously been before the Bench on a charge of attempted suicide, again appeared, and the Chief said the girl, who was in service in Wilderspool-road, was removed to the Infirmary and the Work- house after taking the poison. The Medical Officer reported that she was weak, but thought she would be all right if under proper care and control.—The girl's parents promised to look after her, and she was placed under the super- vision of Mr. Laws for 12 months. Man and a Knife.—A charge of wounding a 16-year-old girl, named Lily Lawson, of 3, Brack- ley-street, preferred against Albert Ernest Gough, who lodged with the girl's mother, was reduced to one of aggravated assault.—The girl said the man had lodged with her mother for three years, and he was violent when in drink. On Thursday evening she was nursing a baby, when prisoner came in and became quarrelsome. He picked up a knife from the table and said, "Give me the baby or I will cut its throat." She (witness) struggled, but prisoner deliber- ately struck her in the eye with the knife, in- flicting a slight wound. When asked for an explanation, prisoner said it was an accident.— Mrs. Lawson said the man was decent and re- spectable and kept in his own place when he was sober.—P.C. Cooke said that when he saw the woman she said he (prisoner) would murder her. They were afraid he would do them some injury. —Prisoner said it was an accident. He would , pay all the expenses and sign the pledge.—He ' was sent to prison for one month. What the Sergeant Heard.—Michael Preston, 37, Bewsey-street, was charged with breaking into the lock-up shop of Mr. Ryder, jeweller, 11, Bewsey-street.—The Chief Constable said that Acting-Sergeant Whitby heard an unusual noise in Bewsey-street that morning, and on making a search found the prisoner at the rear of the premises with a long iron bar in his possession. Prisoner made a small excuse. The bolts of the door had been broken, but although prisoner had got into the back of the shop he had not reached the shop properly.—The case was ad- journed for a week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>MONDAY.</head>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Dr. Joseph- (i.n- the chair), Aldermen Bolton, Smethurst and James Evans, Messrs. W. Roberts, C. S. Burgess and G. Pilling. Police Assault. —When arrested for 'being drunk and disorderly by P.C. Wills, William 11. Platt, 37, Golborne-street, struck the officer on the nose and became very violent. He had pre- viously invited a landlord to fight, threatening to "kill him."—Platt said he had been to India, and a drop of drink went to his head.—He was fined ss. and costs, or in default seven dais' imprisonment. Fell Under a Cab.—Two women, Honor Curley and Annie Noon, were drunk in Winwick-road, and when a cab driven by a man named Drink- water was approaching one of them staggered and fell. The driver had no -chance to pull up, and the woman was hurt. They were taken to the Infirmary in the cab, and when at the Police Station they were helplessly drunk.—The Chief Constable said there was no question of neglect on the part of the driver.—The women were dis- charged. "Fed Up With the Army."—"l was fed up with it," was the excuse of John Nicholson, who was charged with being a deserter from the King's Liverpool Regiment.—Detective-Sergeant Holland arrested the prisoner, who said he had burned his clothes.—Alderman Bolton : What did you do with the buttons?—No answer.—The Chief Constable said it did ,not take much for the prisoner to get fed up. He was fed up with being there. (Laughter.)—The Chairman, re- marking on prisoner's ruddy countenance: He looks well off it.—Prisoner was, detained until the arrival of an escort, when he was handed over.—Detective-Sergeant Holland was recom- mended for the reward.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY. Before Councillor Roberts (in the chair), Alder- Messrs. J. Rymer Young, C. Jack- sonma n Bolton,n es and C. S. Burgess. Drink to Biame.—Mabel Alice Kelley, of Manchester, was charged on remand with steal- ing a pair of scales, value .R 1 Is., from the shop of Mr. Tickle, 9, Scotland-road.--The Chief Con- stable said the ssales were missed, and later Mr. Tickle saw the woman carrying something under her shawl in Winwick-road. He followed her, and in the Market-place he met P.C. Gibbs. As they were talking the prisoner said: "If you don't stop following me about I shall give you into custody." Gibbs searched her and found the scales under her shawl. The woman bad said she did it to spite her husband, and also that she was sent in a shop to buy cigarettes, and that the man for whom she was buying the cigarettes gave her the scales.—Prisoner said if it was not for tile drink she would scorn to do such a thing. She had had trouble with her husband, and had had five weeks of black eyes. —She was sent to prison for 21 days. A Chance.—A case in which John Isherwood, formerly of II aydock, was charged with run- ning away and leaving his wife and child chargeable, was adjourned for four weeks.— Prisoner had been ordered to pay 15s. per week at the Newton Petty Sessions, and he now said he had been looking for a house.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY. Before Messrs. J. W. Hayward (in the chair), W. T. Williams, C. Jackson Holmes, and H. Thornton.</p>
        <p>Theft of Boots.—Jamea Higgins, was charged with stealing two pairs of men's boots, value 2s. 6(1., from the Workhouse, between the 9th</p>
        <p>If there isn't a "Benefit</p>
        <p>Bridge Str WARRINGTO</p>
        <p>and 11th of April, and also with absconding from the Workhouse and taking a suit of clothes. —Mr. Shearer said that two pairs of boots were stolen from the tailor's shop at the Workhouse. Prisoner had been discharged from the hospital and had been in the general part of the house. After he went away he returned the next morn- ing, but left the sleeping cell. On neither occa- sion did he pass the lodge gates. On the Satur- day he turned up for his breakfast and admitted getting over the wall, taking with him a suit and a pair of slippers. He further admitted taking the boots, and said he became frightened and threw them over the wall, where they were found. He had been a source of nuisance to the Guardians for some time. He had been ad- mitted and discharged 25 times within the last two years. He was one of the "ins and outs." He never worked and was difficult to control in the institution.—Prisoner, who promised to pull himself together, was sent to prison for six months.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY. Before Messrs. J. Fairclough (in the chair), J.• Rymer Young, W. Roberts, A. J. Jolley, W. T. Williams and Dr. Naden. Motor Cyclist Fined.—Ernest Harrison, point oiler, Dundalk-lane, Ditton (who did not ap- pear), was summoned for riding a motor cycle without having a licence.—P.C. Clarke said he stopped defendant in Winwick-road, • and he said he had left his licence in his other pocket. —The Chief Constable said the result of in- quiries was that the man had no licen, e and that he had given the name and address of his brother, who owned the cycle. He treated the Court with contempt and endeavoured to get out of the case by fraud. If his story had been true he would only have been cautioned.—A fine of 408. and costs was imposed.</p>
        <p>t</p>
