      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY. Before Messrs. J. J. Bleckly (in the chair), W. Fairclough, J. Rymer Young, and Councilor Roberts.</p>
        <p>Cyclist's Offence.—William Redhead, 70. Crown-street. Earlestown, was fined 2s. 6d. for riding his bicycle on the footpath at Burton- wood. It was stated that the road was danger- ous and many complaints in regard to cyclists had been received. Unfit Horse.—John Fisher, Gray's Cottage, Penketh, was summoned for working a horse in an unfit state, and Eliza Fisher, Tannery-lane Farm, Penketh, was summoned for causing the horse to be worked.—lnspector Dee (R.S.P.C.A.) said the animal was lame, suffering from a very swollen fetlock joint.—Defendants said the horse had been in the field for ten weeks and they thought it was all right. The man was only going to fetch coal with it—The cases were dis- missed on payment of costs.</p>
        <p>Shooting at Bisley, Corporal Ommundsen, the King's Prize man, made a score of 101 out of a possible 105 under King's Prize (first stage) con- ditions.</p>
        <p>A boy living near Old Kent-road, London, picked up four .15 bank-notes in the City early last week and took them to the police. During the week-end he received a money order for £5 from the owner, a justice of the peace living in South London.</p>
      </div>
