      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, APRIL 18, 1914.</p>
        <p>SOME THOUGHTS ON OUR DRINK BILL.</p>
        <p>According to an interesting article— sadly interesting in many respects— sent ,to "The Times" by Mr. G. B. WILSON, the drink bill of the United Kingdom increased last year by no less</p>
        <p>a sum than £5,000,000, making an already large total of £161,553,000 into the record total of £166,681,000, which is almost equivalent to the cost of run- ning the whole government of the</p>
        <p>nation'. The worst cloud is said to have a silver lining, and Mr. WILSON leaves actual for relative figures to find his crumb of comfort in face of this start- ling advance. Last year was one of great properity-7-the best authorities say ii was the most prosperous, indus- trially and commercially, in our history. People had more to spend, and the drink trade benefited along with other trades, which was nattu al if regrettable ; but it is to the fact that</p>
        <p>the year was a . good one that Mr. ,WiLsoN points to show that there are causes for satisfaction in the figures he quotes. In the years 1874 and 1899 wit had outstanding periods of industPial activity, and he compares our expendi- ture on drink in 1913 with those two periods on a population basis. Our numbers have increased at a much greater rate than our drink bill. Had the one kept pace with the other, pro- portionately, we should have paid away for drink last year £242,000,000, or over £75,000,000 more than it really was. The time between these two dates is, almost to a year, the time thab has elapsed since the passing of the Educa- tion Act, and we must put to the credit of the growing enlightenment of the people the relatiye improvement that has taken place in their habits as repre- sented by a smaller consumption per. head of the population of alcoholic liquor. It would have been well if the improvement had been more pro- nounced, but deep-seated customs are difficult to tackle and hard to overcome. When we are considering the bright side of a dark subject we must, of course, take into account the fact that 4'40,000,000 of this money went into the National Exchequer. Although that</p>
        <p>cannot be urged as an excuse for the evils of the drink traffic it is something</p>
      </div>
