      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>FLOUR</head>
        <p>FLOUR</p>
        <p>SELF RAISING</p>
        <p>y tight. led.</p>
        <p>to know that so considerable an amount must be deducted from the gross sum spent iewe are to find the actual amount spent on consumed alcohol. The most wonderful phase of the whole question is revealed when we consider the money spent on drink per head of.the population. Last year the per head expenditure was £3 12s. 5d., which means thdt every familk in the land on the average spent over £lB a year in drink! As there are 14,000,000 chil- dren under fifteen years of age, and as there are more women than men in the land, and women are not, to any extent, isthae ggrise-eant drinking, and eanndabasstatinheerrse, balance consisting of adult males who do drink must drink mightily to make up the average to the formidable figure at which it stands. One some- times hears the statement that the upper classes and the clubs are largely responsible for our inflated drink bill, but at the 9,295 registered clubs, only £3,500,000 worth of drink was sold last year, which is less than a mere 2i per cent. of the total bill. On the other hand we had one licensed wine or beer shop for every 200 of the population over 20 years of age, which is greatly in excess of healthy requirements, especially when we once again deduct the women and the men abstainers. On the whole, however, we are in- clined to follow Mr. WILSON'S lead and view the matter in an optimistic spirit. That the drinking habits of the people are diminishing is, in itself, a gratify- ing sign of the times. Further,.educa- tion will make further inroads upon a, wasteful practice, and will leave a greater margin for the enjoyment of that fuller life which has grown side by side with improving trade and the diminution in the consumption of alco- hol. The standard of life has been raised to an astonishing degree—we shall be greatly astonished if we takel a long view. Contrast, for instance, the year 1874 with•the year 1913, and a more marvellous change than the! decrease in the 'consmption of drink will be apparent. Where were the funds in the former year for the bicycles, the motors, the week-ends in the country, and the annual holidays that are now so common in the ranks! of the working classes? Where were' the place's of amusements and the foot- ball " gates "? Where were the sub- urbs of modern dwelling-houses, and the cars to take their occupants to them? The period is short, but the change is great, and though, even at a fairly accelerated rate, we shall be a long time in reaching the Millenium, still we are progressing at an ever- increasing speed. The immediate future will see the evils connected with the land and housing tackled as those of sickness and *old age have been tackled, and those who know best are not without hope that conditions of life will be so much improved that there will soon be a fuller meaning to his words than GOLDSMITH ever dreamed of when he wrote that ht this favoured country even the peasant claims to "venerate himself as man." In that day we may take it that our drink bill will be actually as well as relatively down.</p>
        <p>• , -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>TIIE " HATED " ACT.</head>
        <p>TIIE " HATED " ACT.</p>
        <p>Mr. IfonnousE, the Postmaster- General, evidently does not believe that men of intelligence regard the Insurance Act as an imposition which every one would evade if he could. Speaking as a guest at the dinner of the Association of Sub-Postmasters, Mr. HOBHOUSE congratulated himself and his audience that the decision which excluded sub-postmasters from the benefit of the Act is being re-consid- ered. The applause with which his words were received shows that the sub-postmasters, at any rate, take a , rational view of the "Hated "Act. The desperate straits to which the agitation against it is being reduced is proved by the latest mare's-nest of the "Daily Mail." According to that ingenuous organ a doctor who certifies a man who wants sickness benefit to be suffering from a disagreeable disease is exposed to the risk of a libel action. The doctor would only give such a certificate if re- quested by the patient. It could not possibly be libellous for a doctor to tell a patient that he seemed to be suffering from a disease which he might not wish to be recorded. The whole affair is a monstrous mare's-nest, discovered at the eleventh hour by those who have engineered a discreditable agitation, and find it breaking down under the . X-rays of experience.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>THE</head>
        <p>THE</p>
        <p>SATURDAY,</p>
        <p>THE PASSING WEEK.</p>
        <p>THE STRIKE: A PLEA FOR PEACE. The notice • issued by Mr. W. H. Rudge, the secretary for MeSsrs. Fletcher, Russell and Co., and the re- joinder issued on behalf of the work- men of the firm, show how near agreement the two are. There has been what cannot but be termed an exhausting strike for both sides to the dispute, and all that remains between the parties is a misunderstanding as to the method by which the men are to be taken on again if the strike is brought to a close. Of course, the most vital questions are bound up with the taking on of men after a dispute, but if there has been a misunderstanding on the point the sooner the misunderstanding is cleared out of the way the better. Firms cannot afford to risk the loss of business, and men, with their families, cannot be allowed to suffer unneces- sarily for such a cause. When our representative spoke to the Mayor last evening, his Worship, with his facility ' for gripping essentials, said that if there was a display of common-sense there should be no difficulty in bring- ing' about peace. It would ill become outsiders like ourselves to suggest how a firm should conduct its business, and say whether Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and Co. should open their works gradually, taking on only a few men as occasion required, or should open them to all the men at once and work short time or alternate weeks until business reaches its normal level. But as a part of the community that suffers while any.. portion of the community suffers we are entitled to say that no effort should be spared to bring the two sides together, with a view to closing a dispute that distresses the whole town. In this case the firm allege a misunderstanding and the men sug- gest a conference to /clear away that misunderstanding. To independent minds standing outside the dispute there appears here the nucleus of an ' effort that would result in peace. Will any man on either side care to risk the responsibility for standing in the way of peace achieved on these lines when peace seems so well within reach? There should be a settlement, and a settlement soon. The best interests of all concerned require it, and . common-sense will suggest the methods by which it can be achieved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>VESTRY MEETINGS.</head>
        <p>THE VESTRY MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>Vestry meetings are not what they used ;to be—in some places, at all events. By a queer paradoxical state of affairs this specially sacred time was once largely made use of for the vent- ing of grievances,, real or imaginary, and violent scenes were thick upon the ground. This year scarcely a ripple disturbed the serenity of the proceed- ings. A sad note was struck at the Padgate meeting, where there is a kind of interregnum owing to the death of the Rev. G. W. Dickinson, whose suc- cessor was promised a cordial welcome when he comes to the parish.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>AN "ARMY OF PEACE."</head>
        <p>AN "ARMY OF PEACE."</p>
        <p>Sir EDWARD CARSON has been ad- dressing the Ulster Volunteers at Belfast, and declaring them to be "an army of peace." They are out, he says, "as soldiers of the KING, and are wil- ling at any moment to tender their services to the KING, who is the emblem of the unity of our great Empire." The utterance is not very illuminating, except in so far as it tends to show .that there is a much greater national affinity of intellect between the type of Irishmen which Sir EDWARD CARSON represents and the type with which he refuses to recognise' a common nationhood. The slower intellect of the Saxon cannot follow Sir EDWARD'S intellectual processes. If the Ulster Volunteers are " willing at any moment to tender their services to the KING" as an "army of peace," would they do so if disturbances of the peace were threatened or caused owing to the Royal Assent being given to the Home Rule Bill? However that may be, Sir EDWARD CARSON'S language on this occasion was more encouraging to those who earnestly desire a settle- ment than, for example, his Hyde Park ' oratory.</p>
        <p>It has, perhaps, conduced to, the clarifying of the general publics Views of the Irish question that, after the second reading of the Home Rule Bill, the Easter holidays have brought a respite from platform discussion of that question. The very fact of that respite has. practically ruled out one misconception which the Opposition have sedulously fostered. They cannot really believe that only a General Elec- tion or a Referendum stands between this country and civil war. Men who honestly held such a belief could not be content to let off rhetorical squibs in Hyde Park and then take a conventional recess. Nor would they have been satisfied, for tactical reasons, to leave the PRIME MINISTER'S seat unchal- lenged, however ,desperate the enter- prise of challenging it would have been. The problem of reconciling the prejudices and suspicions of "Orange" Ulster, while satisfying the legitimate aspirations of the great majority of the Irish nation, is extremely delicate and difficult. But it is not one whichL should strike despair into British statesmanship, which, after all, has solved still harder problems in Canada and South Africa. It would, of course. be less difficult if the Opposition had not entangled it with their irrepressible plans and hopes for upsetting the Par- liament Act.</p>
        <p>ARROL JOHNSTON CARS are excellent value; 4-seater complete £2Bs.—Sole agent for War- rington end district, J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>NOTABLE WOMAN LIBERAL.</head>
        <p>A NOTABLE WOMAN LIBERAL.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Brocklehurst, Butley Hall, near Maccles- field, who died on Sunday in her 91st year-, was an enthusiastic Liberal. She was the widow of the late Mr. William Coare Brocklehurst, who was for many years one of the two Liberal Members who represented the Borough of Macclesfield in Parliament prior to the town being merged into the Macclesfield Division under the last Redistribution Act. She was the mother of the present Member for the Maccles- field Division, Colonel W. B. Brocklehurst, and took a leading part in his election in 1906, when the seat was wrested from Colonel Bromley Davenport, who had held it for the Tories since 1886. T.o celebrate the recovery of the seat for Liberalism, and in memory of her husband, she presented the Macclesfield Liberals with a club and hall, at a cost of £12,000.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>REFINED IMPUDENCE.</head>
        <p>REFINED IMPUDENCE.</p>
        <p>People in Warrington Who receive letters by post from London unstamped, and with "2d. to pay," would do well to hesitate before accept- ing them. The Post Office officials might then take steps to prevent a fraud, and the perpetra tion of a nuisance. Mr. George Smith, Orford - Green, recently had one of these missives, and on opening the envelope found the contents re- ferred to a foreign lottery! He would be a bold man who would risk his money with those who send out unstamped letters, but the satisfaction of " cutting " such a concern is scarcely suffi- cient recompense for having been cheated out of twopence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>"SUNRISE."</head>
        <p>"SUNRISE."</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett's "Sunrise Annual,' as usual, surveys the world literally from Pole to Pole, and contains plenty of readable, gossipy matter, relating to local affairs. Those who would like to read a single article dealing with such matters as Warrington Bridge and the ' naked hideousness of the Home Rule Bill, Chinese women's feet and Colonial preference, Ulster blood and the finding of a snake in War- rington (illustrated), local councillors who turn blue with fright because they are asked to give more air space to the houses of the town, and a resurrection of "the desolating non-trading agitation," witb many other striking contrasts, vill find plenty of entertainment in our lively little friend. This tremendods aggregation of facts-Land fancies—is not all that "sunrise " contains. There are entertaining, articles of various self-contained topics which it is well to have placed on record in such compact form.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>MAGISTRATES WHO SIGN THE "COVENANT."</head>
        <p>MAGISTRATES WHO SIGN THE "COVENANT."</p>
        <p>• The resignation of Mr. T. C. Horsf all from the position of magistrate for the City of Man- chester, after having served in that position for 28 years, calls pointed attention of others to the duty to their conscience and their King—or their common-sense. Mr. Florsfall has signed the British "Covenant," which promises, in a vague way, assistance to " Ulster " against the British Government. The Covenant, which some few have been deluded into signing, says:</p>
        <p>_ "1. —, of —, earnestly convinced that the claim of the Government to carry the Home Rule Bill into law without submitting it to the judgment of the nation is contrary to the spirit of our Constitution, do hereby soleinnly declare that if that Bill is so passed I shall hold myself justified in taking or supporting any action that may be effective to prevent it being put into Operation, and, more particularly, to prevent the armed forces of the Crown being used to deprive the people of Ulster of their rights a,5, citizens of the United Kingdom."</p>
        <p>The wording is delightfully non-committal in some respects, but Mr. Horsfall is too honest to Play with words. He knows that the promise is one of support against authority. He is part of authority and he cannot play on both sides. We have always inclined to the side that believes Ulster Orange leaders are bluffing, in which case there will be no rebellion. If it is a game of bluff, those who are playing with fire may escape the consequences attached to a real conflagration. If Ulster is not bluffing, and fighting is intended, then those who sign the Covenant are aiding and abetting a rebellious movement. Covenanters are on the horns of a dilemlna—either they are making themselves ridiculous, or they are taking the most serious step known to a civilised State. Mr. Horsfall believes in the bona-fides of the Covenant he has signed, and has resigned as a magistrate. A justice of the peace, on taking up his office, makes a Declaration and takes the Oath of Allegiance are US follows: and the Judicial Oath. Their terms</p>
        <p>DECL &amp;RATION. I' hereby declare that I will faithfully and impartially execute the office of Justice of the Peace for the Borough of accord- ing to the best of my judgment and ability.</p>
        <p>OATH OF ALLEGIANCE. I' swear by Almighty God that I will be faithful and bear true allegiance to His Majesty King George the Fifth, his heirs and successors, according to law. So help me God. 4 JUDICIAL OATH. I, swear by,Almighty God that I will Well and truly serve our Sovereign Lord, King George the Fifth, in the office of Justice of the Peace, and I will do right to all manner of people after the laws and usages of the realm without fear or favour, affection, or ill-will. So help me God. We. were not privileged to know anything of 'the signing locally of the Covenant, although we made repeated application for information, but "The Warrington Guardian" published a picture showing two local magistrates appar- ently "about to sign the British Covenant," on table from which was suspended this specious incitement:— • txh\te;el i.elef3, r. Are you going to lend John Redmond the British Army to shoot down the Loyal inhabi- tantsof Ulster? If not, step inside and sign I . BritishNesiNn more about it. - thatCs logvneeniaha 1:e t o."rC "Covenant" nsahnotu"l should was note heardl-ery</p>
        <p>THE SPIRIT OF NATURE. I have a violent longing in my heart For something which I cannot well define; Methinks it must be yearning for a soul That lives and moves ,in harmony with mine.</p>
        <p>My spirit is perturb'd, I cannot tell What makes it so, and yet I guess 'Tis searching for that peace it knew before This life—this life of woes and little happi- ness.</p>
        <p>Yet when I leave behind the smoky towns. Those hellish death-traps made by "perfect" man, And go into the country-side, where God Has been the craftsman, then I can</p>
        <p>Enjoy the peace and calm of solitude Which satisfies the craving of my soul, For there I meet the spirit, pure and good, Of beauty, Nature—there I reach my goal. BESSIE FINN.</p>
        <p>From Everywhere.</p>
        <p>Ire's a poor dentist who is unable to get to the root of the matter.</p>
        <p>There are too many men in this world who axe not content with wasting their own time.</p>
        <p>As a rule the young person who is conspicu- ously lavish with his dough will be found on investigation to be half baked.</p>
        <p>A New Craze. "What a tragic face you have, Miss Pootle." "Yes, you see, I adore misery !"—" Punch."</p>
        <p>How He Made It. "I understand that old Billyonair made his fortune out of a simple invention." ,‘ No; out of a simple inventor."</p>
        <p>No Longer Dictating. Browning: Are you still dictating to that pretty stenographer you had last summer? Greening: Not so you can notice it. We are married now.</p>
        <p>A Century Later. " I wonder if everybody will have what he wants 100 years from now?" "I doubt it. The men will then probably be agitating for the suffrage." American.</p>
        <p>A furniture store in Coffeyville, Kan., runs a standing advertisement: " Marry the girl. We'll furnish the home." Another store runs this one: "Get a divorce. We'll buy your furni- ture !"</p>
        <p>Justice. "My wife and I always settle our little dis- putes by arbitration." "And who is the arbitrator?" "My wife, of course."</p>
        <p>Fascination.</p>
        <p>There is an indescribable fascination about work. The laziest man in town will stand watching with evident enjoyment the labours of a street gang laying pavement. Consoling.</p>
        <p>Bather in water, 10 native on shore) : "Are you quite sure there are no crocodiles about here?"</p>
        <p>Native : " Yes, sal; de sharks do scare 'em all away, sah."—" Tatler."</p>
        <p>The Last Recruit.</p>
        <p>Sunday School Teacher: "And what do you know about Moses?"</p>
        <p>Small Boy: "Please, teacher, it's my first Sunday here and I don't know anybody."— " Punch."</p>
        <p>Yesterday's.</p>
        <p>Teacher: Why don't you wash your face before you come to school? Anyone could see you had egg for breakfast this morning." Tommy: "Please no, mum; the egg was yesterday morning, mum."—" Taller." The Professor to the Rescue.</p>
        <p>" Oh," sighed the aspiring girl, " how I wish I could feel I had a place in the world that no one else could fill !"</p>
        <p>" Urn," remarked the professor, "I believe my wife wants a cook."</p>
        <p>Information. "What is 'Boston' coffee?" asked the cus- tomer at the lunch counter. "It's the kind you put the cream in first," answered the waiter girl. "But why is it called Boston coffee?" "Because the cream is put in first."</p>
        <p>The Very Thing. Park: I don't know what I am ever going to do with that boy of mine. Ile is careless and absolutely reckless of consequences, and doesn't seem to care for anyone. Lane: Good! You can make a chauffeur out of him. Looking after the Horses.</p>
        <p>_ A cavalry sergeant had endured the stupidity of a recruit for many days. One day the " rookie " was thus greeted when he had vio- lated the sergeant's orders:</p>
        <p>"Don't ever come at the horses from behind without speakin' to them !" exclaimed the ser- geant. "They'll be kicking that thick head of yours Then the first thing you know there'll be a lot of lame horses in the squadron."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>A Bad Sign for Mark.</p>
        <p>"Speaking of Easter eggs," said Mark Twain at a dinner at the Authors' Club in New York, "I am reminded of the town of Squash. "In my early lecturing days I went to Squash to lecture in Temperance Hall, arriving in the afternoon. The town seemed very poorly billed. I thought I'd find out if the people knew any- thing at all about what was in store for them. So I turned in at the general store. Good afternoon, friend,' I said to the general storekeeper. 'Any entertainment here to-night to help a stranger while away the even- ing?'</p>
        <p>"The general storekeeper, who was sorting mackerel, straightened up, wiped his briny hands on his apron and said: "'I expect there's going to be a lecture. I been sellin' eggs all day.' "</p>
        <p>The Woman Not to Marry. The woman who proudly declares that she can- not hem a pocket handkerchief, never made up a bed in her life, and adds with a simper that she has "been in society ever since she was 15." The woman who would rather nurse a pug dog than a baby. The woman who thinks that men are angels. The woman who would rather die than wear a hat two seasons old.</p>
        <p>The woman who thinks that the cook and nurse can keep house. The woman who expects a declaration of love three times a day. The woman who buys ornaments for the draw- ing-robm and borrows kitchen utensils from her neighbours; and who thinks table decorations are of more importance than good food.</p>
        <p>The woman who wants things just because "other women" have them.</p>
        <p>MEMORIES. There rises before me a vision of a children's meeting at which, not long ago, I was invited to speak. The little people are singing, with an enthusiasm to which I have witnessed no equal, the lines of that most beautiful hymn, "There is a land of pure delight." Standing where I am, I have a full and unobstructed view of every face. Truly, to one deeply inter- ested in the study of human nature, it is a sight wondrously inspiring. No clouds of care or trouble cast their shadows on those brows. Perhaps, alas! being human, they do have cares and troubles enough, but, for the present at any rate, they are all forgotten in the extreme de- light with which each one now is filled. As they sing, a light as of heaven plays about their eyes, making them bright and happy. When the singing is finished, we shall all kneel in prayer; then I am to give a simple, short ad- dress; and there will follow another hymn. After that, the meeting will be over; and they, will all disperse, each one to his or her own home. Is that the end? No! in after days they will look back upon this night; and the memory of it will be to them—as such memories have been to countless thousands before—a, light upon their pathway, leading them upward and onward, and at last, we trust, to heaven. So much lies it in the power of memory to influ- ence the current of our life. Oh, if you want to be happy—happy, that is, in the highest and truest sense of the word—look well to the mak- ing of your "Memories." See that in all your life's history, no page shall be sullied with any record which can give you pain and sorrow, for, once written, those recol,ds can never be erased or altered.</p>
        <p>WALTER CARTER, Lataford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>-"TOWN HOUSING.</head>
        <p>-"TOWN HOUSING.</p>
        <p>Will a Wealthg Country Bravelg Face a Great Evil?</p>
        <p>THE LAND REPORT.</p>
        <p>Some Suggested Methods to House the People.</p>
        <p>BY C. E, PARKER.</p>
        <p>The second report of the Land Inquiry Com- mittee has just been published. It relates to the urban part of .the problem, and is pro- foundly interesting. It is an impartial docu- ment, is carefully compiled, and contains much valuable information. Its principal interest centres on the Housing of the Working Classes. The first two hundred pages contain an accuratc. and detailed statement of the housing condi-, tions in our towns, and reveals a general short- age all over the country, of course more accen- tuated in some places than others. It would appear that the difficulty is bectarlng greater as time goes on. One-half or more of the towns in England and Wales are short of houses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>REASONS FOR HOUSE SHORTAGE.</head>
        <p>REASONS FOR HOUSE SHORTAGE.</p>
        <p>Dealing with the reasons of this shortage, as far as the last few years are concerned, the fol- lowing causes are mentioned :</p>
        <p>First. The Finance Act of 1910, and the Lumsden Judgment arising out of it, in which the builder may be called to pay Increini:nt Duty of one-fifth of the increment received by him beyond 10 per cent. The Gliwernment strove to put this right in the Revenue Bill of 1913, but, owing to difficulties connected with other sections, the Bill was not carried.</p>
        <p>Second. Dear money. Builders having to pay about one per cent, more interest on borrowed money than a few years ago. '; lie vital impor- tance of this factor will be realised if we remember that on a house costing ..e250 one per cent. aiddition to the rate of interest means adding is. a week to the rent.</p>
        <p>Third. The Increased Cost of Building Materials and Wages. Building materials for a £2OO cottage have advanced .212 to £l5. Wages have also increased. Fourth. Rise in Rates. This is shown to be on the average in the ten years 1991-02 to 1910-11, from ss. 81d. to Gs. 81., equal to 17 per cent, in urban districts. This rise varies much in different places. Fifth. The Inability to secure Higher Rents. The rise in the cost of providing houses has coincided with a general rise in prices, which has proceeded more rapidly than the rise in workmen's wages. Sixth. The Attractiveness of Alternative In- vestments. The great demands for capital has made gilt-edged securities much cheaper, and they can now be bought at very tempting prices.</p>
        <p>A .section is devoted to "Who is to Provide the Dwellings in Future?" They may be provided: '</p>
        <p>First—By Private enterprise. Second—Co-ofierative enterprise. -4'liird—Municipal enterprise.</p>
        <p>Hitherto 99 per cent, of the working-class houses of the country have been provided by private enterprise.</p>
        <p>_ Public Utility Housing Societies are such societies as the Hampstead Tenants, Limited,</p>
        <p>and come under Co-operative enterprise. These societies limit their dividend to five per cent., and to all such the Public Works Loan Commis- sioners lend up to two-thirds of the value of the houses and land, at 3'2 per cent., plus sink- ing fund for loans repayable in thirty years, and 31 per cent., plus sinking fund, repayable in forty years. These societies, if looked at as units, have grown rapidly, but, taking the housing question as a whole, they are a drop in the bucket. The Commissioners recommend that , the State should lend a larger proportion than ' two-thirds, say up to 8.5 per cent, of the value of , land and buildings, provided the payment does not exceed forty years. Possibly a slightly higher rate of interest might be demanded than •in the case of the present two-thirds limit. It might be objected that this would involve some risk to the State, but the risk would be small, and if the housing difficulty can in part bed solved by a further encouragement of collective ,private enterprise of this character the risk I would be worth while running. The societies are brought under Government stiverVision, and hence there is little chance of disaSter. Fur- ther, as the liability lessens every year through the sinking fund, the risk would only be in the early years. And be it remembered that in the case of rural housing it is proposed that the State should x:un the whole risk of any possible loss. The persons who supply the margin are acting in the public 'interest, and not for per- sonal gain, since they cannot get more than their five per cent., and they run the risk of losing all they put in before the State runs any risk.</p>
        <p>—Local authorities have only built one quarter per cent, of existing houses. Such, as a whole, have never regarded building of houses as within their sphere, and if an adequate supply can be assured, it would probably be better for the public authorities to leave building alone. On the other hand, if Private and Co-operative enterprise fails to supply the need the obliga- tion should he shouldered by the Local Author- ities. The report wisely does not enter fully into the vexed question of the merits or demerits of municipal trading. Like many other things, a good deal can be said on both sides.</p>
        <p>DIFFICULTY OF PROCURING LAND. The present system of rating is naturally mentioned as a hindrance to house building, the burden on those who build houses ought to be shared by those who hold up land waiting for an increase. The weight ought to be taken off those who do something and put upon those who do nothing. Public acquisition of land by the 'authorities ought to be made easier and cheaper. Our own Town Clerk contributes amongst other matter the following fact in regard to the procuring of land for public uses in Warrington : "The costs of arbitration pro- ceedings are so high that it very often pays to give more than the value for the property."</p>
        <p>• THE HUMAN TOUCH. The human touch is introduced in the reports of some of the investigators. For example, we 'read the opinion of a working woman:— • " Working folks can't bath children every night—with the best will in the world you can't--you're tired to death already with a hard day's work—and you've no proper convenience. You have to hot the water panfull and panfull, and while you're washing one child in the bowl, or the tub, another's away round the corner into all kinds of mischief, where, if you had a bath you could put in three at once, and look after 'em altogether. Them's the things that worries me. I'm not greedy, I don't want riches, but I want the same accommodation what's necessary to health, for the poor as the rich, in proportion."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>OVERCROWDI NG.</head>
        <p>OVERCROWDI NG.</p>
        <p>"Is there much overcrowding?" "Oh, isn't there! There's overcrowding just as much in these respectable streets as in the slums, only it's less heard of. That house oppo- site, it's rather bigger than the rest, there's three bedrooms, and parlour and kitchen and scullery; there are sixteen people in it; there were seventeen, but an uncle and niece have gone and a new baby has come. It's a puzzle how they sleep. There's father and mother, and three grown-up daughters—one a widow—</p>
        <p>Continued in next column.</p>
        <p>that's the one with the baby—and working, and a lodger, and five yo _</p>
        <p>school.- And two cat's and a dog."</p>
        <p>11.,15...-</p>
        <p>THE BREEDING-PLACES OF TUBERCULOSIS. Bermondsey.—" At No. 181, Mrs. Sim</p>
        <p>be found, but she is in such deep trouble we hardly like to knock. For this war early, Simms woke up, and coughed and Uninsured, with no savings or earnings, poor Simms lies, and the five small chi' stand round, looking, wide-eyed and wade . .</p>
        <p>- on their first sight of death. , A starred flickers in the grate. The windows are merely closed, but round the edges of each s is pasted tight a double strip of paper. „ through the old and loosely fitted come otherwise piercing draughts on vii;, nights. These windows have for long .1' sealed with paper, so as to keep out both draughts and summer breezes. The paper fixed by the preceding tenants, who, 14; evicted, were found to number fourteen sou' two small rooms with sealed windows. The s of death were sown thick and fast on the 0 of No. 181."</p>
        <p>ALWAYS WASHING. " Mrs. Perkins has had to turn the</p>
        <p>out of her two rooms, because she, too, Ls, washing. She never has finished her was' all the week through, for so many things dirty before they get dry. Here in these little rooms all the family must live and a 1 sticky and verminous all the year round. ' Perkins has been pale since she married. the children sniffle, and one of the elder girl!</p>
        <p>got into the habit of coughing instead of eat Three of the family, at least, have been teg visitors at the dispensary; two others' die' phthisis years ago. The small "Erbert tuberculous in every limb. 'My legs hurt; I about the first words he learned as a k.id. suffered daily, and lived till he was nearl/ But one or two of those that remain strangely, bonny children."</p>
        <p>LOCAL AUTHORITIES. Credit is given to many local authoritiel the effort they have made under considel difficulties to improve housing conditions. Commissioners consider the ratepayers. I whole, are not alive to the subject, or thei. terest is neutralised by the fear of the effe4 the rates. Trades and Labour Council</p>
        <p>various towns have taken a -strong and line in regard to housing. In some coun there are a considerable number of liroll owners, whose influence is detrimental. investigator says of one place that out of Oil. two councillors and aldermen twenty-15°_,s landowners or agents for landlords. ()wins the existence of these strong influences council a large number of cases of houses lc! should be condemned are not dealt with. case, we think, must be very exceptional. , The chief reason why the demolition of 6' Property does not prdceed more rapidly is difficulty, everywhere felt, that there ifs alternative accommodation for people disPhe hence to pull down an overcrowded shun Inf increased overcrowding in existing houses. a great deal can be done until the systfle rating is altered, and the municipalities ceive more adequate grants to defray the creased costs of sanitary improvements, proved education, and the like. It is lOW in future that local authorities should be greater powers and ampler means. The reel sibility should be placed upon them for s' that there is adequate and sanitary housing, cotumodation. They should prepare scheniest, control future developments. In cases of 5,9 age of suitable sites available at reasin' prices, the authorities should promote schemes, and the land values so created silos' e secured by the authority providing the enilot The central authority are to keep ce rts through officers appointed to different Pn the country, and the grants to local authero are to depend on their performance of tile 6 devolved upon them.</p>
        <p>WAGES. The Goverrirnent are to deal with</p>
        <p>problem of wages.</p>
        <p>The committee si</p>
        <p>case as follows :</p>
        <p>"The problem resembles in its esse,ll features that which confronted us in rural' tricts, where the difficulty of providing 11°1 arose principally from the poverty of the r lotion. The Government has definitely elec,l, to deal with the rural problem, not by le% cottages below an economic rent, but by r3i; the economic status of the workers, and " enabling them to pay for a sanitary dwelling of their wages. No doubt, even after this been done, there will be a residum of 10 who, for various reasons, are unable to Pei economic rent for a healthy cottage withont,o from the local authority. But these Wip exceptional, and can be dealt with by the authorities without State aid. If the Government, for adequate re° ..! has come to the conclusion that, in the CO agriculture, employers shall be obliged to their workers wages which will allow 01.1,</p>
        <p>pay an economic rent, it seems-rnev. itabl arrive at a similar decision in dealing urban employers. The arguments against 3 sidy for rural housing apply with equal fc'fc urban housing; and if they are valid in tig</p>
        <p>- - ease they cannot logically be set aside 111, other. If, therefore, the Government denil reject the first alternative in the towns, 10: have done in the country, they must fall on the second; otherwise it will remain /03. sible for local authorities to carry out the ( of seeing that none of the members of thef munity are living in houses which are on' human habitation."</p>
        <p>This report shows that the housing pr° is vast and difficult, and will require skill, all the human love of the nation, to be wisely solved. The country is wei enough. Has it the moral enthusiasm to VI face the task?</p>
