      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Primitive Methodist Anniversary.</head>
        <p>Primitive Methodist Anniversary.</p>
        <p>The Sunday School anniversary at Legh-areet Primitive Methodist Church will. be held to-mor- row. The Rev. R. Hall, Bolton, will preach, and in the afternoon an exercise service, "Les- sons from names," will be delivered by the scholars.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>Ambulance "Blue Ribbon."</head>
        <p>The Ambulance "Blue Ribbon."</p>
        <p>Warrington ambulance men have scored an- other triumph, and they will represent the INo. 4 District in the Dewar Shield and Eccles Cup Competition, the "blue ribbon" honours of the ambulance world. On Saturday they took part in the preliminary contest held at Wigan, and after a stiff competition Warring. ton came out on top with 143 points; Brierfield was second with 124 points, and Nelson and .1-lorwich tied for third position with 118 points. Warrington will thus represent the division in London, and all ambulance men hope to see them go far in the competition.</p>
        <p>Employment for the Blind. Through the energies of a number of persons interested in the blind, and who happen to be members of the local branch of the National League of the Blind, a workshop has been erected, and on Monday morning eight or nine blind men will commence there at wood chop. ping and firelighter making. The workshop—a temporary structure—is in Orchard-street, but it is intended that before long an extension will be made, so that extra men will be required, and later that females may be found work. Mr. 11. 11. Mellor, who is also a blind man, is superintending the workshop. The secretary, Mr. T. 11. Shaw, Sunnyside, Chester New-road, will be pleased to receive any help. Preachers for To-morrow. Legh-street Primitive Methodist (Sunday School Anniversary)—Morning and evening, Rev. It. Hall, Bolton. Hill Chile Baptist—Morning and evening, W. Reid. Bewsey-road Wesleyan—Morning, Air. W. B. Mellor; evening, Rev. W. A. Beckett. Bold-street Wesleyan—Morning, Rev. w. A. Beckett; evening, Rev. H. Middleweed. Christadelphians, Bold-street,--Evening, Mr. J. Battersby, Droylsden. Golborne-street Baptist—Morning and evening, Rev. H. Ogden. Latchford Baptist—Morning and evening, Mr. James Greenwood, Manchester Baptist Col- lege. Cairo-street Chapel—Morning and evening, Rev. Stanley A. Mellor. Wycliffe Congregational—Morning and even- ing, Rev. T. T. James. St. John's Presbyterian--Morning and even- ing, Rev. G. Curtis, Hill Chile.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>BLACK SMOKE:</head>
        <p>BLACK SMOKE:</p>
        <p>How Manchester Proposes to Deal With it.</p>
        <p>'An important move towards the solution of the black smoke problem in industrial Lanca- Shire may shortly be made. It is suggested by the Manchester and District Smoke Abatement League that the manufacturers combine to abate the nuisance, while at the same time increasing the efficiency of their plants, and already 50 firms have announced their intention of being represented at a private meeting to discuss the matter. It is hoped that as a result of this meeting a manufacturers' committee will be ap- pointed to make practical proposals. In a pamphlet which has been circulated among manufacturers in the Manchester dis- trict the successful work of a league of manu- facturers similar to that proposed—the Ham- burg Smoke Abatement League—is set out in detail.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>Work of the League.</head>
        <p>Work of the League.</p>
        <p>_ Among the activities of this league are the following :- 1. Testing of steam-raising plant and reporting as to its efficiency, and as to improvements which should be made. For instance, leak- ages in flues, a common source of waste which it is rather difficult to detect, are pointed out and advice is also given as to the desirability of alteration in the boiler plant, the use of automatic stokers, intro- duction of super-heat or forced draught, &amp;c. 2. Special instruction is given to stokers, as it is found that improper methods of firing are largely responsible both for loss of efficiency and production of smoke. It is found that any attempt at smoke abatement by un- trained stokers without scientific supervision generally ends in failure. It is frequently found that after the stokers have been trained the average efficiency of the plant will be increased five per cent. or more,</p>
        <p>which amounts to a large annual saving in</p>
        <p>the coal bill. 3. Tests are made of any important apparatus that is likely to be beneficial to the mem- bers, either in increasing efficiency or in making the chimneys smokeless. Authorita- tive advice can then be given as regards the probable results obtainable by the use of such apparatus in any given case, 4. Special tests are carried out on the different qualities of coal and other fuels available. Tables are drawn up showing the heat value of the coal, and advice is given to the mem- bers as to the most economical quality of coal for them to buy to suit their particular boiler plant. The Manufacturer's Plaint. ,</p>
        <p>The Manchester and District Smoke Abate- ment League point out that over and over again manufacturers have stated that they have spent large sums of money in endeavouring to make their plant smokeless, with the unfortunate re- sult that no improvement has been effected either as regards smokelessness or economy. Absolutely reliable advice on such matters is extremely difficult to obtain, and it I urged that the best way to prevent such futin waste of money is by the formation of a league, some- 'what on the lines suggested.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>LYMM VESTRY METING., RESIGNATION OF AN OLD</head>
        <p>LYMM VESTRY METING., RESIGNATION OF AN OLD</p>
        <p>CHURCHWARDEN.</p>
        <p>At Lymm vestry meeting last night the Rector (the Rev. G. Thurston) presiding, Mr. A. W. Callender presented the church 'accounts, which showed that the year com- menced with a debit balance of .t 6 ss. 6d. and finished with a credit balance of ~t 3 Os. Bd. The Rector alluded to the retirement of Mr. Callender, who had been a warden on and off since 1882. During that period he had been people's warden for 13 years and Rector's war- den for ten years, and had been continuously in office since 1899. The Rector appointed Mr. Fred Evans as his warden. ' Mr. A. Watkin proposed Mr. W. M. Beckett as people's warden, and suggested that a pre- sentation should be made to Mr. Callender. Mr. Callender seconded Mr. Beek-ett's appoint- ment, which was agreed to unanimously. The following sidesmen were elected: Messrs. J. T. Milnes, Pearson, W. Hartley, A. R. Heath- cote, P. F. Dixon, J. G. Farrar, A. R. Oliver I and W. T. Lecomber.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 18, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 18, 1914.</p>
        <p>FLETCHEWS STRIKE.</p>
        <p>Why Cannot the Contending Parties</p>
        <p>Come to Peace?</p>
        <p>THE MAYOR'S SUGGESTION.</p>
        <p>Cannot a settlement be reached in the strike ,at Messrs. Fletcher, Russell's? It seems that only a little commonsense is required to end the unfortunate trouble.</p>
        <p>Interviewed about the matter yesterday after- noon by "The Examiner" representative, the Mayor said he was dceidedly of opinion that if the firm would meet a representative official from each section and commonsense was exer- cised, a proper understanding would be arrived at.</p>
        <p>If confidence was restored on both side, he continued, there should be no difficulty in the future. It seems to me that there is a feeling on the part of the men, which appears quite reasonable from precedents in other cases, that advantages might ultimately be taken if the whole of them were not reinstated. This could be obviated if the firm would agree to say that within so many weeks they. would take every man back to his work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>MORE MANIFESTOES.</head>
        <p>MORE MANIFESTOES.</p>
        <p>On Thursday the firm issued a public notice, to which the men replied. The firm stated that:</p>
        <p>As there appears to be some misunderstand- ing with regard to the statements made at the interview which the Mayor and Mr. Hutchi- son had with our Mr. Russell, we beg to</p>
        <p>say: It was pointed out that if all the men were reinstated en bloc a large number of them would be standing idle about the works, because work cannot be found for them all, and accidents might occur: It was also stated that over 100 would be started on the first day of the opening of the works, but Mr. Russell could not wish to bind himself to the exact number, his idea being to take on as many men as we could find work for, and that each day afterwards additional men would be started until all the Men were employed. (Signed) W. 11. Budge, secre- tary.</p>
        <p>THE MEN'S REPLY. The men's leaders, in reply, stated: The public notice issued by the firm conveying the impression that there exists a misunderstanding should now make pos- sible the arrangement of a conference, at which the misunderstanding might be re- moved. We wish to point out that the detail arrangements could be better settled at a conference than by any form of public notice. If the firm are prepared to offer assurances that all men can return to work, such assurances should be given to repre- sentatives of the men after mutual agree- ment. Unless representatives of both sides meet no arrangements can be agreed upon. May we again suggest that the refusal of the firm to meet the union officials creates a lack of confidence by our men in the firm? We are hopeful that the firm will see the reasonableness of our suggestion, and agree to a conference being convened for an early date. This is signed by Mr. C. Dukes and Mr. A. Bennett.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>MASS MEETING.</head>
        <p>THE MASS MEETING.</p>
        <p>At a mass meeting of the men, Mr. Dukes said that at meetings which had been held in their rooms, they had intimated to the men on strike exactly what the desire of the firm would be, and also they had outlined the ways and means which were likely to be adopted by the firm to achieve their object, and they had been correct. Well, said MI% Dukes, if nothing else has con- vinced the firm of the futility of attempting to break down the solid•front which has been held by the workers during the past five or six weeks, this demonstration of solidarity and refusal to return to work piecemeal or in sections should convey to them that they are incapable of bring- ing about a settlement unless they are prepared to give a, guarantee acceptable to us. If the firm assumed that the men had reached the end of their tether by simply with- drawing their labour they were living in a fool's paradise. Whether the company knew it or not, it rested with the men to make an appeal that was likely to close the firm indefinitely unless they speedily agreed to a reasonable settlement. Letters could be shown wherein men of influ- ence had offered to use their influence if re- quested in certain directions. The day had gone by when employers could dictate as to how, when and who the men should choose as their spokesmen. (Hear, hear.) The temperament of the firm could be very well weighed up by their refusal to meet the speaker. (Hear, hear.) So far as he was personally concerned, he could afford to ignore the firm, but that was not the point. lie had been chosen as one of the offi- cial spokesmen of the workers, and therefore the firm should receive him. (Hear, hear, and ap- plause.) The firm seemed to assume that men who had been intelligent enough to recognise the forces of organisation were going to discard the weapon which had served them so well. If the men had not been organised, and had been a disunited rabble, they could have understood the firm's suggestion. The 109 have not landed, said Mr. Dukes, amid loud laughter, and it is not likely they will get any back until they make it 1,009. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>"Tub-Thumpers."</p>
        <p>A letter had appeared in a local paper in which the leaders of the strike were called "tub- thumpers.f' He would like to say that no "tub- thumper" could bring out on strike a thousand men if the men did not want to strike. (Hear, hear.) There would always be "tub-thumpers" whilst firms took up attitudes similar to that of Messrs. Fletcher, Russell's. (Hear, hear.) The letter also said that it was the women and children who suffered. They knew that was the case, but 50 per cent, of the married men in the town were working for 24 shillings .a week. The Press did not say anything about the women and children who were suffering there.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. Hutchison, general secretary of the Stove, Grate, Fender and Light Metal Workers' Union, said that they had been ever ready to take advantage of the Mayor's offer, and had kept the position half open so that the firm could 'climb down with as little loss of dignity as possible. Comrade Dukes had referred to the letter which had appeared in the Press, which said that it was the women who paid the sacri- fice. They all realised that was the case in international and industrial war, and the firm knew it also, for if it had not been for the women and children they would not have held out for six weeks. The union had not yet touched the fringe of its funds; probably if the strike was not settled they would have funds when the firm of Messrs. Fletcher, Russell's was non-existent. (Laughter.) Mr. J. Carroll, president of the Stove, Grate Workers' Union, said it was an insult to their intelligence to say, the firm could be run with, 109 men. They had been treated with civility by the police on duty that morning, and he hoped there would be no hitch between the police and the strikers. A show of hands showed a unanimous decision not to return to work until all would be accepted.</p>
        <p>PREMIER Dimon CYCLES. Light-weight model, £36.—Snle agent. J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge. street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>IRISH PICNIC.</head>
        <p>THE IRISH PICNIC.</p>
        <p>How the " Liberal " Deputation Spent</p>
        <p>Their Time in Belfast.</p>
        <p>ULSTER LIBERALS' WAIT.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner." Sir,—With reference to Mr. Bellian's letter in your issue of the 11th inst., in which he states that the Belfast Liberal Association (the proper title is the Ulster Liberal Association) "altered the appointment to a later period in the evening without asking ourselves," I am afraid that someone has misled him on this matter, as the place and hour were fixed by Mr. Hayes, of War- rington, and at the appointed time we attended, but were kept waiting for at least 30 minutes before the deputation gathered to meet us. No alteration was made in the original ap- pointment. Mr. Hayes will know whether this is correct or not when I inform him that we at- tended at the Prince of Wales' Hotel at 6 p.m. There are other statements in Mr. Bellian's letter to which I might take exception, such as " the time taken by the Belfast Liberals was all they required, and quite as long as any inter- view accorded to any Unionists." I understand the deputation arrived here on Friday morning and left on Saturday night. Out of this they spent an hour or an hour and a half with Home Rulers. Might I ask with whom and where did they spend the remainder of their time?</p>
        <p>THOMAS 31cDOWELL. Ulster Liberal Association, 29, Rosemary-street, Belfast.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>CORK STATUE OUTRAGE.</head>
        <p>THE CORK STATUE OUTRAGE.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>thank you for the prominence given to the quotation from " Irish Freedom," which I sent last week, but regret that I cannot cona- plimept you upon either the tone or the style of your comments thereon. The red ink, which so much upset you, served saving the letter from the waste _ _ papert its purpose basket,ba yv have where some of my previous con- tributions</p>
        <p>Now, sir, you question the existence of such a paper as "Irish Freedom," but if you had read the London daily papers regularly during the past month or two you would have seen fre- quent references to the paper mentioned, but, like myself, you would have looked in vain for any repudiation of. the same from any member of the Nationalist party. I will, however, go further than this, for if you will name a con- venient time and place, I am prepared to pro- duce for your inspection files of the past two years' issues of the paPer in question. Surely that will satisfy even such an admittedly suspi- cious-minaed person as yourself. As to its Nationalist character, which you also sneeringly question, perhaps the following quotation from a declaration of its principles will be sufficiently convincing : " We stand for the complete and total separa- tion of Ireland from England, and the estab- lishment of an Irish Government, untrammelled and uncontrolled by any other Government in the world." You say that you are sure that there will be no bloodshed. Perhaps ere this you have heard of the blood of the old men and the maidens whose blood has been shed during the past week in the North of Ireland? Perhaps, sir, you will now have the good man- ners to withdraw your unwarranted aspersions upon the veracity of at least ONE OF THE APOSTLES. Warrington, April 16th, 1914.</p>
        <p>]Our correspondent will no doubt be surprised, but we have not found his letter "convincing" on any one point he names. The only con- vincing thing about it is the proof it gives to the assertion of the "Ulster Guardian" that the members of the deputations from War- rington were too much engaged raking in the muck-heap to raise their eyes to see if there was at least one atom of good in an Irish Nationalist. —THE Enrron.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>SANKEY COUNCIL CHAIRMAN.</head>
        <p>SANKEY COUNCIL CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>MR. W. FURNESS RE-ELECTED</p>
        <p>LAST NIGHT.</p>
        <p>' mineeting of the Sankey the Schools. Mr. ParishLa s t Council ewaa an annual meeting W. Furness pnesided. Mr. Furness was re-elected chairman and Mr. Pownall was elected rice-chairman. Messrs. W. Ireland and R. W. Wright were re-elected overseers, and the Vicar was re-elected the representative on the Education Sub-com- mittee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>BUTTERMILK BRIDGE PATHWAY.</head>
        <p>BUTTERMILK BRIDGE PATHWAY.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported on his interview with Mr. Selby in regard to the footpath leading to But- termilk Bridge. Mr. Selby agreed that the path wanted repairing, and he agreed to send .P.. 5 if Crosfield's sent a similar amount. He gave his sanction to• the• opening of the roadway. The Clerk also stated that he had not yet had a reply from Mr. Wrench for Crosfield's Recrea- tion Club, and Mr. Fair said he had heard‘the deputation had had an interview with Mr. Selby, but he did not know what had transpired. It was' ultimately decided that the matter of the temporary repairs be left with the Clerk, and also that the District Council be asked if they were willing to carry out the arrange- ments with Mr. Selby, who had suggested a certain concession in regard to the pathway, and who had also requested that the matter be dealt with in a legal and formal manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>ANOTHER ROAD.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER ROAD.</p>
        <p>from the Surveyor hadThewCrilteterkn rienporrteegdartdhattothlCorucednty borough boundary to the Penketh Lane End to the effect that the order had been placed for the tar-macadam required for the re-sheeting of the road, and the work would be carried out im- mediately the material arrived.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>13FilDGE DELAY.</head>
        <p>13FilDGE DELAY.</p>
        <p>The • Clerk also reported that Mr. Compton Hall, the county bridge-master, had written to the effect that another delay had arisen with the Postal Telegraph Department with regard to the underground lines running beneath the bridge. The information had been sent to the Railway Company, and he waa. hoping to have the tenders for the whole work to submit to the Main 'Roads and Bridges Committee Du the 13th of May. Mr. Ireland: They have been moving in this for years, and I consider it is very snail-like. The Chairman said they had the intimation that the authorities were moving in the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>LIST OF HARDY ANNUALS.</head>
        <p>A LIST OF HARDY ANNUALS.</p>
        <p>The following are worth growing: Bartonia aurea, calliopsis, candytuft, chrysanthemums, clarkia, collinsia, eschscholtzia, godetia, gyp- sophila, larkspur, limnanthes, lavatera, linum, nasturtiums, nemophila, phacelia, shirley pop- pies, saponaria, silene, viscaria, Virginia stocks. For perfume: Sweet peas, mignonette, am- brosia, sweet Sultan, sweet scabious, night- scented stock, schizopetalon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>SAMPLING PROTECTION. A System Under Which the " Spoils " Go to the Victor.</head>
        <p>SAMPLING PROTECTION. A System Under Which the " Spoils " Go to the Victor.</p>
        <p>HUMAN GAHHISH HEAP.</p>
        <p>To the 'Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—The winter season of San'''''''''' is not a very terrible affair. After some seven or eight months of dry and sunny weather, we wel- come very heartily, and with noisy joy, the first appearance of what we call the "rainy season." In our city by the sea we hold a somewhat peculiar position, if not a unique one. We are placed on the extreme Western Coast of the North American Continent. Between us and the Middle States, and, again, between us and the Eastern States, lie vast tracts of prairie land. Huge ranges of mountains also enclose us in their prison-like embraces. When a penniless person leaves the train or ship at Oakland he instinctively feels that his first business must be to put money in his purse Until that is done he will feel that, though he is near a whole cityfull, home he has none. The days of '49 are gone. It is only on rare occa- sions that gold is found on the midden heap. I have in mind the letters recently given to us and written by Warrington boys who are now threading their devious ways in Canada. It was well these letters were placed before the readers of "The Examiner." An opportunity is now afforded for the giving of advice; and who so likely to give, at least honest advice, as those who have nothing to gain and have a friendly feeling for those who hail from the same dear old town! I have a son and a son-in-law in Canada. They are both doing well, and are in a fair way to become "well-to-do." I had an- other relative—by marriage—here at my home a little while ago. He was honeymooning. lie had a great story to tell me about his prosperity. He now owns large tracts of land, employs a dozen men, and has numerous heads of cattle. Townsmen are not wanted in Canada, nor yet in the U.S. Men with capital are wanted, and they should be men who are used to and are acquainted with the farm and love the land. In</p>
        <p>, , 0 /</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>'R., HENRY WHITHAM,</head>
        <p>'R., HENRY WHITHAM,</p>
        <p>A. frequent contributor to "The Examiner," an old Warringtonian, now in San Francisco. Aged 74; photograph taken at the age of 7L</p>
        <p>snort, the men who are wanted are such men as are sometimes driven off the land—the cotch crofter, of whom I wrote in "The Warrington Examiner" near upon forty years ago. I am making arrangements for a Warrington-born boy to take up this subject.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>Business at Lowest Ebb.</head>
        <p>Business at Lowest Ebb.</p>
        <p>In San Francisco to-day business is at the lowest ebb it ever knew in its history. It matters not what the point of view may be. As employers, they are seeking cheap labour. Why? Well, they scarcely know why. Protection is supposed to keep the products of foreign cheap labour out of the U.S. It does not, by any means, accomplish this. The results of this so- called " foreign cheap labour" come here in the shape of the best quality of goods sold in our stores. Rich people only buy them. Among the rich people I include the working man, whose powerful labour organisation enables him to demand and to receive IBs. and 20s. for eight hours' work. For ways that are dark, and for tricks that are vain, commend )ne to the Ameri- can statesman. __</p>
        <p>Of course it follows quite naturally that the members of our labour organisations look at things in much the same way that their em- ployers do. "To the victor belong the spoils." Once uppn a time the canny Scot—Carnegie— being called before the Court to give evidence touching some trade and labour question, con. fessed that the immense profits that the Trust of which he was head was receiving were simply "spoils," and the Press reported him as saying "The jig up, boys!" An honest business is man would scarcely refer to his legitimate pro- fits as "spoils." Should he do so, it would be an acknowledgment that he knew he was en- gaged in a bad business, and would have no kicks coming should those around him follow his example. The jig is not yet up; no, it is I going on a little while longer, but others are likely to pay the piper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>Right to Live.</head>
        <p>The Right to Live.</p>
        <p>Concerning an "incipient revolution" in California, Mr. Whitham says of the speakers: They were quite sure, quite in earnest, about theik conclusions, "Something must be done." Befike the "high-browed" statesman or the "high-brewed" clergyman has found time to seek hi a study and make an attempt at thinking the man in the market-place and the cry of the children have determined what that something must be. Vocalised, it sounds something like this—"A home to live in; bread to eat, and clothes to wear." Quite a long time ago I was one at a meeting held at the lien and Chickens, Oldham-street, Manchester. The meeting was held for the purpose of putting into shape the idea that something should be done to commemorate the birthday of Ernest Jones. This dear, old self- sacrificing lad was born January 25th, 1819. To enliven the proceedings, songs written by Ernest Jones were interspersed in the intervals of speech-making, of which the following is one:—</p>
        <p>A vote in the laws they make; • A home on the land I till; Where the hearts of the many break The cup of the few to fill. By the right of their laws I pine; But what are their laws to me? For I live by right divine, And that is the right to be free. A home in my native isle! A share in the wealth I heap! Where the rich in their revel smile, And the poor in their anger weep. The poor—the poor—the poor in their anger weep, The rich—the rich—the rich their revels keep.</p>
        <p>At the word of the cruel few The clouds of the battle frown; But, long as the many are true, We'll say let the storm Come down— And on as the masses sweep, Our cry shall meet them still : A share in the wealth we heap, A home on the land we till, A home in my native isle, A vote in the laws we keep, Then the rich, if they like, may smile, But the poor shall cease to weep. The poor—the poor—the poor shall cease to weep. The rich—the rich—the rich their revels keep.</p>
        <p>I may say just here that the meeting was held under the presidency of Dr. Pankhurst, whose speech I have near me.</p>
        <p>The name of Ernest Jones pulls me; I was with him on the last evening he ever appeared in public. He caught cold while passing from a hall somewhere up Oldham-road to the Free Trade Hall. My only boy is named after him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>Poor of San Francisco.</head>
        <p>The Poor of San Francisco.</p>
        <p>The time soon came when the authorities of San Francisco had to sit up and take notice of its own poor, and of the idle poor strangers it had within its gates. Something had to be done. Poverty, hunger and dirt were clamouring at the gates. Well, if the idle poor were not equal' to the occasion, the idle rich were. Why shouldn't they be? As adepts in the fine art of— not murder—but advertising, they decided to give these idle poor, men and women, money, good, solid money. "here," these idle rich virtually said, "here is money, good American dollars; take them, and no questions asked. Get out of sight, and do be good. Especially do we beg of you—do be good." That was a most fetching ad., and it fetched 'em all right. Soon they were flocking into the city. Funds ran out, and the days of '49 were but a dream. Advertise for labour, and soon there was a glut in supply. Take the same course for dollars, and Hie word " shortage " ran up to the top of the flag-pole in double-quick time. Then money in exchange for work was tried on. That did not go; money without work was all right; 'anyone could stand that. After considerable trouble, worry and expense these unemployed got a move on themselves once more, and landed in Sacra- mento, the capital city of the State of Cali- fornia. Another very troublesome element began to develop now. Why should San Fran- cisco dump its surplus products into Oakland? Why should San Francisco, Oakland and Stock- ton dump their—what?—not "unemployed," not idle wastrels, not even "surplus products"? but we put it in this shape, "Why should San Fran- cisco, Oakland, Stockton and Sacramento carry their REFUSE across the Sacramento river and establish a garbish heap in Yolo County? To get.the garbish out•of Sacramento was compara- tively easy. Call out the militia, police, and, happy thought, call out the firemen. I have no idea what force there may be in the nozzle, or in the water that issues from the nozzle of a hose that is connected with a powerful fire engine. We must have law and order, it is said, even if it is administered through the nozzle of a fire hose. That hose was like a huge python. The inanimate yet lively hose rolled the human refuse of just a small portion of the world's surplus labour right out of Sacramento, over the broad rivers and into and among the bleak and barren sand dunes of Yolo County. Now, a number of sheriffs, representing the surround- ing counties, got together, and, as iron shar- peneth iron, so did these sheriffs sharpen the wits of each other. These poor, hounded human beings were peaceful; they wanted to go to Washington. The sheriffs' decision was that they must not go to Washington. They must not go East, West, North nor South, nor yet could be allowed to stay in Yolo County. There are now 150 of these sons of God and brothers of ours waiting hungrily but cheerfully near the ridge of the Sierra mountains, waiting for the officials of the railroad to dispossess them of the freight train they are holding up. No food is being furnished them either by the citizens of Redding or officials.</p>
        <p>HENRY WHITHAII.</p>
        <p>March 20th, 1914.</p>
        <p>TIIE COLONIES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>Letters from Warrington Men in</head>
        <p>Letters from Warrington Men in</p>
        <p>Canada and Australia.</p>
        <p>UNEMPLOYMENT IN VANCOUVER.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ernest Eastham, who was a member of the Liberal Club and lived at 14, Gibson-street, and who went out to Canada about twelve months ago, has written to a friend from Van- couver. Ile says: There is no big improvement in my economic circumstances. In fact, I have every reason to be thankful that I am able to keep myself at all. The population of this city is very similar to Warrington, but I heard a public lecturer say that the official calculation was over 10,000 un- employed. What work I have had has been with the Canadian Pacific Railway, mostly as wharf hand and longshoreman. I have had about seven or eight journeys to Calgary, in Alberta, taking charge of freight; cars, that is, which it is necessary to keep to a certain tem- perature, to protect against the very severe frosts. It is very cold in those parts, I can tell you; it was 29 degrees below zero when I was there. This zero weather is no joke, and one has to be very careful to guard against frost-bite.</p>
        <p>Coming Home Soon as Possible.</p>
        <p>I don't think that I shall.ever get anything in this country worth having. The longer I stay the more disgusted I become. I understand that lots of people are intending coming here next summer from the Old Country. Well, if they come to British Columbia I feel sorry for them. I intend to go East as soon as the weather per- mits; if there is nothing doing there I shall be so much nearer England, at any rate. I shall only stay long enough to make enough money to bring me back. As I cannot see anything to justify me bring- ing out my wife and family, I am anxious to get back to them, as you may be sure. The winter has not been severe on the coast— something like it is in England. I have talked to business men and they hold out no hope or prospects of there. being any improvement in Vancouver during the coming summer. There are lots of shopkeepers and little contractors and traders going to the wall. And there are scores and scores of able-bodied</p>
        <p>Continued in next column.</p>
        <p>' men Suffering the pangs of hunger and distress. This has been called the "Golden West." And I believe in times past money was good here, but I am afraid it has come to an end now. I am looking forward with pleasure to the time when I shall have a game with you, and the sooner the better. Short as the time has been that I have been here, it has seemed half a lifetime. Of course, everything has been against me, but, at any rate, I have certainly benefited in health, I am glad to say.</p>
        <p>DIFFICULTIES IN WESTERN</p>
        <p>AUSTRALIA.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner." Sir,—ln my last letter to you I spoke of the experience of a farm labourer, who had left his home in Devonshire to seek his fortune in this "Great and Golden West." This week I en- close cuttings from the "West Australian" showing the conditions and difficulties under which the farmers in certain districts are strug- gling to make a living. , This is no doubt a country of great possibili- ties, but I think it is only fair and right that intending immigrants should have some hard, solid facts placed before them, besides the allur- ing advertisements that are appearing regularly in the home papers. I remain, yours faithfully, WARRNGTONIAN. Osborne Park, Perth, N.A.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>CUTTINGS.</head>
        <p>THE CUTTINGS.</p>
        <p>-- -; --- - - --• Eastern Settlers' Plight.—A petition, asking that rents should be deferred pending better times, has been forwarded to the Minister for Lands by the Kununoppin branch of the Farmers' and Settlers' Association. The peti- tioners state that the coming wheat yield in the district would not be a payable proposition, and urge that no drastic measures to enforce pay- ments should be taken against those who hare worked their lands legitimately. They proceed to say that, almost to a man, they took up land by reason of the rainfall records furnished some years ago, but they had come to the conclusion that the records were misleading. They be- lieved the rainfall to be precarious, and that, from the experience of old pioneers, the past two seasons were an average of what they might expect. Their money had long since vanished, and the proceeds to be obtained from the pres- ent harvest would only enable them to pay a pro rata distribution to the storekeeper, the merchant, the Agricultural Bank, and the Seed Wheat Board. "You and your party," add the Petitioners, "who insist so strongly on a living wage, will surely be consistent, and give us a chance to make good with the advent of better times and more thorough methods." The Min- ister has replied, intimating that he is calling for a report from the chairman of the Seed Wheat Board, as to the lines on which requests from settlers are to be considered.</p>
        <p>The Dry Areas.—A correspondent at Nun- garin, who signs himself "Too Far East," com- plains of the lack of rain, and proceeds: Of the hundred public servants who were placed on the land not 50 remain to-day, some having sold out as soon as they realised the impossibility of making a living, let alone a competence, and some having deserted before the end of 12 months and returned to the serfdom of the desk. The balance would quit gladly to-day if there was a possibility of anyone giving them a little of their own back. As a paying proposi- tion, wheat-growing in these drought-stricken areas is out of the question, and it is only in years of abnormal rainfall that a profitable crop can be reckoned on. Stock-raising is im- possible on account of the lack of water, and even when the scheme water is available, it will not be possible to buy water and make a profit out of growing mutton. During the past four years we have averaged four and a half months' water carting in each year, and in many in- stances we had then to buy the precious fluid. In another few weeks we shall have to "face the music" again.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>REAL-ESTATE BOOSTERS IN SASKATCHEWAN.</head>
        <p>REAL-ESTATE BOOSTERS IN SASKATCHEWAN.</p>
        <p>A correspondent, writing from Shellbrook, Saskatchewan, says : Things out here have been in a parlous state ever since we came. There seems to be utter stagnation of trade and work in the West, and this winter relief kitchens have been opened in many of the western towns. Prince Albert has shut down all her works and stopped all exten- sions. As a consequence there is no loose money for anything. The real-estate boosters have been hit hard—serve them right.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>N.U. TOUR.</head>
        <p>THE N.U. TOUR.</p>
        <p>FIFTEEN PLAYERS SAIL FOR</p>
        <p>AUSTRALIA.</p>
        <p>OTHERS TO FOLLOW.</p>
        <p>Fifteen of the Northern Union football team who are making the tour in Australia and New Zealand, where they will play eighteen matches against Colonial teams, left London yesterday morning for Tilbury, to join the P.O. steamer Marmora.</p>
        <p>The remainder of the party will travel later, as circumstances permit, but all are expected to reach Sydney before the first match. The players who comprised the first party were:—</p>
        <p>Full-back: A. E. Wood, Oldham. Threequarter backs: F. Williams, Halifax; W. liall, Oldham; W. A. Davies, Leeds; J. E. Robinson, Rochdale Hornets.</p>
        <p>Half-backs: F. Smith, Hunslet; S. Prosser, Halifax; J. O'Garra, Widnes.</p>
        <p>- Forwards: W. Jarman, Leeds; W. Smales and J. H. Guerin, Hunslet; D. Hollands, Oldham; W. Roman, Rochdale Hornets; A. S. Johnsen, Widnes; J. L. Clampitt, Broughton Rangers. Mr. J. H. Houghton, the secretary-manager of the tour, was in charge of the party.</p>
      </div>
