        <p>Ae.urssa</p>
        <p>answered here, or by Post, if seat to , the Enitor of 'Vita Column. Let:ers slv,uld byar the Name staid of se der. _ CL—Since the Apostle clearly explains in 1. Cor. xy. 35-38, that the body will not be resur- rected, what does he mean when he says: "So also is the resurrection of the dead. It is sown in corruption, it is raised in incorruption," &amp;c. (I. Cos. xy. 42-44)? What is meant by the ex- pression " it "? (PUZZLED.) A.—We answer that "it" is the soul that is restored or resuscitated. What was. it that died? The Scripture reply- is: "The sourthat sinneth ' it' shall die." The living, intelligent, senti- ent being is to be raised up by Divine power in the time of resurrection—" when all who are in their graves shall come forth." A gramo- phone furnishes an excellent illustration of the resurrection. The "record" placed in the machine will have the impression formed upon it by sounds produced by the voice or some other means. After the record has received its Cull impression it may be laid away and pre- served for some years while the gramophone is destroyed. By-and-bye the record is taken from its place and put into a new gramophone, where the original sounds are reproduced. Thus it' it with the individual. His character is formed by the impressions received in the daily experi- ences of life. His body corresponds to the machine. In course of time he dies. The body 13 destroyed and, the character or identity is Vreserved by the Lord, we know not how or where. In the resurrection the character (record) will be placed in a new body (machine)</p>
        <p>and the original thoughts, memory, &amp;0., will be reproduced. A fine " record" will be placed in a high-class machine. A poor record (if not destroyed because of worthlessness) will be placed in a lower-grade instrument. (I.—ln what sense was our Lord the "first and the last"? Rev. i. 11. (L.8.C.) A.—Our Lord was the channel through whom the Heavenly Father created all things heavenly and earthly; without him was not any- thing made that is made (John i. 3), but he him- self was created by God. Revelation iii. 14, says he was the beginning of the creation of God Then, after his death, God, having begotten him to the Divine nature at his baptism, raised him to life again, and set him on His own right hand (Eph. i, 19, 20). Thus we Re our Lord Was the first and the last of God's direct creation; all other beings and things were created through the Son. 4—What is meant by 11. Cor. v. 21: ". . . made him to be sin for us, who knew no sin"? (TRUTH SEEKER.)</p>
        <p>A.—This cannot imply that Jesus became a sinner, or was guilty in any sense. The expres- sion "who knew no sin" is sufficient to prove that. See also I. Pet. ii. 22, 24: "Who did no . sin, neither was guile found •in his mouth." "Who his own self bare our sins in his own. body on the tree." Our Lord did not bear our sins in his body, but he could, and did suffer on account of these. "He tasted death for every -man." We must, therefore, understand the terse thus: " He made Him a sin-offering—a sin-</p>
        <p>bearer—for us." "The Lord hath laid -on him</p>
        <p>the iniquity of us all." The --a7OT•cl-t-3in is ti-s-e7l with the meaning of sin-offering in Hosea iv. 8: "They (the priests) eat up the sin (sin-offer- ings) of my people!' See also Heb. ix. 26-28, and aiii. 11.</p>
        <p>Q.—Please explain I. Cor. xv. 17, 18: "If Christ be not raised, . . . then they also which are fallen asleep in Christ are perished." . (ROSE.) •</p>
        <p>A.—The Apostle argues in this chapter that the only hope of a future life for anyone is in resurrection. In verse 13 he says, "If there be no resurrection, then is Christ not risen." Carrying his logic a bit further, in verses 17-18 he shows that the climax of this "no resurrec- tion" theory, which he is combating, would be that death ends all; and even believers in Christ are perished for ever! How absurd all this reasoning would seem'if the general idea,were true that when a believer dies he goes straight to heaven! Sleep is used here as a figure of death, and it is a very appropriate one, too. In a sound sleep there is no consciousness, and in like manner the Scriptures tell us that "the dead know not anything." Q.—How do you harmonise the two statements, " Flesh and blood cannot inherit the kingdom of heaven" (I. Cor. xv. 50), and "All flesh shall see the salvation of God "? (Luke iii. 6.)</p>
        <p>( E L. H.) A.—ln I. Cor. xv. 35-36, the Apostle describes the resurrection of the dead. He tells us that the body which goes into the grave is not the body which is raised. God gives to every seed his own body. Just as one star differeth from another star in glory, there are celestial and terrestrial bodies. Some will be raised with earthly bodies and some with heavenly. Those whose characters have become copies of God's dear Son will be raised like him. They go down o the grave in dishonour, with imperfect human bodies, and are raised in glory. They die as men, and are raised spirit beings like their Lord. Only such can inherit the kingdom of heaven, and reign as kings and priests with the Lord. A human being could not look upon the glory of heavenly being any more than Saul could, when on the way. to Damascus, look on the glorified Lord. Luke iii. 4-6, gives us a picture from Isaiah xl. . of the completed work of Christ. .Then the knowledge of the Lord will fill the whole earth, and all shall know him from the least to the greatest. To see this all mankind will be brought back to life on the earth (see John v. 28, 29). God " will have all men to be saved (from Adamic condemnation), and come unto the knowledge of the truth" (I. Tim. ii. 4)• Then all flesh will see the salvation God hac planned for mankind on the earth (coaf. Rev. xxi. 1-5; Isa. xxv. 6-10; xxxv. 1-10, &amp;c.). Q.—What is "the mark for the prize of our high calling of God in Christ Jesus" (Phil. iii. VI)? (1.H.5.) A.—lt is surely true that a great prize i:. offered to those who are now called according to the Divine purpose during this Gospel Age, and it is equally true that there must be, that there is, a mark of character development, nothing short of which would be acceptable to our Heavenly Father, and bring to us the great reward promised to the overcomers who are to sit with the Lord in his throne of' glory as his bride. The Scriptures explain this mark to us in various terms. We are told that we are to be "more than conquerors" through the. merit and aid of our Lord. Such shall share in the</p>
        <p>first resurrection, and be presented before ldm blameless in love. "Be diligent that ye may be found of him in peace, without spot and blameless" (11. Pet. iii. 14). He exhorts the adding of the graces of the spirit, Saying, If ye do these things, ye shall never fall. For so an abundant entrance shall be ministered unto you into -t,e everlasting kingdom of our Lord and Saviouriresus Christ" (11. Pet. i. 10, 11). St. Paul gives us a most comprehensive statement of what will be re- quired of those who shall be accounted worthy of a share in the glorified, elect church. He assures us that God has predestinated, fixed in advance, unalterably, that only by becoming character copies of His Son can we hope to be sharers with him in his "kingdom, glory, honour, and immortality" (Rom. viii. 29).</p>
        <p>SYNINOTO (Regd. Trade Illcte.'d COFFEE ESSENCE The Cofee Maid, of SymintYlon's, says: "Sing a song of Symin,gton's, the kettle's on the sing, A little drop of Essence, makes Coffee for a king." THOS. SYMINGTON 4g CO.. Edinburgh and London- EVE-</p>
