      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>GULF STREAM MYTH.</head>
        <p>THE GULF STREAM MYTH.</p>
        <p>'lle chief topic of conversation amongst all classes of English people is the weather. It is, looked at from this point of view, so variable, that it is inexhaustible. With a mention of the rain or the sunshine, the humidity of the atmosphere or the force of the wind you may open a converlatian with a perfect .stranger who would otherwise regard you as a poten- tial pickpocket or tout if you attacked him with a remark upon any other subject. And, ten to one, if there has been anything partic- ularly striking in the character of the weather at the time of the conversation, you will hear it set down to the Gulf Stream. A prolonged period of rain, an absence of snow, a heat wave, or an unusual hail or thunder- storm will always be traced, by anyone who wishes to embroider his remarks upon the weather With a display of knowledge dimly remembered from the days of his youth, to the pernicious or helpful Gulf Stream. The truth of the matter is that the Gulf Stream has no more influence on our weather and cli- mate than the moon, though there are as many people who believe the latter as the former.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>UNFOUNDED IN FACT.</head>
        <p>UNFOUNDED IN FACT.</p>
        <p>----- I It is true that in some schools, teachersl whose geographical knowledge has not exact- ly been brought up to date, still continue to instil into the minds of their pupils the idea that the ini:dness of our winter as compa,red with that of Labrador in the same latitude is due to the Gulf Stream. But all the geo- graphers of repute have long since given p and no notion as being unfounded in fact, UP no modern school text-book of geography that has any pretensions to accuracy indulges in 1-remarks upon the beneficent effect of this ocean current. It is not to be understood that there is no such thing as the Gulf Stream. Far from it. There is certainly such a current and it plays an important part in the life of the world, but it is not the cause of our mild winters or of any of the changes in our climate that weather-foolish people are always discover- Away in the region of the equator the North-East Trade Winds set up a steady drift of water to the West causing what is known as the North Equatorial Current. When this meets the north-east corner of the continent of. South America it divides and one of the branches makes its way into the Carib Lean Sea, round the Gulf of Mexico, and out into the ocean, south of Florida. At this point it is a strong swift current, from 50 to 100 miles wide, and 2,000 feet deep. It has a speed of from two to five miles an hour. As it passes north and fairly parellel to the coast of the United States of America it expands, at the same time losing both in warmth and vel- ocity. As soon as the current reaches the region where the permanent south-wese winds get hold of it, it is turned to the east and its definite character as a great river of water sweeping through the ocean is still further diminished. When half the distanoe between America and England has been traversed it ceases to be recegnisable as a current at all, and its influence on any climate is at an end. So much for the theories that are so often in- vented to account for unusual weather; the Gulf Stream must be put with the moon as a myth so far as it concerns our climate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>WARM WEST COAST.</head>
        <p>THE WARM WEST COAST.</p>
        <p>And yet the fact remains that we do possess a milder climate than many other places in the same latitude and equally near the sea. Also the average temperature down the west coast of England in January is warmer than that down the east coast. If we take a map and mark on it all the places in the British Isles that have a mean January temperature of 40° F. we shall find that they lie to- wards the west. If we join these places by a line, called an isotherm by the geographer, we shall find that it begins in the Isle of Wight, crosses over the head of the Bristol Channel, passes up the centre of Wales and then goes in an almost direct northerly line right up the west coast of Scotland. Near Cape Wrath it turns to the east and cuts the southern end of the Orkney Isles. Thus we see that it is as'warm in January in the north of Scotland as it is in the Isle of Wight. And it was the Gulf Stream that was suppos.sd to be reeponsible for this Ourious phenomenon until the fact discovered that the Gulf Stream did not exist beyond the middle of the Atlantic.</p>
        <p>The explanation of the warm west in winter is to be found; not in any current but in a great surface drift of warm water that is sent permanently across the Atlantic ocean by the west and south-west winds. Such a drift is quite a different thing from a current. A cur- rent flows like a river, divided from the rest of the ocean waters by banks of water dif- fering from its own in temperature, density and salinity. I drift is confined to a movement of the superficial water and is wide spread. The waters of the drift are the waters of the ocean and do not differ from the surroundings in any marked degree. THE GULF STREAM DRIFT. The Gulf Stream waters mcrge in this wes- terly drift and the drift is therefore known as the Gulf Stream Drift. About twenty degrees west of Greenwich it divides; one branch sweeps down the coast of Spain and the west coast of Africa; the other turns to the north and washes the west coast of the British Isles. It finally passes into the Arc- tic Ocean by three arms—one between Green- land and Iceland, a second between Iceland and the Faroe Islands, and the third between the Faroe Islands and the Shetland Isles. It is to this warm, superficial, wind-driven layer that Norway as well as ourselves owes its comparatively mild winters. But if there is this constant drift of water into the Arctic there must be some means of it getting out again. The water becomes cooled and turns south by different channels, flowing in ice-cold currents. One of these is marked on the maps as the Labrador Current and it is always shevrn as running between the Gulf Stream and the coasts of North America. Well, here we have to destroy an- other popular belief. The cold water between the Gulf Stream and the American seaboard is not the Labrador Current but a stream of cold water that geographers now call the Cold Wall and which is formed by the waters issu- ing out of the Gulf of St. Lawrence. The Labrador Current, which is a part of the water of the Gulf Stream Drift returning from its voyage to the north, skirts the coasts of Labrador and Newfoundland, chills them like a refrigerating apparatus and bombards them with ipe-bengs, ISO accounting for the terriole winters of these regions. But, as everybody knows, cold water is heavier than warm water, bulk for bulk, and when the Labrador current meets the warmer Gulf Stream it gets out of the way by diving un- derneath it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>FOGS OFF NEWFOUNDLAND.</head>
        <p>THE FOGS OFF NEWFOUNDLAND.</p>
        <p>The air above the Gulf stream is warm and saturated with moisture while that above the Labrador Current is exceedingly cold. When the two air currents meet a great deal of the moisture in the warm one is condensed and Iheavy fogs are produced. These fogs are the nightmare of the sailors who have to pass the Newfoundland coast. Hidden under the misty curtain other vessels come sailing, mak- ing the air hideous with their syrens. But worse than this, great icebergs, the children of the frozen north. come drifting silently through the fog, bringing destruction to many a gallant barque. The evidence for the movements of the water in the North Atlantic is based chiefly on the reports of bottle voyages and the drift of derelict vessels. This evidence has been col- lected and ,analysed by Lieutenant Hautreux of the Oceanographical Institute at Monaco. For the last twenty three years the pilot charts of the United States have shevrn the drift of de:relicts and during that period no fewer than one hundred and fifty seven have been dealt with. Aa many of the have been seen several times over, the material at our disposal for tracing the path of the currents in this part of the world is fairly ample.</p>
        <p>mi•ENINgi</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
