      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <p>SATURDAY, APRIL 18, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>z F – • ,• • DISTRICT ifiEws !4r</head>
        <p>z F - • ,• • DISTRICT ifiEws !4r</p>
        <p>STOeKTON HEATH.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>NSIIIP TITTLE-TATTLE.</head>
        <p>NSIIIP TITTLE-TATTLE.</p>
        <p>The Vestry was a bit lively. It all arose over freewill offerings. It is many years since such stirring vestry meeting has been held. The Parish Council has a new chairman. He is Councillor Mclntyre, and, according To custom, he will hold the office two years. Cyclists and motor cyclists find the Heath -A fine "jumping off P. -place for tours. ' The Square was a favourite starting place, With quite a large number during the holi- days.</p>
        <p>Councillor Whitley thinks the "Heathens" are well-behaved. At the Police Court, he paid, not one of the Cases involved a resident of the Heath. He thinks this a high tribute to The policing of the district, and this is correct. A few swallows have been seen in the district. Their arrival is said to be a week in advance of last year.</p>
        <p>On the Bench.—The magistrates on, the Bench at the Stockton Heath Petty Sessions on Tues- day were: Sir Gilbert Greenall (in the chair), Messrs. H. C. Border, H. Roberts, J. Hepherkl, L. Greening, P. W. Monks, J. J. Whitley, C. Jackson Holmes, and Captain H. G. Roberts.</p>
        <p>After a Refusal.—Charged with sleeping out, and also with assaulting a Mr. John Jones, of Ashton-by-Sutton,. Walter Page, • a :man of the roving type, was sentenced to three months' hard labour by the magistrates at the Stockton Heath Petty Sessions on Tuesday.—The evidence showed that prisoner, who was remanded from an Occasional Court, was found sleeping out at Preston-o'-tle-Hill. To this he pleaded guilty, but denied the charge of assault.—Mr. Jones stated that prisoner met him on the road, and upon refusing to listen to his solicitations for alms prisoner struck him in the face, and after a struggle kicked him.</p>
        <p>Case Dismissed.—At the Stockton Heath Petty Sessions on Tuesday, Charles Massey, "The ' Hollies," Antrobus, was summoned for causing ininry to Mary Gibbons and Edna Taylor by Wilful misbehavi6r in driving his horse and trap on the wrong side of the road.—He was represented by Mr. Steel.—The police evidence showed that on Saturday, March 21st, Mrs. Mary Giblmins, of Antrobus, was driving a pony and trap along Arley-road in the direction of Stock- ton -Heath, in company with Mrs.,Dclu,a Taylor, of Antrobus. When the trap was nearing the corner of Chester-road defendant came round the bend on the wrong side and a collision fol- lowed. Mrs. Gibbons fell out of the trap and was slightly injured, whilst Mrs. Taylor was thrown forward between the shafts and also injured.—ln cross-examination Mrs. Gibbons ad. mitted the corner was a bad one, and that tilt high hedge ,was as much to blame as anything. She did not believe defendant was guilty of wilful misbehaviour.—Mrs. Taylor also said she did not think defendant acted wilfully.—Supt. Owen asked that the charge might be reduced to one of negligence.—Mr. Steel objected and asked for the case to be dismissed, stating that negli- gence would be another charge altogether.—The Bench agreed to dismiss the case.</p>
        <p>Cows That Strayed.—At the Stockton Heath Petty Sessions on Tuesday, Joseph Percival, Tan Haase, Appleton, was summoned for allowing two cows to stray in Castle-lane, Appleton.— Mr. T. S. Steel defended and pleaded not guilty —Superintendent Owen stated that on Saturday, March 7th, Mrs. Caroline Clarke, Tan House Cottage, Appleton, saw two cows belonging to defendant on the roadway, and saw nobody in charge of them. She later heard that a Mrs. Gill had been knocked down by one of ,the cows and went to lend assistance.—Emma Percival, wife of Arthur Percival, of Castle Farm, Apple- ton, said the cows went into her yard. She saw nobody in attendance when they went into the yard and .she- sent them out. The place where the accident happened was not on the highway, but on some land leading to a field. The husband of the last witness said the man who was supposed to be in charge of the cows came on the scene a minute or two afterwards. He could hardly have any control over the cattle.—Cross-examined, he said that the cows appeared to have been running.—Mr. Steel submitted that no case had been made out. A man employed by the defendant turned the cows out of the shippon and they commenced running. quickly as he Na man breathing could have kept up with them. but the man followed them as missed. possibly could.—The case was dis.</p>
        <p>Highway Offences.—A number of summonses for highway offences was heard at the Stockton Heath Petty Sessions on Tuesday. One of the defendants, William Little, 30, Parr-street, Liverpool, was charged with obstructing Grap- penhall-road, Stockton Heath, by allowing a motor trailer to stand there. Inspector Breeze said that he received, a complaint from a Jus- tice of the Peace, and on inquiry found the trailer obstructing half the roadway. On tax- ing the defendant with the offence he replied that he was waiting for his "mate," who had gone shopping. A fine of 10s. and costs was im- posed upon the absent defendant, the Chairman remarking that a more lenient view would have been taken had Little attended.—Clarence Bing- ham, " Sunnyside," Great Sankey, was fined 58. for failing to produce his motor cycle licence at Whitley Inferior. He Fiaid it was purely an oversight that he had not got it with him.—Max Woods, Rock Villas, Latchford, was charged with failing to produce his motor car licence and with not having a lamp illuminating the identification plate. A constable said defend- ant' told him the lamp had slipped. A fine of ss. was imposed in the first case and 2s. 6d. in the second case.—Samuel Allen, 52, Lord Nelson- street, Warrington, pleaded not guilty to a charge of leaving two horses attached to a furni- ture van unattended at Walton. The police officer said the whole of the van was on the road. Ile wfitclied it for some minutes and f6und de- fendaht inside the Ship Inn. Allen, who con- tended that the vehicle was not on the road but on an open space at the side, was ordered to pay the costs,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>UNDER WOMAN'S INFLUENCE.</head>
        <p>UNDER WOMAN'S INFLUENCE.</p>
        <p>A Servant Girl's Offence.</p>
        <p>The story of a servailt girl yielding to tempta- tion and stealing money from her mistress's bed- room was related at the Stockton Heath Petty Sessions on Tuesday, when Gladys Baker (17), in the employ of Mr. Thomas Fitzgibbon, Wal- ton-roa.., Walton Inferior, was charged with a h 21st and 25th. rc stealii. j a leather Parse containing £l3, the property 'of her employer, between M Iney-</p>
        <p>Mrs. Fitzgibbon said she drew the •mo from the bank and put it in a purse, which,she placed in a drawer in her bedroom. She after- wards found the money was missing. The girl helped witness to look for it and suggested she might call in the police. Inspector Breese said Baker told him she knew nothing about the money, but later she made a statement, in which she said that on March 21st she met an elderly woman in the road, who asked her whether she wanted any- thing. She told her that she did not, and then the woman said: "There is a purse in Mrs. Fitzgibbon's bedroom, with some money in it, which she drew from the bank last night. If you don't get it you will die by my spell." A man got hold of her (defendant) and said: "Get it, or I will kill you." She took the purse and money from the drawer, gave the woman the cash, and burnt the note, which was included in the amount. She was afraid to tell her mis- tress the truth. The Inspector said the girl admitted that the statement was not true, and that she gave the woman ~e 1 because she was afraid of her. She kept .Q 5 herself. Defendant, continued the Inspector, had paid .23 38. for a costume, 48. for a taxi-cab fare, ss. 11d, for a blouse, and 2s. 11d, for a pair of gloves. She could not say what she had done with the rest of the money. It was found she had also paid otl 4s. for a dinner set.</p>
        <p>Superintendent Owen said the girl, was a native of •Penketh and had been in different places of service, where she had been strongly suspected of petty pilfering. Baker said she did not know wily she com- mitted the theft. She thought the bank-note she burnt was simply a piece of ordinary paper. She said the story regaiding the woman was true.</p>
        <p>The Chairman expressed the view that it was not true, and the matter was a very serious one for so young a person. Baker was bound over to be of good behaviour for 12 months on promising to go into a home for three months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>NEV PARISH CHAIRMAN.</head>
        <p>NEV PARISH CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>Councillor Mclntyre Appointed.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting Parish Council was held on Wednesday in the Council Schools. 0:n1116110r J. Whitley, the retiring chairman, presided at the start of the business. of the Stockton Heath _</p>
        <p>Councillor Westbrook proposed the election of Councillor Mclntyre as chairman for the en- suing year, and paid a tribute to the work he had accomplished as a councillor. Councillor Pearson seconded and also spokwof what Councillor Mclntyre had done. The proposal was carried unanimously, and on Councillor Mclntyre taking the chair he thanked the members for the honour they had conferred upon him. The Overseers for the parish were elected as follows: Messrs. J. Whitley, Pearson and' War- burton.</p>
        <p>The meetings were fixed for once a quarter, and a number of accounts were passed for pay- pent. _ _</p>
        <p>EX-CHAIRMAN'S TRIBUTE. Councillor Wiiitley said they all agreed with the selection of Councillot Mclntyre as chair- man. He hoped the new chairman would be given health and strength to carry out his duties, and that Councillor Mclntyre would have the sympathy and loyalty extended to him while chairman. He also spoke of the great help he had received from the Clerk. Many important matters have, said Coun- cillor Whitley, been discussed during the yew at the Parish Council, the Parish Meetings, 'and the Parochial Committee, and a large amount of the time of the councillors had been devotsfl to.advancing the welfare of the parish. _</p>
        <p>Best in 40 Parishes. The parish now stood first in all the 40 Parishes connected with the Runcorn Rural Dis- trict Council, both in regard to rateable value and sanitary matters. There had been advance- ment all along the line. The parish at the present time, I am glad to state, is free from any serious crime. At the Petty Sessions on Tuesday, out of the ten cases which came before the magistrates, thete was not one resident of Stockton Heath involved' It speaks well for the attention given to the police affairs of the district, and it is a credit to the Parish that the police have so few cases.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>GRAPPENH AL L.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENH AL L.</p>
        <p>resi- dence, Death of Mr. A. T. Dakin.—The death of Mr. Alfred Thurstan Dakin took place at his nesdaY morning, after the unremitting attention "The Willows," Grappenhall, on Wed- of his medical adviser, Dr. Joseph. The deceased gentleman carried on the business of a master tanner, which he relinquished some few years ago, when he retired into Private life', He was a Conservative and Churchman, but did not take any active part in public affairs. He leaves a mourn his loss. widow, two sons and one,daughter to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>HIGH LEG Fl.</head>
        <p>HIGH LEG Fl.</p>
        <p>Cricketers' Dance.—The High Legh School was the scene of a merry company on Thursday evening, when a dance was held in aid of the funds of the Village Cricket and Bowling Club. There were about a hundred present, who danced to music supplied by Mr. Percy Healey. The event was a marked success. Mr. J. T. Bend was a capable M.C.</p>
        <p>took highl Funeral of Mr. John Percival.—The funeral John Percival, of High Legh, a well-known and Place on Tuesday at Oughtrington of Mr. gentleman in the district. Deceased, who was a hay and straw dealer, was Y'resPeeted on business in Manchester on Thursday of last week, when he was taken ill and had to be re- moved to his home, where he died the following day. He leaves a widow and a family of six. The Clarke, Edward (widow), Mr., Charles Percival, Mr. George Per- cival, Mr. and Dirs. Powell, Mr. and Mrs. and Miss Houghton. The bearers were Messrs. John ardmsoiumrpnseorns an were Mrs. Percival d Jackson. Mr. E. AtkinsQn, High Legh, had charge of the funeral arrangements. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>PRETTY LOCAL WEDDING.</head>
        <p>A PRETTY LOCAL WEDDING.</p>
        <p>Marriage of Miss A. Clarke.</p>
        <p>A pretty wedding was solemnised at Ros- theme Parish Church on Monday, when the contracting parties were Mr. Alfred Rowson Cragg, son of Mr. and Mrs. William Cragg, of Rainow, Macclesfield, and Miss Annie Clarke, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. John Clarke, of High Legh.</p>
        <p>The bride, who was glyen awn'y by her father, wore a cream costume, with silk hat to match, and carried a bouquet of lilies. She was attended by her cousin, Miss Emma Percival, as bridesmaid. The R. E. H. Edwards (chap- lain of Colonel Legh's private chapel) was the officiating clergyman, whilst Mr. Harry Todd, of Knutsford, acted as best man. Prior to the happy couple leaving for Southport, where the honeymoon is being spent, a reception was held at the home of the bride's parents. A large number of presents were received. The bride- groom is a well-known Warrington school- master, who was formerly connected with a Knut::ford school.</p>
        <p>iii•ammoimimmomimmimmilmiimm</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>LY 111</p>
        <p>Licence Granted.—Mr. Sydney Collin was gritnted permission by the magistrates at the Lf mm Petty Sessions on Wednesday to sell at an off-licence beerhouse at Rush Green, in lieu of Emily Benton.</p>
        <p>On the Bench.—The sitting magistrates at the Lymm Petty Sessions on Wednesday were: Mr. A. Watkin (in the chair), the Hon.,J.:E. Cross, and Messrs. A. Callender, J. Holder, Ridgway, and J. E. G. Groves.</p>
        <p>Easter Collections.—The collections at the Lymm Parish Church on Good Friday, amount- ing to £3 17s. 5d., were for the Chester House of Mercy, whilst the offertories on Easter Day-7 .6108 16s. 2d.—were for the Rector (the Rev. G. B. Thurston).</p>
        <p>Territorial Camp.--On Good Friday about 60 officers and men of the 7th King's (Bootle), Royal Engineers (St. Helens), and West Lancashire Divisional Signalling Companies, RE., came to Lymm, when a week-end camp was pitched in Mr. Lea Warburton's field, near the station. Many local people visited the camp.</p>
        <p>Easter in Free Churches.---The Free Churches celebrated Easter by holding a united service in the Higher-lane Eaptist Chapel on Good Friday morning, when the Rev. E. E. Lovell preached a powerful and appropriate sermon from 11. Cor- inthians viii. 9, on the subject of "The Poverty and Gift of the Lord Jesus Christ."</p>
        <p>• Local Wedding.—A pretty ceremony, which attracted a large number of persons to the Parish Church on Monday afternoon, was the marriage of Mr. Fred Jones, of Heeley, Sheffield, to Miss Alice Elizabeth Guest, daughter of Mr. Alfred Guest, of the square, Lymm. The Rev, G. B. Thurston was the officiating clergyman.</p>
        <p>A Dear Cycle Ride.—At the Lymm Petty Sessions on Wednesday, Albert .nedhead was: charged with riding a bicycle without a lighted lamp.—P.C. Atherton said that on the 20th of last month he saw defendant in-Warrington- road, Lymm, riding the cycle' without a light. When taxed with the offence he replied that the bicycle had been lent to him, and he wanted to get home to see some friends, and that lid was only riding a short distance.—The Chairman said it was dangerous .to ride without a light, especially in the days of motor traffic.—lie Was fined 2s. 6d., including costs.</p>
        <p>Gave a Wrong Name.—At the Lymm Petty Sessions on Wednesday, Thomas Grant, of 173, Liverpool-road, Cadishead, was charged with not having a lighted lamp on his lurry on March 21st at Lynam.—The police evidence showed that defendant was driving a horse and lurry. His lamp was not lighted, and when questioned he said his candle had' burned out. On examina- tion of the lamp it was found that only half of the candle had been used. When asked for his • name he said " William Wateley, of Cadishead" —the name on the cart. This proved to be wrong.—The Clerk: Have you anything to say? —No, sir.—He was fined ;is.; including costs.</p>
        <p>Easter at Parish Church.—}'or the extra ser- vices at the Lymm Parish Church during Eastertide the Rector has had the assistance of the Rev. Hubert A. Dempster, the organising ' secretary for the S.P.G. in the Diocese of Lincoln and Peterborough. Mr. Dempster took the devotional service on Good Friday afternoon, and at night conducted a largely attended lan- tern service. The pretty and chaste decorations at the church on `Easter Day were the work of Mesdames Burrowes, Cooke, Dixon, Far car, Giles Hunt, Johnson, Newmarsh, Smith and! Watkin, and the Misses Boumphrey, Callender, Garton, Hartley, Hatton, Hubback, Moles. worth and Rawlins. The Rey. H. A. Dempster addresser'', the .childrerew.service held in the afternoon and preached at evensong.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Serious Motor Cycle Accident.-31r. Fred Thompson, of Bank Gardens, Penketh, met with a serious accident on Tuesday morning. While following his employment as ganger over the road construction workmen, under the Lanca- shire county Council, he was knocked down by a motor cyclist on. the Liverpool-road. Help was obtained and he was removed\ in a float to his home, where medical attention was given.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Tea.—The annual Good Friday tea was held in the Wesleyan schoolroom, at whidh a goodly number of members, scholars and friends did ample justice. Afterwards, with the Rev. Harry Middlewood in the chair, the various secretaries gave their reports on the year's work. Then came a concert given by the children, under the direction of the Misses F. Moores, A. Davies and M. Routledge, and this was well re- ceived. 'The distribution of the prizes for at- tendance by Mrs. James Dodds brought a pleas- ant evening to its close. •</p>
        <p>Cycling Club. Penketh Cycling Club, although only of this year's formation, is mak- ing most satisfactory progress in its, member- ship. The first long run was held on Saturday:, the destination being West Houghton. On Monday the members went to Eastham. The two runs were greatly enjoyed by all those g'ho took part in them.. If any cyclist in the dis- trict would like any particulars regarding the club, the genial secretary, Mr. A. Hicks, the Park, Penketh, will be only too pleased to oblige.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>BANDSMEN'S TRIUMPH.</head>
        <p>BANDSMEN'S TRIUMPH.</p>
        <p>A Victory in a Competition.</p>
        <p>The Penketh Tannery Prize Brass and paid a visit to West Houghton on Saturday in the hope of bringing home some prize money, medals and another Cup. The band contest took place in the Gala Field, the entries numbering 26. All the bands competed. The test piece was a fan- tasia, "Sons of the Sea," arranged by the well- known conductor, Mr. W. Rimmer, of South- port. The judge was Mr. Albert Lawton, of Leicester.</p>
        <p>Owing to the number of competitors it was a late hour before the decision could be given. The judge, in his remarks, siiid that there could be no possibility of a doubt as to which band was entitled to the first prize, and that was Penketh Tannery, conductor Mr. J. A. Green- wood. The other prize-winners were: 2, Thorn- soft; 3, Horwich R.M.I. ; 4, Glazebury Church: 5, Pendleton Public. . The first prize consisted of .110 and a silver challenge cup valued at ..tlO 10s. Not satisfied with that, the Penketh first, second and third trombonists each received a medal for the best trombone trio. Owing to a difficulty in starting the motor engine of the char-a-bane, it was some time before the home- ward journey began. If the arrival home was in the wee sma' hours, what did it matter? The journey had not been in vain. The next trip was on Easter Monday to Eastham to provide efficient music for the danc- ing in the Gardens.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>PREsTON BROoK.</head>
        <p>PREsTON BROoK.</p>
        <p>FIFTY-SEVEN YEARS IN PRIMI- 11'1V E METHODISM. • An , Interesting , Re-union.</p>
        <p>O. .A service held on Good Friday at the Primi- tive Methodist Chapel, Preston Brook, Was Of more than usual interest, inasmuch as it formed the means of a re-union on the part of three workers in the cause of nearly sixty years' Standing. . Two of these personalities,--the Rev. Dr. 'Cooper Antliff (now of Canada) and the Bev. T. H.. Hunt (now of Southport)—have worked in the cause as ministers while the third —Mr. James Whitley, of Stockton Heath—has been an active lay worker. The last-named has lived in the vicinity all his life, but the work of the other two has taken them to various parts Of the world. ' Mr. Whitley was born at Aston in 1840, on the 10th of April, so that on the day of the re-union he was 74 years of age.</p>
        <p>. Dr. -Antliff's father was station minister in the Preston Brook Circuit over 50 years ago. Dr. Antliff entered the Colonial Field in 1878. He became President of the Montreal Confer- ence in 1891, and secretary of the first general Methodist Conference in recognition of the pro- minent part he took in promoting the union. For nine years he was a Professor in the Mon- treal Theological College, and has edited the " Christian Journal." He has lost none of his enthusiasm for religious re-union, and looks for- ward confidently to the amalgamation of the Presbyterian, Congregational 'and Methodist Church in about two years' time. The Rev. T. H. Hunt, who, like Dr. Antliff, has filled many important circuits and offices, has also been President of the Conference. Ile is a native of Hatton, his father having been a Primitive Methodist lay reader for many years, and highly respected.</p>
        <p>THE EVENING MEETING. Mr. J. Whitley's Reminiscences.</p>
        <p>There was an excellent attendance at the evening meeting, following the splendid tea pro- vided by the ladies. The Rev. J. S. White, superintendent of the Preston BrOok Circuit, who now resides in Runcorn, opened the meet- ing. Mr. J. Whitley, of Stockton Heath, presided, and said lie was pleased to be present on such a memorable-occasion. For 57 years Dr. Antliff, the Rev. T. H. Hunt, now of Southport, and himself, had been closely connected with that society. Dealing .with the history of Methodism in the district, he said he found that the Wesleyan cause was founded by Mr. Joseph Janion, of Parkside Farm, Aston, who was converted to Wesleyanism at the age of 23 years. He was assisted by, George Pugh, farmer, of New Pale, Delamere Forest; Mr. William Hayes, of the Salt Works, Frodsham Bridge; Mr. W. Dutton, Weston, and Mr. Daniel Barker, of Little Leigh. They formed a society- class in 1775. They were then in the Liverpool Circuit, and one noted worker was Hannah Dodd, of Preston-on-the- Hill.</p>
        <p>A Rough, Rude Structure. The place in which the earliest Methodists worshipped was a rough, rude strubture, some- thing like the log cabins of the wild West of America. The roof w 9,8 of thatch, the walls of logs, and the floor of clay, upon which rushes and fern fronds were strewn. There were no window-8 and the light came through holes in the wall.</p>
        <p>Primitive Methodism was founded in the dis- trict by Lorenza Dow, who came to England from America in 1806, and preached several times at Preston Brook. Hugh Bourne and W. clowes, who came .from Mow Cop, North Staf- fordshire, also carried on services at Preston Brook. .Then the district was in the Tunstall (Staffordshire) Circuit, and those men used to walk 40 miles from Tunstall to preach at Pres- ton Brook. In 1823 their district was formed into a separate circuit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>FIFTY YEARS' MINISTRY.</head>
        <p>FIFTY YEARS' MINISTRY.</p>
        <p>The Rev. T. H. Hunt, who, 57 years ago, was connected with the circuit, gave a lecture on ''Reminiscences of a 50 years' ministry. in Eng- land and Canada.." Dr.- MAUL Canada, who 'also had a half; century association with the circuit, also spoke.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>ORFORD.</head>
        <p>ORFORD.</p>
        <p>No Sermon.—A special service was held in Orford Church on Good Friday evening, con- sisting of shortened evensong, solos and no ser- mon. The Vicar conducted the service. Mr. J. Gibson sang with feeling "He was despised" ("Messiah"); Mr. W. Knowles sang "0 rest in the Lord" ("Elijah"), and Mi. W. Povey sang "The Holy City?" The services on Easter Sun- day were as follows: 7.15 a.m., hymns on the tower; 8 o'clock, holy communion; 10.30, matins, holy communion and sermon; 3 o'clock, child- ren's service; and 6.30, evensong and sermon. At the evpning service special music was rem 'dere‘the'choir singing the anthems "We bow in prayer" and "They have taken away ray Lord." The preacher at all the services was the Vicar (Rev. G. Vale Owen). The collections throughout the day were given to the Vicarage Fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>WIN WICK.</head>
        <p>WIN WICK.</p>
        <p>CHURCH SALE OF WORK.</p>
        <p>Mr. John Stone's Reminiscences.</p>
        <p>A very successful sale of work was held in Winwick School on Thursday. There' was a large attendance of parishioners and friends. The proceeds were in aid of the Church School extension fund, for which a sum of £1,200 is required. Canon Penrhyn presided, introduc- ing Mrs. John Stone, of Roby, who declared the sale open. Mr. William Massey (churchwarden) proposed, and Mr. P. Irving Dutton seconded, a vote of thanks to Mrs. Stone. Mr. John Stone, late high sheriff, responded on behalf of his wife, and gave some interesting reminiscences of his boyhood days in Winwick. Amongst •other things he mentioned that as a boy he climbed the church steeple and carved his name on one of the stones, which is now, since the church renovation, in the garden of Nekon Park. During a recent visit to Ceylon he stated that he met an old friend named Wright, formerly of Winwick, who pointed to his ovetmantel in his Eastern home, and asked Mr. Stone if he recognised a piece of oak, and said that it was from an old palw in Winwick Church.</p>
        <p>Mr. Robert Stone proposed a vote of thanks to the Rector for presiding, and enlarged on the impodtinc6 of helping Church schools, the object of which is to educate children not only in worldly knowledge, but also in that character for which Englishmen are known throughout the world.</p>
        <p>The vote of thanks was seconded by the Rev. J. T. Owen.</p>
        <p>Amongst those present were: Mrs. Lionel Fairelough, Mrs. Nield (late Mayoress of War- rington), Mrs. Fairelough, Mrs. Penrhyn, Mrs. Williams, Ashton-in-Makerfield; Miss Carson, Wargrave; Miss Wood, Newton-le-Willows; Mrs. Henry Roberts, Warrington; Rev. H. V. and Mrs. Pigot, Rev. E. C. E. Carleton and Mrs. Carleton, Rev. G. Vale Owen and Mrs. Owen, Rev. W. Williams, Downall Green.</p>
        <p>THE STALLHOLDERS. The stallholders were: Miss Penrhyn, Mrs. J. T. Owen, Mrs. Dutton, Mrs. Pettiner, Mrs. Longton, Miss Burrows, Mrs. Greenhalgh, Miss Jefferson, Fraulein Paschen, Mrs. poden, Mrs. Davies, Middleton Hall; Misses Marjorie and Nellie Davies, Miss Langton and Miss Clara Longton. The refreshments were in charge of the Misses Bessie and Mary Penrhyn, assisted by Miss E. Yates, Mrs. Baker, Mrs. Singleton, Mrs. Edging- ton and Miss Robinson. _</p>
        <p>The sale was managed by a committee, of which the Rev. J. T. Owen was secretary, and Mr.. P. Irving Dutton treasurer.'</p>
        <p>Tbe proceeds were £l9O, including .250, the gift of Mrs. John Stone.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
