      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>TABLE TALK.</head>
        <p>TABLE TALK.</p>
        <p>The World's Tribute of Interesting</p>
        <p>Items of News.</p>
        <p>QUAINT AND AMUSING.</p>
        <p>From '_Ameriast comes 'notifiCation'Of a new dual device—piano-playing with shirtmaking, says " Musical News." It seems a keyboard has been devised which, besides presenting the means of ordinary playing, is so planned that whenever a key is.put down a stitch is also made in some garment. At once an encouragement and a solace. to sempstresSes. The More notes played the more stitches made, and, of course, the more money earned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>North Australia.</head>
        <p>North Australia.</p>
        <p>Captain H. V. Barclay, who three years ago left Melbourne with nine assistants and a string of 22 camels to explore the Northern Territory of Australia, on behalf of the Federal Govern- ment, has returned to Melbourne. For the past 18 months he has been engaged on survey work on the north coast, principally in the vicinity of the Fellow Islands, where there is a likelihood of a harbour being made for trade development and possibly a naval base. Since leaving Mel- bourne, Captain Barclay, now 70 years of age; has travelled over 5,000 miles and examined 3,000 miles of , coast line. He thinks the ,Territory is distinctly a white man's country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>Premier's Smile.</head>
        <p>The Premier's Smile.</p>
        <p>It Willi amusing .to see, through a pair of powerful binoculars, the play, of emotions on Mr. 'Asquith's face• as he Watched theßugbjr. football international at Twickenham,,the "Car- penter" 'writes in the "Express." He was sit- ting. on the right of the King, and, While his Majesty obviously appreciated every incident of a glorious struggle, .the Premier seemed to be unaccustomed to the friendly ferocity of Rugby exchanges. Every now and then he clutched his highly-buttoned overcoat collar with nervous 'fitigers, and his face relaxed into n smile only when the English forward, Brown, had such a bad tear in his raiment that the teams made a four-cornered human dressing-room of them- selves while he changed., •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>Weather and Working Hours.</head>
        <p>Weather and Working Hours.</p>
        <p>Many, of those who advocate changes in the working hours of the agricultural labourer do not fully realise how vastly the conditions differ from those which govern industrial production. During the past month farmers have had the utmost difficulty in finding work for their regu- lar hands, and the day to day workers have been very frequently turned back. Our readers know that we strongly advocate long engagement. A day to day bargain, or even a week to week bar- gain, is a pernicious one for the labourer. The very first step in the improvement of his condi- tion should lie in the direction of getting greater security of employment. But in order to do this he ought to make a reasonable con- cession. In months such as the one we have just passed through the payment of regular wages has been a loss to the farmer. It was impossible that he should receive an equivalent for them in work. The most reasonable way out of the difficulty would be to make the hours as short as possible in bad weather, and in return the labourers might give extra time when it im- proves. This is a concession which those who are engaged by the day might very well make in return for regular employment.---" Country Life."</p>
        <p>Curious Inn Signs. .In a,„return, of, the licences of public-houses which are in the courSe of extinction, issued by the London County Council, occur many pecu- liar names. The Freetrader is to disappear from Woolwich, and the Cobden Tavern from Kensington. The Hampshire Hog will be seen no tnOre in the West'EnCl. The ComicaFFellowS are quieted at Camberwell, and the True Friends cannot be met with any more in White- chapel. Other disappearances are, the Log and Partridge in Bow, the Chequers and Greenland Fishery, Shadwell, and the Queen's Landing in the same district. Fubb's Yacht leaves Gr9k,n- wich.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>Novel _ldea in Road Making.</head>
        <p>Novel _ldea in Road Making.</p>
        <p>puts are the bane of country roads, and one farmer has experimented until he has found a way to eliminate them. The idea is simply to fill the ruts of a prairie wagon road with con- crete. The filling should be four or five inches thick, and about eight inches wide on top, the surface being hollowed out about i inches deep for vehicle wheels. It is easy to drive over, as the wheels follow the tracks "with first intent," and four years' wear on the experimental roads built by the originator show no wear nor corro- sion. The cost is given as £lO5 per mile, and the roads are not only free-driving in wet weather, but are almost dustless in dry condi- tion.—From the March Number of "Popular Mechanics Magazine."</p>
        <p>, Driving Signals. For the regulation of motor traffic, a 'corre- spondent of the " Autocar" suggests a simple system of signalling by horn blowing. When a motor is approaching a cross-road and it is thel intention of the driver to go straight forward, he should signal this intention by one blast of the horn. If he be turning to the right he should give two blasts; if to the left (the most danger- ous position) he should give three blasts. Each driver then would be aware of the intention of the other and know exactly what to do. Horns should be used exclusively by motors and motor cycles; bicycles (and auto-wheels) should follow the same rules, but should use bells. By this simple method of signalling motorists would be relieved of much anxiety, and their pleasure and comfort be greatly increased, and the pub- lic would have a considerable safeguard.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST</p>
        <p>—continued.</p>
        <p>against rheumatism, and I know of more than one instance of well-to-do ladies picking up lumps of coal in the roadway because of their supposed talismanic significance or value. Of Parnell's horror of the number 13 many stories are told. Once, when at a vital crisis a series of thirteen public meetings had been planned for the Irish leader, he refused to speak at the first of -the series until the arrangements for a four- teenth had been concluded. Before a Parlia- mentary Committee not long ago a witness from the remote island of Rona (Skye), which a doc- tor rarely visits, told of a "cure" recently ap- plied in the case of an epileptic. A black cock was buried alive beneath the spot where the patient had had the first attack of epilepsy. He also described the successful treatment of a woman suffering from "King's evil" by a sevent4 son, to whom she had gone all the way to the island of Scalpay (Harris). I was always fond,of listening to quack doctors in the market, and I lately heard one claiming something like supernatural powers on the ground that he was the seventh son of a seventh son. A doctor in South Harris tells of a person in his district who had a carbuncle on the back of his neck, and it did not heal, so he got a seventh son to go to his house, and every night for a long time he put water on it and a sixpence round his neck. It is in such a field of ignorant faith that the " Skilly " woman can practise all her arts at will. But really there is abundance of evi- dence much nearer our own .doors. I know A wealthy lady in Southport who is in the habit of consulting a "wise woman" who pretends to read the future by crystal-gazing, and she has many such clients. I do not think very much of the increasing use of mascots, as it is prob- ably more a fashion than a superstition. Alto- gether apart therefrom, however, I have plenty of facts in support of the proposition with which I set out—but I am exceeding my allotted space, and must lay these aside for the present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 18, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 18, 1914.</p>
        <p>THE WORLD OF LETTERS. AMATEUR PHOTOGRAPHY.</p>
        <p>The Magazines.</p>
        <p>TROUT FISHING. The innocent Pleasure of Intellectual Men.</p>
        <p>It would not be altogether fair to our eels, our 4/nixi,nows, our •roach,: dace and pike to sug- gest that the trout is the commonest fish of our country. Nevertheless, he is certainly the most popular. And his distribution is very general, and would be far more general still if river pollution was—where proven and wilful—an offence punished by imprisonment. As it is, our fair streams and rivers are all too often deluged with filth, and the law of our land is but a pastime for the idle rich. Nevertheless, it might reasonably be expected that any Govern- ment, even our own Government, would have looked upon the watercourses as an heirloom to be handed down to posterity with increased rather than diminished value, and would have spared no effort towards this end. And there is, too, the question of poaching, happily not a serious one in England, but in Wales and Ire- land menacing enough if not already past that 'stage. But poaching is becoming in certain districts more and more a question of party politics, and once in that trealm, we know that nothing counts except the vote. Let us, there- fore, 'remove the game laws that men may poach without fear and vote—for us; let us be blind to the killing of spawning fish, to the dynamit- ing of pools, that men may say, " These are the fellows for us, these are the sportsmen for whom we have long been looking—come, let us -vote for them."</p>
        <p>rtild 'after all,'it is only the few who bother themselves with rod and line, a,nd they are the privileged and the idle. The ral worker, the horny-handed pet of politicians, has only time to run down to .the river and use lime or dynamite. Why should he not have his "sport," bless him?</p>
        <p>But I, for myself, shall take the liberty of cursing him, and all those with him who allow anything to flow into streams and rivers that destroy the life of fishes. For not only is there an almost inexhaustible food supply maintain- able with care beneath the surface of the water, but above it there is a .vast army of intellectual men taking their innocent pleasure in the use of fly and other lures, and recuperating themselves in the best of all possible manners after the toil and labour they have employed on behalf of their families and of their country.—A. R. Haig Brown in " Fry's Magazine."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>ENGLISH AND AMERICAN CONTRASTS.</head>
        <p>ENGLISH AND AMERICAN CONTRASTS.</p>
        <p>The April number of "Great Thoughts" (Smith's Publishing Co., 6d.) contains an inter- esting interview with Principal Starr Jordan, of Leland University, California. Talking about "Young America" he says: "In America youth is afforded every oppor- tupity—far more, I imagine, than is the case in -England. For instance,' we have ten times as many universities as you have; even California, with only two millions of population, has 7,500 students in the universities; that is a higherl ,average than at any other university in the 1 States. Well, this is affecting the whole lives and prospects of our youth. They are better trained for life than ever before; they are berng shown the way of life as never before in the his- tory of the world. With us the national char- acter is improving all the time. For instance, we could never bear the interruptions at public meetings that you bear , here, especially the suffragettes, public opinion would be against it. With us, too, the character of young men is wonderfully improved. Drunkenness is discour- aged. A man cannot attend drinking places and I retain his position in society, except in the gilded circles, where all thing s are .possible, I regret to say. Fortunately, they have no real influence. They, alas! are neither improving nor are they capable of improvement. The gilded youth of America is like the apple—rotten at the core and quite beyond hope and reclama- tion. Fortunately, they are as devoid of influ- ence as they are of the respect of the community at large; they are like the foam on the water with no fundamental influence, and they are taken seriously only by their frivolous selves or in Paris, where even now they are not very highly esteemed or thought of."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>MOTORISTS SUMMONED AT ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>MOTORISTS SUMMONED AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Robert Heyes, of BiHinge, and George Jones, of Pemberton, were charged at .St. Helens on Tuesday with riding to the danger of the public at Moss Bank on March 25.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Ashcroft said the two men rode through the village at a mad pace, racing each other. There were a number of thildren playing in the road, and they were in great danger. She had known Heyes for ten years, and was 9uite certain that he was one of the riders. She also went to Pemberton on the 6th inst. and identified tones as he was alighting from a tramcar along with a number of other colliers returning from work. Supt. Mq.ean : If you had not risked your own life to pull your own boy out of the way he might have been killed?—Witness : Yes.</p>
        <p>Constable Hunt said he saw the two defend- ants riding past his house. They were going very fast. He blew his whistle for them to stop, and Heyea, turned his head, witness put- ting his hand up for him to stop, but they took no notice. He saw Heyes next morning at Bil- linge and told him he would be reported, and he said: "My motor bicycle is broken down, and I was not out." Witness said the cycles were travelling from thirty to thirty-five miles an hour.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. C. Davies, schoolmaster, said the motor cyclists travelled at the rate of at least thirty miles an hour. There were at least a dozen children in front of the school, and witness was perfectly horrorstricken when he saw the cycles travelling so_ quickly.</p>
        <p>Heyes said Jones was out with a very old machine and 'a sidecar, which would not ascend Moss Bank Hill on either side.</p>
        <p>Edward Abbott, a farmer, said he saw Heyes and the other man near iMoss Bank Station, after they had ridden down the hill. They were then going slowly. He was sure that Heyes was there, because he had known him since be was a little boy.</p>
        <p>_ Sergt. Fidling said he went to see Jones and old him he would be reported, when he made a statement that he was on the Carr Mill-road with an old motor cycle and sidecar, but he had never been up the hill. Jones and Heyes both swore on oath that they were never up Moss Bank Hill on this night, Jones being most positive that he had .never been over Moss Bank in his life. John Rogeison, motor mechanic, of 4, Chapel- street, Pemberton, 'said Jones's machine could not possibly ride this hill. - -</p>
        <p>- James liimmer and Heyes's brother gave cor- roborative evidence.</p>
        <p>_ _ The magistrates recalled Constable Hunt and Mr. Abbott, who both again swore 'that they knew Reyes well, and were clear as to the date and place. • The Chairman (Alderman Bishop) said they dismissed Jones, because they did not think the evidence was conclusive. They convicted Heyes and fined him £1 Bs.. Gd. or seven days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>Literary Gleanings.</head>
        <p>Literary Gleanings.</p>
        <p>FOOD -REFORM. The normal, healthy man quite rightly does not think much about what he eats so long as it tastes fairly good and there is plenty of it. There are reasons, however, why even he slvuld 'take an interest ir. the qiestion of diet ieforki— a question Which has become so acute of late years. The great movement of scientific re- search and social study have both made per- fectly clear the fact that the majority of the people are not as healthy and as efficient as they might he, and one of the root causes of this is that they do not obtain sufficient food, or that they do not obtain the right kind of food. The last statement applies not only to the poorer classes, but also, as has been fully shown by Chittenden, Hindhede, and others in recent years, the classes with ample means. The great a few scattered cases fifty years ago to the increase in the fr4queney of appendicitis, from thousands of the present day ; the persistence of the cancer plague; the continued proportionS.te increase in the number of mentally defective; all call for an investigation ,of, amongst other things, the food from which the nation builds up its flesh and blood.—A. G. Simmins, in." Good Health."</p>
        <p>PIERCING THE SELKIRKS. The Canadian Pacific Railway Company are building a five-mile two-track tunnel through the Selkirk Mountains, near Rogers Pass, British Columbia. This tunnel was commenced last April, and the two pioneer bores are well, on their way to completion. It is anticipated that the cost will be tlooooolt is one of the largest of North America, and will be completed bY the latter e.urn pd ae tt undertakings nngesx to f its kind on the Contin- ent year. 'The building of the tunnel will reduce the rail distance through the kelr otuhnetmainsBl444feemtilleesss than at the present , and will make the When it is complete and in operation no snow or earth slides will be able to get on the tracks, and there will be no more than six miles Sgtilem.alkdeie.r over all told of 2.2 grade through the mountains.</p>
        <p>. CHURCHWARDEN STORIES. A e warden goes to •church must be anything. one never knows what to expect, perd for ctbwutlienlcertainly never could have " imagined " what actually happened to me one Sunday. It is my habit to carry the plate to "the real swells" of our suburban congrega- tion, and upon a certain memorable day, a woman dressed in the height of fashion kept me waiting at the end of her row, while she first removed her glove off her right hand, with great deliberation; then fished .her purse out of her pocket, extracted a shilling therefrom, grace- fully put the coin in the plate, and then leisurely helped herself to six-penn'orth of cop- pers as change! Another experience with a female worshipper may be recalled. She came early one Sunday evening and lodged a complaint with me that she had lost her watch at the morning service. "Where were you sitting?" "Close by the pulpit !" "Are you sure you lost the watch in the church?" . "Oh, yes! Quite sure. I remember unfasten- ing it from my wrist'and putting it on the book. ledge !" "Well," I said, "I will go and, ask the verger." I did so, but he had seen no watch, and re- minded me that at the afternoon•service the church had been packed with school children; he also added for my own special information that "a watch is a watch, sir, and a boy is a boy, sir, an' I need say no more, sir !" I reported to the aggrieved parishioner that the watch had not been seen, and took her name and address, and promised to make inquiries from the school teachers. With this she was satisfied and w'ent to lc:er seat. Presently the evening service commenced, and when the time came for the first hymn, the Rector, who knew nothing of the incident, gave me a thrill which I shall never forget, by announcing, in piercing tones, "Hymn 362—Lord, her watch Thy Church is keeping."—" A Chat About Churchwardens," in the "Sunday at Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>GERMAN STUDENT DUELS.</head>
        <p>GERMAN STUDENT DUELS.</p>
        <p>to come . however, Lots of fs tlr afinr first AThnedyitaiies gterematerdbuuergllertsheint IMasartbuyregu! with me in order to learn something of the ways German a n clearer t tae ytpelua,y o things h te will b tell you in a few words something of this old- world university town of Southern Germany, and something, too, of that strange and interest- ing practice which we as a nation left behind with the blunderbuss and the lighwayman. "Marburg," said one of its students to me one day, with pride and affection, •` is quite different from Berlin, Heidelberg, Leipzig, Munich, and most German university towns. These places have universities, it is true, but Marburg, sir, is a university." The more I saw of his univer- sity, the more I became impressed with the truth of his remark. Marburg is the university and the university is Marburg. There is no phase of life there into which the university atmosphere has not penetrated. ' The students in the university number about , two thousand four hundred, and are split up I into a considerable number of clubs or " Kor- Porations," many of which Possess very fine club-houses, and each of which is governed by very strict self-imposed regulations. Generally speaking, these clubs can be divided into two classes:—fighting clubs and non-fighting clubs, the members of , the one fighting (i.e., duelling) on principle, and the members of the other ' class abstaining for exactly the same reason. Duels are fought for sport regularly each Sat- urday morning, and also at specially appointed times to avenge insults. " Siibel" and 1" Schlager " are the weapons used, the former ' being long, curved swords, used only in duels of a serious nature, and the latter, long straight' swords, ground to a fine edge on both sides, blunt at the point and provided with a huge basket-shapd hilt. Duelling is, of courO! prohibiOd by the uni- versity authoritiei and by the civil authoriti•m, too, but the custom has got sal+ a firm hold that they are compelled to rest content with a formal prohibition and look with a blind eye on its practice and results. Students walk into the lecture rooms on Monday morning with bandages covering half their' faces, but no in- convenient questions .are asked, though all the world knows that Saturday's duels were respon- sible for them. The fighting corps make some little compromise, however, by holding all their fights " out of bounds.",--" Duelling at a Ger- man University," in •• The,l3coy' Own Paper."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>BABY'S ECZEMA SPREAD.</head>
        <p>BABY'S ECZEMA SPREAD.</p>
        <p>48, E Stepney, London, E. Eng. My weeks old when I noticed a - absatfibevld ;satsrese ixt, pimple on his face and in two or three days there were several more spots came and they gradually peeled off until his face was like a raw piece of beef. I used to sit and cry every time I looked at him. It began to itch and the baby would scratch it until it bled. Wherever the blood ran more spots came. We had to watch him closely and tie his hands up to prevent him scratching it. It used to itch more night than day which meant 'keeping us awake, and then when he got in a nice sleep it would commence itching and would swell, his eyes being sunken in his head. It began to spread all over his face. " Then they said it was a kind of eczema. He couldn't zrest; all he wanted to do was scratch. .I was told to try Cuticura Soap 4.nd Ointment. It only took four tablets of Cutictira Soap and I have still one-half of the second box of Cuti- cure Ointment left, and he was cured." -(Signed) Mrs. T. Rooks, July 22, 1913. Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists everywhere. A sample of each with 32-p. book post-free. Ad- dress: F. Newbery and Sons, 27, Charterhouse- square. London. or Potter Drug and Chem. Corp., L'ortou,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>Warrington Society on the</head>
        <p>The Warrington Society on the</p>
        <p>Welsh Hills.</p>
        <p>BY SILVER-STREET.</p>
        <p>One of the most -useful modifications I have ever tried in my "photographic ou'tfit -was taken from the A.P. and P.N. of last September. It is an acid fixing bath which prevents any cloudiness in either bromides or gaslight papers, or- in lantern slides. After development the print (glass or paper) is quickly rinsed in water and fixed in: Water, 200 z.; hypo, 30z.; sodium bisulphite lye,.loz. The lye is best prepared by a chemist, especially for those unaccustomed to the use of corrosive acids, and so little of it is used that the cost is not worth-saving. However, to those who still desire to make their °WTI, 4t is easily made, as follows: 6os. sodium sulphite is dissolved in 14oz. warm water, to which, when cool, is added, by very small instalmgnts, say, a dram at a time, loz. sulphuric acid. The operation should be con- ducted out of doors, as sulphurous acid fumes are thickly evolved. It is well to stand the sul- phite solution in a basin of cold water whilst the sulphuric acid is _poured in.</p>
        <p>Those who have had the annoyance of finding plates or papers spoiled in the fixing bath by the precipitation of a cloudy layer of sulphur will appreciate the value of this simple acid bath.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>TWO (NEW?) STORIES.</head>
        <p>TWO (NEW?) STORIES.</p>
        <p>The following yarns have no connection with photography further than that they both come from society meetings.</p>
        <p>A youngster was asked by the teacher during the Scripture lesson why Joseph was put in the pit? "Please, ilium-, it as because Joseph had on a coat of many colours, and it was only dress suits in the. circle." (Warrington P.S.) From the London Camera Club comes the story of a pavement artist, who was asked by a passing amateur photographer what "that" was.</p>
        <p>"That in the saucer? It's milk." "But it doesn't look a bit like a saucer of milk to me."</p>
        <p>"I know it ain't," was the reply; "and it's goin' to stop like that. I did one so like the real thing once that a beastly dog come and lapped it up." (A.P. and P.N.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>MOEL. FAMMA.'</head>
        <p>MOEL. FAMMA.'</p>
        <p>For the third year in succession Mr. Holloway on Monday led the season's first outing of the Warrington Photo. Society to this summit of the Clwydian Range. Arriving at Mold Station about 10 a.m., the seven members walked past the "Loggerheads," to within half a mile of the top, where al fresco lunch was taken. The group was photographed under the shade of the ruined Jubilee Tower, of George lll.'s reign. One does not wonder to see it in ruins, for a breeze in the valley is a hurricane up there, 1,820 feet above the sea, Tea was obtained at Cilcain, and the long-threatened rain now came down in sheets. It cleared up again with the clearing of the tea-things i and a delightful evening walk was enjoyed through the valley of the Alyn. It was there that in 182:3 Mendelssohn composed his famous song "The Rivulet." Warrington was reached by 10 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>PHOTOGRAPHERS' NOTEBOOKS.</head>
        <p>PHOTOGRAPHERS' NOTEBOOKS.</p>
        <p>I so' thoroughly agree with the sentiments of the following letter that I make no apology for lifting it bodily from "Photography and Focus." From August 21st, 1899, the day I ex- posed my first plate, I have always kept one myself, and thoroughly recommend it to others. Sir,—The concluding lines of "Spirit of the Times" are "We believe that the photographer's exposure notebook is one of • the most useless items of his equipment. I believe the exposure notebook to be of the greatest value. I should not dream of going on a photographic lioliday without one—l would .sooner leave one of my lenses behind, if not, as "the Walrus" would advise, my camera. For three weeks at a time I went away for several years on a purely photographic holiday. I carried a half-plate outfit and was "at it" all, day. The most I ever exposed in one day was twelve plates; but on that occasion I began at five o'clock in the morning and finished at nine in the evening. As a rule, on my return home from the holiday I had a hundred plates to develop. Without my notebook I could not pos- sibly remember what each plate was suppose to have hidden in its film. So I refer to the notebook and find exposure No. 1 was on a sub- ject full of contrast, of sunshine and foreground with deep shadows. I then know I must keep down the pyro and' bromide and not allow devel- opment to lag. No. 2 may have been exposed on a grey day, everything flat, so the pyro and bromide are increased and development carried on slowly. What a trade one might do in in- tensifiers and reducers were the plates developed alike.</p>
        <p>Ithaemold a Pagan—a Pagan oboe l eovien,g soi or,o ywohuo wotiilol hsoatyo religion— and that I have not been blessed with the light that has opened your eyes, that, in short, ex- posure is everything and development cannot modify it. Well, it may he so, but let me keep my exposure notebook !—Yours,</p>
        <p>W. R. BLAND.</p>
        <p>Th..</p>
        <p>..------ . ,</p>
        <p>~ BUTCOUghS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>NO POSTAL STRIKE.</head>
        <p>NO POSTAL STRIKE.</p>
        <p>Militant Proposal Defeated.</p>
        <p>The question of a strike policy was discussed at the conference of the Amalgamated Postal and Telegraph Clerks' Association, which was held in London on Tuesday. As an amendment to the defined duties of the executive it was pro- posed to add: "To secure the fullest possible share in the adTinistration of the Post Office" and "to maintain the right of and to organise the effective power of striking." , Mr. Marquess (Liverpool), moving the amend- ment, observed that they would agree with re- serving absolutely the right of striking. They must always be on the alert for any attempts to enchain them. It had been said- that they had no right to strike and that limitations shbuld be imposed on their freedom. But they had no other way of maintaining their liberties in the last resort. . -</p>
        <p>The amendment was lost by 124 votes to 99. A "card" vote was then demanded by the Liver- pool delegates, and this resulted in a declara- tion against the amendment by 219 to 162, each vote representing 50 members of the association.</p>
        <p>Resenting the attempt of the bishop at Catan- zaro, Italy, to deprive religious processions ot all forms of paganism and to restore them to Christian simplicity,-the\ peasants rushed into the cathedral and the bishop and the other priests fled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <p>•</p>
        <p>H CHILDREN'S CORNER. E CHARM OF WOMAN. Conducted by UNCLE JACK. 1 ii.~,Nstcoewootok2soo424</p>
        <p>OH! THESE HOLIDAYS! My Dear Children,— I suppose your holidays are coming to an end and that you are not sorry (?). lam sure you will be glad to be back at school again. Now I have a little story for you, which shows how nice it is to be members of the "Children's Corner."</p>
        <p>Two of my nephews went a walk into Cheshire on Easter Tnesday. They came across a number of children playing, and got talking. Then they found all but one were members of the "Children's Corner" in "The Examiner," and after that they had a jolly good time to- gether.</p>
        <p>It gives me great pleasure to record the above little incident, and I hope when members meet they will make themselves known and have a good time together. Your loving UNCLE JACK.</p>
        <p>THE AMBITIOUS BIRCH-TREE. , Once upon a time there lived in a forest a wise old oak tree; he was very tall, very spread- ing, and his trunk was larger and stronger than any in the forest; being such a magnificent tree, he was so admired by the other trees that they made him their king. Now near this tree grew a straight silver-birch sapling; he, too, was a beautiful tree, for his thin, white bark did indeed look like the silver in the sunlight; but this foolish birch had been spoiled by too much flattery from the daisies and the tall grasses that grew around him. At last he became, so vain that he thought himself better than the great oak, and he said one day to a hawthorn that grew beside him:— " Do you notice that King Oak is getting old? 1 He is not so handsome as he used to be, and I soon he will begin to rot. Now, my dear Mr. Hawthorn, I think the trees would do well to make me king in his place." But the hawthorn shook with laughter, and replied (rather rudely the birch thought) that the trees would like a king tall enough to be seen.</p>
        <p>At this the birch decided to grow as high as the oak, so he began that very morning; he I grew quite quickly, and when he saw that he was taller, he thought, "I must try to be crooked and gnarled like King Oak," so he did his best t 9 do so, until the daisies said, "Why, Mr. Birch, you are losing all your straightness! What is the matter?" At last one night there came a great storm; for two hours the thunder rolled, and every moment the lightning flashed brighter and brighter, until—crack !—the birch-tree split in two and fell. The oak felt his fall most, be- cause he fell right into the outstretched branches of the King. knocking off some of his best acorns !—" The Children's Friend."</p>
        <p>SPRING. Spring is a youthful maiden Clad in a robe of green, Her head it is crowned with snowdrops, As pretty as e'er were seen.</p>
        <p>If she finds a small withered flower. Softly she'll raise its head; " My dear little child, so sweet, I'm sorry that you are dead."</p>
        <p>She'll trip o'er the moorland wild, And dance on the flowery plain, The daisies will lift their heads, Saying, "Sweet spring has come again?' On through her season she goes, Doing kind deeds everywhere, When it's over she goes from her throne, And warm summer takes her place there.</p>
        <p>CATERPILLARS AS PETS.</p>
        <p>Very few children know what interesting pets can be made'of caterpillars. Some children, it is true, are so unfortunate as to feel, or pretend to feel, dislike towards things that crawl; but most of us like to hear about the strange lives that are lived in the insect world close under our often unseeing eyes. First of all, before we can keep any pet, we must provide a suitable home for it. When we have done this, we must then catch our cater- pillars; and this ought not to be very difficult, even although we do live in a city, if we have a garden or a common near by. One of the caterpillars most readily found is that of the gooseberry moth, and it is about this insect I am going to tell you now. Unlike most caterpillars, this one is not green in colour, bat is very vividly marked with orange, black and white. It walks about quite openly on its food-plant; and sometimes several dozen may be found on one bush. When we succeed in getting the caterpillars, we must pick some twigs of food-plant, which may be gooseberry, currant, cauliflower, leaves, or other shrubs, put them in a jam-jar with water, and put the jar itself into a' muslin cage, or simply cover the top of the jar with thin muslin or netting. We must be prepared to change the twigs and leaves very often, as cater- pillars eat very fast, and have to lay past a store of food in preparation for a great change which is soon coming over them. In time their skin gets too tight for them, and then it splits, and they crawl out of it, clad in a beautiful and many-coloured coat. But a time comes when our pets will not eat anything. When this period arrives we must not come to the conclusion that they are dying— they are, only about to change into chrysalides. They commence by walking about very worried- like, and then we understand that they are looking for a quiet spot where no one will dis- turb their solitude. Then they hang themselves up by the tail, and some of them spin threads of silk to make a cocoon. This change usuaily takes place in the dark,, so that it is not often noticed. The chrysalis skin now appears, and we see that the old one is left shrivelled up near the tail end. This chrysalis, if not disturbed, generally lies in this position for some time, and then the case bursts, and the complete goose- berry moth wriggles out. As in its caterpillar stage, its colours are very bright, and, unlike other moths, it flies in daylight, and is plainly visible. Because of this, it is often mistaken for a butterfly, but it does not fly very speedily, and is thus often caught. , This moth has two means of protection; one is by pretending to be dead, and the other is due to its very unpleasant taste, which makes it dis- liked by birds. This is the reason why they are so brightly coloured, so that any foes may in- stantly recognise and avoid them. After a time, the moth will, in all likelihood, lay eggs, and when the eggs are hatched the young caterpillars crawl out and immediately start, to eat. Thus the life history of the very interesting gooseberry moth commences again.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>Pains after Meals.</head>
        <p>Pains after Meals.</p>
        <p>If your digestive organs are in a sound and healthy condition, if they are extracting. nourishment from the food, you eat, there ought to be no sign of pain or dis- comfort. But to-day thousands are afraid to eat because of the pains that follow even a light meal of good and wholesome food. Possibly the stomach is out of order, the liver a little sluggish, or the bowels con- stipated. Get them into a state of healthy</p>
        <p>activity by taking Mother Seigel's Syrup, and you will be able to eat without an,. _ . _</p>
        <p>- painful after effects.</p>
        <p>ky Your food will be well</p>
        <p>'digested. You won't know it is being digested, and that, after all, is the best test o; good digestion—not to know about it. Thirty drops of Mother Seigel's Syrup, taken after meals, have helped tens of thous- ands to enjoy their food, and enabled them to a% old the pains and miseries al indigestion..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>Quality Which Exists But Baffles Analysis.</head>
        <p>A Quality Which Exists But Baffles Analysis.</p>
        <p>GOOD TACTFUL KIND.</p>
        <p>The charm of woman ! In what does it Consist? Its existence is undeniable but its lanalysis is apt to prove baffling. Poets from the very earliest times, when they first woke the echoes with their songs, have employed their talents in warbling about the fascina- tion of the mis-called weaker sex. They nearly all devote their rhymes either to the beauty or the character of women, rarely to their intsileot. Goethe says "We love her for her beauty, her youth, her mirth, her confidingness, her character, with its faults, caprices, and God knows what other inexpres- sible charms; but we do not love her for her understanding. Her mind we esteem (if it is ' brilliant), and it may greatly elevate her in our opinion; nay more, it may enchain us :pion we already love. But her understand- ing ie not that which awakens and inflames (y,,r passions." And Herbert Spencer, a man of a totally different type from Goethe says "Men care comparatively little for erudition in woman, but very much for physical beauty, good nature, and sound sense. How many conquests does the blue-stocking make ' through her extensive knowledge of history? What man ever fell in love with a woman be- cause she understood Italian ? Where is the Edwin who was brought to Angelina's feet by her German? But rosy cheeks and laugh- ing eyes are grqat attractions. A finely- roundcd figure draws admiring glances." The moral of the double verdict of poet and philosopher is, that the charm of a woman, as such, is not of the intellectual variety. It is a matter of external beauty and goodness of character.</p>
        <p>PHYSICAL BEA.IITt. Let us examine this question of physiol beauty a little more closely. Young girls are often told not to pride themselves on their good looks. But why not ? They are a natural gift just as much as her brain or her heart. People say that beauty is transient and will yield no lasting benefit to the posses- sor. But is there really any reason why the beautiful young should not become the beautiful old ? The truth is that there are hundreds of really fine looking old ladies. Tho preservation of the physical uttraction of a pretty face is probably due to a life of kind deeds. A person ot sour temper and disagreeable habits could never live to be eighty and still be charming. Colour leaves the face and wrinkles invade the smooth skin, but the kindness that lives in a noble heart is a lamp that illuminates the features of I many whose claim to personal beauty is not of the regulation order. It is not a question of hue or features but of a mind and spirit dominated by the unselfishness that is oftener a characteristic of women than of men. But in any case, those who are admittedly good- looking in their youth, have a fine start in the race.</p>
        <p>Parents advise their sons not to be misled by a pretty face. This is sometimes taken to be a kind of condemnation of a pair of rosy cheeks or snarkling eyes. The advice is wrong. If it be permissible to stand en- thralied before a mountain, a precipice or a waterfall; if we are to be encouraged to ad- mire beautiful works of art, whether in stone or on canvas, surely there is much more justi- fication for finding a very powerful charm in a pretty face and a shapely figure. It is true that a fine appearance and a deceitful heart are poor equipment for life but a kindly nature is none the less valuable because it is wedded to a lovely form. GOODNESS OF CHARACTER. Then there is the question of the charm that lies in personality, in character. Solo- mon tells us that the virtuous woman has a 'price above rubies. The heart of her hus- band doth safely trust in her. . . . . She will do him good and not evil, all the days of her life She openeth her mouth with wis- dom; and her tongue is the law of kindness.!' Character is made up of many factors and that of a woman, of some that are almost peculiarly her own. At one time I used to meet many men whose work took them into parts of the earth where Englishwomen were not existent. There they gradually, but unknowingly to themselves, degenerated. When they came home again they were often ashamed to meet their own relatives and friends. They knew, - once they came within the circle, of woman's influence that they had deteriorated. A 'week - in the company of educated ladies made them themselves again. No one knows the power of women of a refining influence till he has had to do without it. Awkwardness flies as they bring their words and looks to bear upon anyone. But it is perhaps the stutent who most readily understands this aspect of woman's charm. He feels his mind cleared, sees how to introduce pleasing methods into what is dry, and difficult, talks to them be- cause he wishes to be listened to, fears to . fatigue them, and acquires graces of conver- sation that he can get in no other way. Our tenderest memories are those of the women we met in our childhood and perhaps woman in our later days has a charm for us born of our sentiment for the past. The nature of woman is such that she seems al- most to have been 'lent to us to teach us how virtue may be made lovely to children, how grace and order may be spread throughout the world. A WOMAN'S TACT. A woman possesses tact, a man firmness; but the firmness of a man is often nothing else but an unreasoning obstinacy while the tact of a woman is an instinct that hardly ever fails the fortunate possessor. Tact is worth all the other qualities, is perhaps better for the purposes of life than most other qualities. Old Lord Chesterfield when he wrote the famous letters to his son was not always wise as he seemed, for he tells him to manage, please and flatter women. Flatter you may, but manage—never ! The woman whose mental endowment includes great tact can manage her admirers without much beauty, and her enemies too for the matter of that. One of a woman's charms then is her tact, not because it is tact, but becalm she knows how to use it to charm. She is a good listener. If she were not, she would have been bored out of existence by this time. She will patiently allow either young or old to our into her willing ears all their sorrows, failures and ambitions. She will not yawn while a young lover tells her of the graces of hie beloved or a philosopher discoures of the 'nebula hypothesis or the con- stitution of matter. She can be so attentive that you might think she understands, and be inclined to flatter yourself upon your powers of lucid explanation. All the time she knows you are a simpleton of the highest order but she will never shew it. If you are shy she will ask you about some subject in which she knows you to be interested and set you bub- bling like a kettle on a furious fire. We like to talk about ourselves and she lets us; hence we find her adorable.</p>
        <p>She a"cAceSpußlNsiDmpAleS AserVcvi'ceoMAreNad. 'il3r and graciously. It is better to give than to receive as we have been taught for two thou- sand years. A woman knows that, as a rule, you will love her better if you do things for her than if she does them for you, When you are active von feel heroic and virtuous and it is pleasant to feel heroic. When she waits on and ministers to you in small matters you feel a beast. So you may give her flowers and hand her a chair and she will allow you to continue in the way of well-do- ing. You give her pleasure and she wins your smiles before She condescends to show her own. She has pity and tenderness of such a high order that when we wish to speak .of a man who has been known for extreme thouThtful- uess and generosity we say, he is as kind as a Woman. Now we usualrY admire most in other people things that we do not possess ourselves and this graciousness of manner and this benevolence in action. are two of the things that make men fall at the feet of Women and Ndorship. rn woman and her cry What about the mode for votes ? In Finland, where women have votes and sit in Parliament, I have heard the young ladies say that the young men have lost their sense of chivalry and their polite- ness of manner. If this be true is it the re- sult of the intrusion of women into the sordid world of politics ? Per not. But Oliver Groldsmith was perhaps not so far wrong when he said "The modest virgin, the prudent le, or the careful matron, are much more serviteable in life than the .petticoased Oilers, • blustering heroines or virago liras!" •</p>
