      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>COMPLETE EXAMINER STORY.</head>
        <p>COMPLETE EXAMINER STORY.</p>
        <p>ondale with your respected uncle. The spring runs on apace, London is getting hot and frowsy, the playhouses are stupid—" "And there are some pretty girls, or will be, at Saxondale. Ho, ho! I know you. How many are you going to make love to there?'' "I know not. Are you going also?" I am. You have heard that we are to have some news there?" and he looked expressively at me as he spoke. "I havo, and, in spite of my lightness and frivolity, the most important part of that news is to be sent to me, just because I am so light and frivolous that no one will ever suspect me ,of being its recipient. Oh, I can toll you," I went on, "it is no bad thing to have the reputa- tion of being unfit for confidence, and yet to be the recipient of the most valuable of confidences. Not a bit, is it?" "My respected uncle will gather some strangely-assorted guests at Saxondale," said Bertram, meditatively. "He is a shrewd old tehow. staunch Jacobite as he is. They will never catch him napping; and I'll pound it that, whether Charlie comes over the water or not— and if he dpes my uncle will lend money and as- sistance-Lhes will, nevertheless, not get impli- cated."</p>
        <p>"Who are these strangely-assorted guests, though?" I asked. "Sir James.. Saseby has a reputation for having the prettiest women and the most distinguished men, at Saxo in th ndale _ summer whom it is possible to collect together. "e "Nay, never fear," laughed Bertram. "The pretty women will be there; so, too, the Clever men, the latter of whom need not be stated since you and I are to be amongst them. Them is, to Wit, your old sweetheart though 'still a young beauty, Cyilla Sneyd, Then there is Clorinda Scrope, also the Lady Hortensia Bulstrode, and r —but you will see them all soon enough." "Yet." said I, "I do not yet see the strange assortment yout speak of. Those whom you have mentioned are all of our following. We all think alike, and live in hopes of the one great thing that is to happen. This is no mixture." "No?" asked Bertram, with a careless air. "No? You do not think so? Well, neither do I myself—yet there am others. To wit, the Earl of Mountmort is coming." " Mounts-nort?" I cried. ",hat fellow That weathercock who has been of every side and party, who is politically everything by turns and nothing long. Why," continued I, hotly, "he has been Jacobite and Hanoverian half•a-dozen times already, and will be—." "He is Hanoverian now—for the time being— and he will continue to be so if Charlie is sent back to France. If the Prince stays in England my, lord. twill be one of his warmest adherents." . '4.-Faugh I But, alb the same, what is he doing, or. to dn. as a cniest in veer uncle's hoese?", "Among other things," replica Irtratu, as imperturbably as ever,•" he is going to bring his bran new wife. Perhaps be wishes to nh.ov our ..r.eart beauties that ha este stet • hanosome</p>
        <p>iiMI,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <head>------4 ALL RIGHTS PIEZRViD./ A MARKED WOMAN</head>
        <p>------4 ALL RIGHTS PIEZRViD./ A MARKED WOMAN</p>
        <p>I . ;</p>
        <p>_ you pens?_"</p>
        <p>BY JOHN BLOUNDELLE-DURTON, Author of "Serva.nts of Sin," "The Scourge of God," "The Ilisparthpa Plate," "De- nounced," "The Clash of Arms," ttc.</p>
        <p>• "This is not a love story, but it is all abou; love," which was what pretty Loberte Palle- miens used always to say when she began to nar- rate to us Pages of the Backstairs at Versatile the story of the old Comte de la Ravigote'sll affections for a girl of sixteen, and how he wedded her, and how dire trouble ensued, and how she ran away from--. But it is not Roberto's story which I am going to tell you, but one of my own in which •1 played the princi- pal part. And. there is a sort of love in it, too, of a strange kind, as you shall see if you will but read. Wherefore, now, I begin. I was a boy in those days when Robette, Attila of Honour to the French Queen, used to tell us those stories over a, •plte de becasse and a flask of tokay in her parlour, and I was also an exile, being an English yotith in the service of King Louis XV., because my father, who was a follower of the, dethroned /louse of Stuart, would not live id England. But at.the time of which I write, which was the spring of 1745, my father—fleaven rest his soul l—had gone to his account; thy mother had retired to our country scat in Surrey, and /, Claud, fifth Baron Severn. was living in London. Fortunately none e us had ever done or com- mitted sufficient treason, 86 it WCA called, to get either proscribed • or attainted, so that, up to now, both my mother and myself were in pos. session-- of comfortable enough means, and. if I could only keep a. cool head on mv shoulders and did not meddle too openly with polities, it was just as likely as not that I should be com- fortable all the rest of my days. "And I do protest," said Bertram Samby to me ono night at this period, when we sat in my rooms at Warwick-oprt by Spring Gardens, "that you will never be a worthy Jacobite so long as you are as good-looking and proper a man as you are now. Claud, mon anti, you think too much of love's dalliances and woman's love-locks and pr.tches ever to strike a blow for the good cause. And, by the way. Claud," he continued, "are you going to--well—Say, Scot- land, later, when the certain event which we know of takes place?" "/ am," I replied. "And, what is, more, I am 'and an attempt as any of going to make just as g ius to restere Charles—' "-</p>
        <p>" llsh ! I " said Bertram. "Wars havoi ears! Not so fast, not so fast. Illeanwhile, what sue going to do until the great event bup- stay</p>
        <p>a likanwhila I sm noina down to</p>
        <p>at Sas-</p>
        <p>--..,..NWiliffganni</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
        <head>EATING'S</head>
        <p>EATING'S</p>
        <p>The Return of the Tassel.</p>
        <p>Black moire bags with diamond clasps, trimmed with Valenciennes lace in narrow , bands are used in Paris. Plain leather bags are beautifully embroidered in most intricate de- signs with small jet beads, and finished with a dangling fringe. Both velvet and moire bags might be made by anyone skilled in the use of the embroidery needle or the paint brush, at very small cost, but. in the.shol?s, these -charm- ing accessories are expe'riSive. "Tastiehi hive come back again, and are used to finish all kinds of draperies, the ends of scarves, sashes, sleeves, and hoods of opera cloaks, and, of course, smart bags. They are of various lengths, from the short ones that look like ball trimmings, to the tassel of half a yard in length. The materials of which they are made are as diverse as their length, and include silk, clusters of tiny beads, and strings of seed pearls, big, coloured, wooden beads, and crystal drops as large as the old- fashioned lustres on a chandelier. Garnets, too, are employed for fashionable tassels, which it seems impossible to use amiss on any garment or accessory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>Sleeve&amp; and Yokes.</head>
        <p>Sleeve&amp; and Yokes.</p>
        <p>Sleeves are-as various as it is possible for them to be, but as far as coats are concerned, they are cut ill one with the upper part of the gar- ment, or they may be set flat into a deep wide armhole, defining the shoulder line very clearly. The lower part of the sleeve is a matter of indi- vidual taste; it may be straight, belled, or drawn in to the wrist with gathers. The yokes of some of the new shirts are their most original feature. A favourite shape is carried well over the shoulders, and is a part of the front. The sleeves and body are in one, and the only seam is under the arm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>COOKERY CORNER.</head>
        <p>COOKERY CORNER.</p>
        <p>A Surprise Dainty.</p>
        <p>Instead of the usual cake or bread and butter, try the two together in this way: Cut tile bread and butter thin, of course, but not so fragile as to cause it to break. Between each slice put a very thin slice of cake, and cut into triangles or squares. -Several of these will be eaten with enjoyment. Sliced bananas may be substituted for the cake by way of variety, and a very thin Slice of toasted bread, buttered and sprinkled with castor sugar, is very much liked, served between slices of bread and butter. In fact, there is no end to the list of 'little "surprise" dainties.</p>
        <p>One of the worst things we can do is to ask the invalid "What would you like?" Thit questiOn should never be asked. Prepare any- thing you think will be fancied, and serve it nicely. In all probability it will happen to be the very thing wished for.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>Date Pudding.</head>
        <p>Date Pudding.</p>
        <p>' • • A suet pu Ad ing in which stoned dates are used instead of sultanas, &amp;c., is a welcome change in most households For a really good pudding, use one pound of dates to four ounces of flour and four ounces of chopped suet, two ounces of sugar and four good tablespoonfuls of milk. Add a little nutmeg, and a pinch of salt. Turn into a greased basin, cover with greased paper, steam for four hours. To make a more economical pudding, allow only two ounces of suet and half a pound of flour and sufficient milk to mix to a stiff paste. Beefsteak Pie.</p>
        <p>Cut 31b. of rump steak into pieces 2in or 3in. square, trimming off any undesirable portions. Beat the meat with a chopper or mallet until it 'is very tender. Chop three onions very fine, and add } oz. of pepper and salt mixed; sprinkle a little of the chopped onion on the bottom of a baking-dish, then put in a layer of steak; then more of the onion, and so on, until the dish is I full. Add half a gill of mushroom or other 'rather mild catsup, and about the same quan- , tity of gravy; cover it, and bake two hours. 1111‘ Woomaiik *aft we, Nom *um fook _ Oh, Ann-rung. 'vast snail we say! (3ome now, money or what? In reason, of course." "Suppose," I said, " you wager that brace of spaniels his lordship brought you out of France, against a score of silver-fringed gloves from Tackett's by the Exchange in the Strand. They are the best, the ladies slay." "Doubtless you know." she laughed. "I will he bound you have had to buys-many. Yet," she added, " I love my spaniels." "And I love Cyrilla," I replied. "And I have set my heart on getting her for an hour to myself." "Well, so be it. The spaniels against a score of silver-fringed gloves from Tackett's." Then, as she turned her head away, I could have sworn I heard her whisper: "After all 'tie worth it, and he will lose." "Is it a wager?" I asked. "It is," she replied. Then her ladyship con- tinued, in a mocking tone: " Why do you not try to-night? It is the masked ball, , if Cyrilla would come to you at all, she could do so then." Now this was true. There was to be a masked ball that night, to which all the gentry of the county were invited, and, in solemn truth, it, was to be a " masked " affair in more ways than one. For it was to be used as a sort of disguise of what was a-doing, and words of import were to be spoken as to what was likely to happen in the North ere long, and as to how my Lord This and Sir Somebody That were to find themselves, by accident, in Scotland at a given moment; and so on. Though, all the same, doubtless there would be some love-making and coquetting amongst the younger members of the company. "I'll do it," I said. "I will try to-night. Though I am not bound to this night alone., If I fail, I may try again to win the spaniels? ' "You may try until his lordship and I depart from Saxondale. And I depend on your honour to tell me the truth as to whether you succeed or nat."</p>
        <p>• "You may depend," I said. So upon this we parted, I being more than ever determined now that I would induee Cy- rilla ,to meet me alone in the picture gallery to hear my prayer. Yet, it was hard to do so, and I cudgelled my brains as to what reason I could giye that would induce her to come—what rea- son beyond my love for her! Yet, when the masked ball was at its height, I was waiting in the picture gallery for the girl. For—oh, happy man !—I had sent her a fervent letter imploring her to meet me and hear what I had to say, and for answer I had received a tiny scrap of scented paper on which was written the one word, " Yes ' She had consented, there- fore she loved me! She was coming! And vim should be alone; no one else could come, and that for a very good and sufficient reason. The picture gallery had been under repair for some time, the walls had been repainted, the pictures were down. Therefore, the place was at present in disuse, and, instead of being a blaze of light, as otherwise it would have been on such a night as this, it was dark and deserted. All dark and black it was, except for a ray of light from the moon that now and again crept in through the irrn, srofrrping mdp 14, "44,110. UUL L.v WIL it Clear and tr.* tor s rtio•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <head>WO.M.A 'S WORLD.</head>
        <p>WO.M.A 'S WORLD.</p>
        <p>Next year she will join the Royal circle, and appear at the various Court functions-. The young Princess is very desirous of accompanying her brother, the Prince of Wales, on his next voyage. At present she thoroughly enjoys the riding with her brothers in the private park at Windsor, which she prefers as a'residence, next to Sandringham and Balmoral.</p>
        <p>Women Explorers, and Scientific Discoverers. Women' are almost every day adding to the world's store of geographical knowledge, and performing feats of mountain climbing that would tax the powers of endurance of the hard- est masculine travellers. There were great women explorers and travellers hundreds of years ago. Many inaccessible mountain peaks, on which no man's foot had trodden, have been successfully negotiated by women in these latter days, although it is now about one hundred years ago that Mont Blanc was scaled by the first woman alpine traveller. Mrs. Bullock Workman, who has done so much exploration work in the Himalayas, recently discovered an tin themselves espe- entire range of those wonderful mountains. Frenchwomen dis cially in scientific research, and Madame Henry has just made the remarkable discovery that under certain species of light the germ of one guish disease can be seen transforming itself into quite another. Great as is this marvel, it is only in line with indications found in other quarters of the world of matter.</p>
        <p>The Natural Waist and Soft Fabrics. Rumours are afloat that the curved waist will come back with fuller skirts, but there are at present no indications that the so-called natural waist—now accepted as the standard of fashion will be changed this season. The waist line may be, and is, movable, but there is no diminution in the size of the figure. That remains as before. Soft fabrics of every kind will be in the van of fashion, since a certain ,shapelessness is de- manded. Soft crepe de chine and cotton crepes and chiffon cloths, plain and figured, are among the first favourites. Moire and taffetas will be used for the dressy type of tailor-made, although several new materials in silk and wool run them very close. Manufacturers have been especially successful in a cotton imitation of an expensive fabric called peau-de-peche ; it is lustrous and remarkably like the material it imitates, and promises to wear as well as it looks. Cotton eponges in all the new shades will be among favoured materials as the summer advances.</p>
        <p>Tangerine for Little Girls. Various shades of orange are very fashionable this season, and a particularly bright tone has</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>Queen Mary's Dresses for Paris. . Queen Mary's preference for British fabrics, and her patronage of London dressmakers is well-known on the Continent. Consequently, Parisiennes are looking forward with much in- terest to the garments that will be worn by her Majesty on her forthcoming visit to Paris. For that occasion the Queen has ordered several mag- nificent dresses, and the criticism these will evoke across the Channel will be looked forward to by the designers and dressmakers of London. The Queen will wear superb jewels when she ap- pears in evening these, a custom More prevalent in London than in Paris, where there is no Court. Besides, • smart Frenchwomen do not consider tiaras becoming to their type of features, and rarely wear them, but to Queen Mary this kind of ornament is particularly suit- able. Jewels of all kinds she wears with a grace and becomingness peculiarly her own, and her taste in the choice of the gems she selects on special occasions is never at fault.</p>
        <p>The Coming of Age of Princess Maud. The date of Princess Maud of Fife's twenty- first birthday clashed with the political crisis, ' and the festivities that were to have honoured the occasion had in consequence to be limited to a family luncheon party in Portman-square, at which the King and Queen were present. The King is very fond of his nieces, and had intended fittingly to celebrate the coming.of age of Prin- cess Maud, and will still carry out this intention later in the season. Lord Farquhar also in- tended giving a small dance, and the Princess Royal had contemplated a dinner party. Princess Maud, although not a great heiress, is well dowered, and, like her sister, is very fond of her Scotch home, and the out-door, simple life she leads there with her Royal mother. It goes without saying that many beautiful pres- ents and numerous messages of congratulation were received by the Princess on attaining her ' majority.</p>
        <p>Princess Mary's Birthday. Another Royal birthday—that of Princess Mary— occurs this month. It seems scarcely possible that File can have reached her SOVPII- - year, for she looks two years younger.</p>
        <p>MEMS. FOR MOTHERS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
        <head>Some Uses tor I‘'aste String.</head>
        <p>Some Uses tor I‘'aste String.</p>
        <p>Don't throw odd pieces of string away. Knot them tidily together and crochet them, any stitch, into squares, six inches or larger. The squares make excellent oven clothe, dish cloths, and floor cloths, and are practically indestruc- tible.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <head>Purse Hint.</head>
        <p>A Purse Hint.</p>
        <p>Procure a brass ring to fit the second finger of your left hand. With some silk double crochet well round it. Then from the ring work four rows of chain each measuring three inches long. Secure the ends of these to the purse, sewing them firmly. When shopping slip over your finger, and jou need then have no fear of losing your purse.</p>
        <p>To Keep Lamps In Order. Some lamps always seem to burn dimly, no matter how carefully they are tended. This may be remedied by dropping small pieces of camphor into the bowl with the oil. Never fill a lamp, and do not light it when it is nearly empty, for fear of an explosion. Every winter well wash all the lamps in sodawater, and boil the burners in an old saucepan. Dry every part thoroughly by the fire before putting them together, especially the oil vessels. A few drops of water left in a lamp will rise to the top of the oil and make the lamp dicker.</p>
        <p>The best lamp oil is that whin is clew' and nearly colourless.</p>
        <p>To prevent the lamp from smoking, soak the wick in strong vinegar, and dry it well beforo you use it. It will then burn splendidly.</p>
        <p>Do not wash your lamp chimneys, as they are liable to break if they are washed. Instead. hold them in the steam of a boiling kettle for a moment, and the task of rubbing them dean with a cloth is then reduced very easy. Newspaper. too, is excellent for the purpose, and can be burnt directly afterwards.</p>
        <p>'llllik 'Wilk NWIlk VW VS" 1111" UM VW" NIS" Valk,</p>
        <p>woman tot a bride, eatnougm no is a - weattor- cock.' "</p>
        <p>------ "Is she handsome?" not "My word she is! But mighty haughty, too. Though that is natural since, you know, she is to the naanner born. My lady is of the trader' class, therefore be very sure she s vastly snore imperious than those who aro used to rank i and fashion."</p>
        <p>"But why does Sir James have them at Sax- ondale at all?"</p>
        <p>"Because," said Bertram, - • "he is a ly old for, although the best of uncles. And—and—well, if s Mountmort is there, then ho can be cited as a testimony to the fact that the party at Saxondale could not have been got together with a view to plotting treason, since, in that case, he would have been the last ; man to be asked as guest. Dos't understand, my lad?" Soon it drew to the middle of June and Sir James Saxeby's guests were all assembled at Saxondale, and, in solemn truth, they did jus- • tify Bertram's statement that they were a strangely-assorted set of people. For, now, see of what strange m6langeo we were cornposed. Firstly, there were those beauteous maidens mentioned above, arrayed as though fok slaughter of men's hearts, and wearing their laces and, frills and love-locks, and casting glances from their roguish eyes as though they had not to think of aught in this world but love and marriage. Then we, 'the men of the party, were also coquetting, and love-making, and cast- ing sheep's eyes,- and playing at bowls, and in- dulging in archery contests as if life was nothing but a long summer's dream. Here, too, was the Abbe Le Bleau from Paris, who was supposed to be a traveller in England who intended later to write a book on our reli- gious institutions and customs, and who smiled and bowed and scraped as though he was the happiest man in the world and without care. Also, our good old host seemed intent on nothing more than providing pleasure for his guests, in seeing that the horses and coaches were always in readiness for the outings, and that the tokay was properly chilled and the repasts of the very best.</p>
        <p>I say that we were all gay and bright and blithesome, as though existence was but a plea- sant fête; .yet below the surface how different it all was! For the young ladies knew well enough that a blow was about to be struck which, if it sucoeeded, would place their fathers upon a higher pinnacle than they had ever attained, and if it failed, would send them to theiblock. The Abbe, too—who was no more an Abbe than I, but, in- :mead, a most devoted follower of the dethroned house and a Scotchman Avho spoke French like an angel—if angels ever speak that tongue, which I take leave to doubt l—know what would happen to his neck if things went wrong, whits Sir James Saxehy would sometimes look most uncommon glum over his last glaze at .night when one began to weigh up the fors and the sgainsts of Prince Charlie's chances of success. • But amongst us all there moved two people who seemed to think of nothing but the way in which these long summer days might be passed most pleasurably; of how many times they could 1 lac ea. -01,1, in ii.e target or get ne.ircst the .1.,,,i, upon this bowling-green, and whether my Lady Slountrriort could whip more trout out of tte Duman in • given time than fiertram tiatet,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>HINTS ON HEALVI.</head>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALVI.</p>
        <p>To Cure a Cold.</p>
        <p>First stop eating. The system is overloaded with impurities and they must be eliminated. Past until these poisons can be disposed of in a natural manner. Take long walks, drawing in many deep, full breaths, exercise every muscle of the body' that the circula.tion may be quick- ened, and each part of the' body theroUghly cleansed by this accelerated circulation. Bathe at least once a day, rubbing the surface of the body briskly all over for five or ten minutes. After missing from two or three meals, if a ravenous appetite is acquini it is, of course, desirable to indulge this appetite, but in moder- ation (says the "Medical Press"). Under no cir- cumstances should the stomach be gorged, and those foods which are unwhol.3seme, or but moderately nutritious should be avoided.— ' "Health Record."</p>
        <p>A Scrubbing Cure. Writing of the strain imposed on prominent stage people by the exacting work of their pro- fession, Clara Kellogg, the retired American prima donna, says that before a performance she was always in an excited condition before the curtain rose and could never become com- fortably limp in preparation for the demands of the active hours. "I was nearly as bad," she continues, "as the Viennese prima donna, Marie Willt. The story is told of her that a reporter from a Vienna newspaper went to interview her the afternoon before she was to !nag in Trovatore' at the Royal Opera, and inquired of the scrubwoman in the hall where he could find Frau Willt. 'Here,' responded the scrubwoman, sitting up to eye him calmly. hen the young man expressed surprise and incredulity, she explained, as she continued to mop the soapy water, that she invariably scrubbed the floor the day she was going to Ring. 'lt keeps me busy,' she concluded, senten- tiously."—" Health Record."</p>
