        <p>NOM b.</p>
        <p>Well. Perhaps they had reason to be at peace. For Mounttnort was deeply sworn now to King George and his dynasty; and since all of that Bide thought that Charles Stuart had not the thousandth Part of a chance of ever repossessing himself of the throne of his ancestors, it was not atrange that he should be so calm and undis- turbed. On the other hand WO, who, ELS you might say, were setting our lives upon a cast, were by no means at ease. As for me, I had other things, which were far removed from politics, to disturb my mind— things which I do assure you can make the bear beat quite as fast as anyt. impending change oz throne or Government, and cause equally es.easy and restless nights. For I was in love, as always, and badly, too, who was the haughtiest of 411 the Young be.auties at Saxondale; while, for with Cyrilla Sneyd, 1 some reason, Cyrilla seemed by no means dis- posed to give me the opportunity of telling her so. Once I had asked her to meet me in the I Lime Walk at dusk after supper, when we gene- -1 rally strolled about in couples, since 'twas too hot to be indoors and she had refused point- blank;. and on another occasion I had asked her to come and see the pictures in the long gallery, but 'twas always the same. She would not do se, being an extremely cautious and dignified damsel. - Whereat I used to get as mad as though a P golden curls, hornet had stung me, which only served to make my Lady llountrnort laugh—she was a winsome, giglot thing, and too good, we all thought, for her lord—and shake her pretty which were the prettiest of any at Saxondale excepting Cyrilla's, they being as golden as hers. For her ladyship generally happened by some strange circumstance or other always to be in the way when I was pleading with the girl for a quiet moment; while, if I made my request to Cyrilla in the leaf-arched grove, it was a cer- tainty that my. lady happened to be close by; or, twain, if I whispered the same thing in the girl's ea win a we were in the summer-house by the fislt-pond iadame la Comtesse was sure to be imt behind the bower and coming towards that pond with her hands full of crumbs for the carp. "It is of no avail, my ,lord," she used to say, after Cyrilla had made her escape, while her beauteous. face would be all suffused With blushes. "Cyrilla is not for you. I vow she do'n`.Shkedoloevsesnmote_—alslckinnogw yito,uornlyladzeshii loves someone else." sp'sa liptatlre coy. Yet I shall win her." "Would you wager?" asked her ladyship. "A small wager, say?" "Wages about what?" "That she will meet you in—well, in the pic- ture gallery."</p>
