      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>WORK-A-DAY WONDERS.</head>
        <p>WORK-A-DAY WONDERS.</p>
        <p>L-TELth ITOW A CLEVER WOMAN UNDECEIVED THE NEIGHBOURS.</p>
        <p>"Yes," said Patty, "I've always been fond of experimenting. Directly I hear tell of a likely thing I want to try it—and that's how I came to use Diamond Dyes. "1 had been disappointed ever so many times )afore, and had come to the conclusion that the ray that looks TOO easy in the beginning gener- illy turns out pretty hard in the end. So when read that if you want to dye a woollen coat 7ou must use a proper silk-and-wool Diamond i)ye, and when you want to dye a cotton or linen dress you must use a cotton Diamond Dye, then I said to myself: Now, Patty, get on with your sxperiments. Take the same serge skiik that you spoilt with the one-tablet-for-everything dye and do it all over again, and if it comes out like new —show it to the neighbours! "I got down the poor patchy-dyed skirt and looked at it; then I went out to the stored and bought a sixpenny packet of Diamond Dye for wool. They gave me a first-class instruction book with pictures and full directions, and I followed the rules to a T. I got my skirt dyed a most lovely deep blue, and when it was dried and pressed I asked the neighbours to come and inspect it inside and out—so you may guess how it looked! "They had had the laugh at me before, but the tables are turned now and no mistake, for Mrs. Finch—the 'most particular lady in our street—has asked me if I will kindly Diamond- dye her best silk blouse!" (To be continued.)</p>
        <p>/YE CLEANLY TO USA Dir,ction Book Fres</p>
        <p>,NiiM</p>
        <p>Nollk</p>
        <p>vipb, `10111),V11111 NUM NNW NNW *llk Nolilk Imagine my delight when at such an instant I saw down at the further end of the room a sha- dow darker than the room itself; a figure stand- ing there hooded and masked, the long dark cloak enveloping all beneath, so that the pink and orange dress which I knew my sweetheart wore was quite covered up and obscured. She was there! She had come! I could tell her all! Yet she was mighty coy an timid, I found, when I approached her. She would not advance further into the room nor would she be seated, while her whispers were so low that I could scarcely distinguish the tones of her voice, and as for an embrace, a kiss—my word! it was not to be thought of. "I would not have come on any pretence whatever," I heard her whisper, her voice being quite muffled by the cloak sho held to her face, but that I know you go so soon to the North. Oh, Claud, when do you go to join him?" "Go to join whom?" I asked, astonished. For that, Cyrilla Sneyd should not know the exact day and week when "Mr. Scott "—as we called tinn—wae expected, seemed to me beyond belief. "You know as well aa I," she said. "Tell me," she whispered again. "Tell me when does he come?"</p>
        <p>I was standing by her as she asked the ques- tion, and, perhaps so that I might the more easily bend over her, I had rested my left hand against the wall, the palm outstretched against it. And now, as she spoke, asking the 50 strange a question—for strange it was when pro- ceeding from the mouth of the daughter of :-43 staunch an old Jacobite as Sir Henry SneSd• who knew all--I bent down closer to her to ob- serve, if it were possible her features. While as I did so, I—half lover-like, and perhaps, even in such circumstances, in a too familiar way:— placed my hand on her shoulder as I said, quietly:</p>
        <p>_ "Cyrilla, we meet here to talk of love: Come, why do you not, shekv me your pretty face? You know it is the fairest sight on earth to me. Come, I say, let Inc see it: unmask and let thai cloak fall. -Come," and as I spoke I endeavoto,d to remove her vizard with one hand and to loosen the folds of the cloak with the other.</p>
        <p>But she was as slippery as ?ri eel, and wriggled about so that, as I could not hall licr without hurting her, I had perforce to let her go, when, like a lightning's flash, she had, gained the in a moment and was out of the room.. Yet, in the instant that she turned and fled, the ntoen burPt out in all her glory, and, in .the light which streamed tl,irough the window, I saw that upon the shoulder of her velvet cloak there wio, imprinted the mark of a man's haud—the palm and fingers being plain and distinct— the mark of my hand, which had become whiteited by con- tact with the freshly-cleaned wall (it not having yet received its final coating), and so had, next, made its impress on the velvet.</p>
        <p>And silo did not know it. That was very cer- tain!</p>
