        <p>- _1).)</p>
        <p>By 4 4 Scrum.”</p>
        <p>repetition of the first period, only with the difference that Warrington did score. Waywell, who all through played the best game of the Warrington quartet, found in the end his plucky work rewarded with a clever try. Nicholag con- verted, and he also kicked two good goals on his own. Taylor kicked a penalty goal given against Warrington for obstruction. A most disappoint- ing game resulted in a win for Widnes by 17 points to seven. The Warrington side only gave a mediocre display. Waywell was undoubtedly the smartest mr,n in the ruck. Nicholas was very useful, but Young did not seem up to pastform. Jolley was not so reliable as usual. In the forward line George Thomas, Clare and Skelhorne were more in evidence.</p>
        <p>141. 4 biatri,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <head>DEFEAT AT HULL. %Varrington Fail in the " Strike " Match</head>
        <p>THE DEFEAT AT HULL. %Varrington Fail in the " Strike " Match</p>
        <p>Warrington proved equally as disappointing at Hull on Easter Monday. They were palpably outclassed by the Kingston Rovers, and the times they did anything really clever could al- most have been counted upon the fingers of one hand. They looked stale, and this impression was confirmed by their lifeless display all through the game. As an additional incentive the Hospital Cup and medals were competed for, yet this did not put the desired life into the match. Soon after the start a kick by Jolley was charged down and a score ensued. Other points were gained by Kingston Rovers in the second half, and they deserved their victory of 14 points to 'nil. War- rington played one of the worst games of the season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
        <head>LAST MATCH OF THE SEASON.</head>
        <p>LAST MATCH OF THE SEASON.</p>
        <p>The Visit of Hull Kingston Hovers.</p>
        <p>Hull Kingston Rovers, who defeated War- rington at Hull on Monday, come to Wilders- pool to-day and will thus close the season for Warrington. The local team are hopeful of avenging the Easter Monday defeat. The home side is practically the same as did duty at Hull, with the exception that Dainteth substitutes Tranter in the threequarter line. The War- ringt9n side selected is as follows: Jolley; waywell, Brookes, Dainteth and Baker; Nicholas and Young; skeihorne, Cot, ' Fearnley, Thomas, Clare and Naylor. The kick-off is at 3.30. Hull Kingston Rovers will play: Carmichael; nissey, Brain, Oliver, and Lord;: McGeiver, Clarke, or Broxholme; Mann, Moore, Sandham, Hoskin, Trump, Boylen, or, McDonald.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <head>WARRINGTON " A's " HOLIDAY</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON " A's " HOLIDAY</p>
        <p>MATCHES.</p>
        <p>Two Games on One Day.</p>
        <p>The "A "men had a hard week-end. On Easter Monday they played two matches and won them. In the Lancashire Shield semi-final iit ' Wilderspool on Saturday Warrington defeated Widnes by five points to nil. On Mon- day in the League they defeated Widnes again, by 14 points to nil, and in the afternoon they I secured a comfortable win over Runcorn, watrrington !`A." to-day play Wigan Ifighfield- at Wigan, and the team will be: 'Bramhall i Davis, Steele, Cherrett and Shaw; Douglas and 1 Jordan; Green, Davies, Boardman, Critchley, Harmer, Pouncey, or Broadhurst.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0241" n="art0241">
        <head>ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>LOCAL PRIZE-WINNERS.</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL AND SPEED.</p>
        <p>• . -- - The track season opened on Good Friday, but very few locale competed. At Liverpool W. Bol- ton won his half-mile heat in convincing style, but had to be content with second prize in the final, wherein, although running over a dozen yards faster, he failed by a yard to catch a big upstanding runner, who ran from the limit mark.</p>
        <p>In cloee finislies4, A. Willeock took second in thel2o and,third in•the 100 yards, after cleverly winning the heats and second rOund.</p>
        <p>• On Saturday and Monday Willcock was the only competitor from Warrington, and be an- nexed third prize in the 120 yards at 'Salford Harriers' sports, after winning a good heat and a fast second round.</p>
        <p>On Monday, at New Brighton, the same run- ner experienced hard lines in taking only fourth prizes in the 100 and 220 yards; thus Willcock ran in five events and won a prize in each—one advantage of being fit early. Football calls for speed, but evidently does not produce it; this Sid Grounds found out on Good Friday, when he proved to he much slower than last season. .</p>
        <p>Local junior footballers would experience less misdirected effort and find improved speed an asset for the 1914-15 season if suitable track run- ning was indulged in during the coming summer,. aln&amp;the ambitious would do well to communicate early with the honorary secretary of the Athletic Club, Mr. R. Bennett, 194, Gorsey-lane, with a view to beginning easy exercises and gentle track work immediately the present football season closes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0242" n="art0242">
        <head>BILLIARDS.</head>
        <p>BILLIARDS.</p>
        <p>SENIOR CONSERVATIVES WIN THE CHAMPIONSHIP. Exciting Finish With the Juniors. A large and enthusiastic gathering assembled at the Libel;al Club on Thursday evening to witness the deciding game for the League cham- pionship between the Senior and Junior Con- servatives. The Seniors won an exciting match by '46, and this enabled them to hold the "Harold Smith" Cup for another twelve months. Scores: Seniors. Juniors. Scratch Handicap 76 (7. Marshall T. J. Lawrence R. J. Ellis W. Ford ............... 100 11. Bloomfield 11. Bennett . ........... 100 T. A. Gorton H. Parkes ............ 100 W. Ray R. Straw ............... 28 J. Jolley T. Horrabin (eapt.) 100 J. Bate (eapt.) 34</p>
        <p>724 678 Senior Conservatives won by 46.</p>
        <p>010, AND !10; ELAGE 4,6 -oug.as, 'nudge, 8.5. A., Sunbeam, and Bradbury Motor Cycles. Agent:—W. KNIGHT Stockton Heath Garage, 63, 109, LONDON ROAD.</p>
        <p>Itinhunt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0243" n="art0243">
        <head>FIELD OF S "DORT.</head>
        <p>THE FIELD OF S "DORT.</p>
        <p>Photograph by Burrell.</p>
        <p>MR. W. BAILEY,</p>
        <p>Orford-street, Warrington, and the pike he took from the/ Gowy. The pike, weighing 111 b. lloz. &amp;Ir., and measuring 38in. in length and 16in. in girth, is the record fish taken from the Gowy. It is also the record for the Northern Anglers' Association, under whose auspices Mr. Bailey fishes. The fish is to be "set up" by Mr. George Ilamblet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0244" n="art0244">
        <head>NORTHERN ANGLING NOTES.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN ANGLING NOTES.</p>
        <p>As our canals are closed until June 16th, mem- bers who follow the sport during close season made the journey to Mickle Trafford, and although no great bags were taken, some had fair•sport with trout, pike and eels. The last- named are not yet on full go, but with this fine . _</p>
        <p>weather they Will be more plentiful this week- end. Messrs. Bailey, Ifewitt, Fallon, Stout and Potter were responsible for the best catches of trout, pike and eels. W. Bailey's pike is the finest taken from the Gowy by any Northern- Angler, and is the record, its weight being 111 b. lloz. Bdr., length 38in., girth 16m. As will be noted by its length, it is a fine-fed fish, and had it been caught about a, month earlier, according to Mr. G. Hamblett (who has it in hand for setting up in the club- house), one of our best authorities on these matters, it would have been at least 141 b., as it is a female fish, and has gone through the course of spawning, Mr. Hamblett declares it is the best shaped pike he has had for years. W. Sanson's, of 1913, was 37in, long and weighed 91b. 60z., so the lin. in 'difference makes the weight almost 211 b. That will give anglers an idea what a fine fish it is. Mr. Potter was very unfortunate to lose a very fine trout, after getting a couple of runs out of him, and had he got it safely to land it would have taken a deal oi-beating from the,Gowy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0245" n="art0245">
        <head>MR. BAILEY'S FINE RECORD.</head>
        <p>MR. BAILEY'S FINE RECORD.</p>
        <p>-•--- - --- Mr. W. Bailey started his angling almost as soon as he left school. He has been a member of the Northern since its formation, and so en- thusiastic was he on becoming a member that he waited outside the club-house to ask one of the members if he could join. He was not old enough at the time to go into a public-house. He has been angling about sixteen years, and has upwards of forty prizes to his credit, be- sides medals and specials from the set board. After winning several minor prizes, his first great success was when he carried off first in the N.A. annual match, winning the Mather Cup and gold medal. He has had five firsts at head- q• uarters The other notable wins are two seconds, third and fourth at the Roan Cow, and three seconds at the Golden Ball Club. His favourite stream is the Dee, at Chester, and along with his colleague, J. Bryers,.has carried off every set in two clubs in one season: Last year lie visited Killikenny, Caventown, Ireland, where he 'caught half a dozen pike in one day, besides some sensational bream and trout. These lie would not have recorded, because be said it was not fair to the members in general, as they could not all get to such places. Since his friend, W. Samon, took the pike 0f.91b. Gaz. last year, it has been his ambition to have the record from the Gowy, and success has. followed his efforts, to the delight of all members who know him, and the members are getting up a sub- scription to have it mounted for the club at headquarters. He has been on the committee for about eight years, and last year served on the Bay Horse Committee. He was asked to serve as assistant to the general secretary -last year, but refused. This year he was approached again and accepted office.</p>
        <p>He is a believer in fishing in the lightest pos- sible manner in matches, sometimes fishing with- out shot, having his tackle made to his own instructions; is always a willing helper of the novice, and a worker of value to any club. That he may be in harness many years and bring more specimens like his latest capture is the wish of all his clribmates.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0246" n="art0246">
        <head>NOTICE.</head>
        <p>NOTICE.</p>
        <p>Clnb night at Gladstone Arms, Monday next,. April 20th, at 8 p.m. Members must be in benefit to weigh, for specials. A.S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0247" n="art0247">
        <head>ASSOCIATION TEAMS.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION TEAMS.</p>
        <p>Orford St. John's play Emmanuel Old Boys, at Stockton Heath ground, West-avenue, in the semi-final of the Medial Competition. 'rho St John's team is: J. Forber; W. Smith and W. Millward ; J. Denny, W. Stretford and L. Ather- ton; T. Tickle, T. Scarisbriek, A. Meachin, S Hankinson and W. Smalley. Emmanuel Institute meet Orford Juniors, at Victoria Park, with: Knight; S. Hughes and F. Hughes; Banner, A. Hughes and Ayres; Walter Eaton, Oakes, Roberts, T. Hughes and W. Eaton.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>The death occurred at Northwich of Mr. Samuel Slater Mellor, aged 57, for thirty years manager of the NorthArich Gas Company.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0248" n="art0248">
        <head>By di Linesman.”</head>
        <p>By di Linesman.”</p>
        <p>In the Warrington Minor Medal Competition the first of the semi-final ties was decided last Saturday bn the Orford St. John's ground, when St. Barnabas' and Longford United were in The eof the Longford United Club, in wieaeresfouulgthtisinflaattkeereinngantdo th nChdhad to acknowledge defeat from run the St. Barnabas' teamtfothlhiaepe friendly tl;yoliP:oynasrp. sie-roTreiasrt aaagtnha ndtl eet tal end of 90 minutes' play. aeshire League the Crosfield's Vernon had the honour of scoring the only goal good goal it was • Bowdon Vale on Saturday by two goals to one, and were very unfortunate in doing so, as on Recreationo f tnhethme aNt ctoher three occasions very confident appeals. .were made for penalties, but the referee's decisions were against them. On sEaster Monday Crosfield's and Latchford met in their return League fixture. Neither side were fully represented, and in the en,l the Latchford team were successful by two goals, to none. During the Match the Altrincham Amateur Cup was on view and was greatly ad- ' mired by the spectators. Stockton Heath and Orford St. John's met on the West-avenue ground on Easter Monday in a friendly match, the proceeds of which are to be • ,en to the funds of the Stockton Heath Nurs. ing Association. There was a good crowd present and a nice game was witnessed, and the receipts at the gate and from the sale of tickets are greatly in advance of the amount obtained at a similar match lasfseason. Emmanuel Old Boys met St. Barnabas' team in .Victoria Park on Easter Monday, and, after having the best of the game, ultimately won by three goals to none.</p>
        <p>retained their lead, at half-time the score being one 'goal to nil in their favour. On resuming, the visitors were quickly on the aggressive. Millington saved well and was well supported by Monks. The home team conceded a corner, which was well taken by Tickle, and Stretford scored a grand goal. The home sick, now •transferred play to the opposite end, but offside against Appleton sent them back. How- ever, they would not give up, and Osborne beat several opponents and passed out to Bates, who had not much difficulty in scoring. This success to, the home players encouraged them to play more keenly for the equaliser, but the St. John's defence was on the alert, and after ,even play in mid-field Tickle registered a third goal foi the visitors, the result being: Orford St. John's, , three goals; Fairfield, one goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0249" n="art0249">
        <head>NORTH tHESHIRE LEAGUE. , Crosfield's V. Bowdon Vale.</head>
        <p>NORTH tHESHIRE LEAGUE. , Crosfield's V. Bowdon Vale.</p>
        <p>The above teams met last Saturday on the Thelwall-lane enclosure in their return League fixture. The first portion was keenly contested, the teams being very evenly matched, and the defence on both sides stood out more prominent than the attack, and at the interval the Vale were leading by one goal to nil. On resuming, the Soap Works' representatives played with more dash and kept the Vale defence busy, and they were unfortunate in not gaining the referee's decision on three occasions for penalty kicks. The Vale representatives wero more fortunate and increased their lead, and ultimately ran out winners by two goals to one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0250" n="art0250">
        <head>Crosfield's V. Latchford.</head>
        <p>Crosfield's V. Latchford.</p>
        <p>The above teams met on the Thelwall-lane ground on Easter Monday in their return League fixture, before a fair number of spectators. N Y represented. In the firsttmiinuctlueb was fully llaftuchford team gained an .un- productive corner, which Was quickly followed by another. From the goal-kick the Crosfield's forwards gained ground, but were sent back by Robinson .making a. good return, • anthSiddall sent in a stinging shot which was well cleared, and Crosfield's had another narrow escape from a good shot by Maddock. At this stage Latch.' ford. were having the best of the game. and were awarded a penalty, from which P. Webb opened the score. The Cros-field's forwards now im- proved, but Whitfield failed to get through, and them the. Latchford' forwards .carne-again; but failed to accept several easy chances, Crosfield'sl goal having a narrow escape from being cap- tured, Rawlinson falling back and kicking away on the goal-line. Half-time arrived with Latch- ford leading by one goal to nil.</p>
        <p>On re-starting, the exchanges were very even for a while, and then the Latchford team gained the ,upper hand and kept Crostield's in their own' half, but the forwards failed at many easy chances, P. Webb registering the second goal with a grand shot. From the centre-kick Cros- field's applied the pressure on the Latchford goal, but Green was not troubled owing to good defensive play by Robinson and Downs. Whit- field was the most prominent of, the Crosfield'E: forwards, but did not receive much assistance from his colleagues, and after a visit to Cros- field's goal the Latchford citadel was nearly captured close on time. The whistle sounded without any further score being made, the 'result being: Latchford, two goals; Crosfield's, nil.</p>
        <p>ground at WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT MINOR MEDAL COMPETITION. math.ock, Glover, Semi-final Tie. St. Barnabas' v. Lonspgtifa.oyr jdeo:;;lnteded. out :...._ This semi-final tie between the above teams Lwoansgplanyee.d oTnhethfeololorwfoinrdg St. Barnabas'—Long; Spruce awl -Ong; Apple_ ton, Challinor and Forster; Shuttleworth, Vernon and Bevan. Longford United—Holloway; Mosley and Holding; Leo, H. Finch and Sherratt; Houghton, A. Finch, Brown, Helsby, and Barton. Linesmen, Messrs. Rule and Whittaker; ,referee, Mr. J. Smith. Brown set the ball rolling and the opening exchanges were evenly contested. Eventually , St. Barnabas' were the first to become danger- ous. The United defence held out very well when hard pressed, Holloway calving nice], on ~ several occasions, whilst St. llarmi,s- e'rpnit several good openings by getting into offside positions. Owing to Shuttleworth shooting high over the bar from the penalty mark tim -United defence got relief, and their fon, ra:..la transferred the play to the opposite end and gained several 'corner-kiCks. On one occasion St. Barnabas' were decidedly lucky to save thzda citadel. Then by good mid-field play the United goal was again attacked. Vernon, after sending in several good, long shots, was successful in defeating Holloway from 20 yards' range. This , success was ' followed by St. Barnabas' having ' the best of. play, their work in mid-field being very good, while they finished badly in front of goal. Half-time arrived with St. Barnabas' leading by one goal to nil. The second portion opened in a more spirited fashion. The United showed up hest and threatened the St. Barnabas' goal, Long being quickly called upon, and he was ably supported by his brother and Spruce at full-back. The United were gradually forced bucx to their own end, Challinor doing good work at centre-half, and Vernon and Bevan showing up well on the left. Holloway on one occasion made a splendid save when he appeared to be wvll beaten, and c Shuttleworth made a good attempt to inere.. the lead, When Holloway again saved well. The e United forwards now appeared to take a new lease, and made tracks for the St. Barna:bus' gpueunle,trbautet,thtreydeafsentchee. s y woouflud;tl tirli?Y ould not scoring was done, and at tile call of time St. Barnabas' were just winners of a close game. Result; St. Barna- I has', one goal; Longford United, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0251" n="art0251">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE Second Division. Fairfield v. Orford St. John's.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE Second Division. Fairfield v. Orford St. John's.</p>
        <p>The above met on Saturday to play the return fixture with the following players :--Fairfieiti - Millington; Webb and Monks; Hallows, Osborne and Jackson; Day, C. Taylor, W. fay:or, Api:le- ton and Bates. Orford St. John's—I. Forber. Smith and Harrison; Denny, Stretford and J. Smith;. Tickle, Meachin, Millward, tlankinson, and R. Millward. Referee, Sir. B. Howell. Taylor kicking off, good play by Appleton and Bates gained ground in the visitors' half, but Harrison cleared in fine style. The homesters were awarded a free kick in the visitors' half, but failed to utilise their chances, and Denny made a pass to Tickle, who centred well across the goal-mouth, Mills-ard putting the ball be- tween the posts. • The home team now played vigorously to get on equal terms, but Denny and Stretford made a fine defence, and the visitors</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0252" n="art0252">
        <head>ii■••</head>
        <p>ii■••</p>
        <p>Emmanuel Old Boys v. St. Barnabas'.</p>
        <p>On Easter Monday. Emmanuel at once be- came aggressive, Brooks scoring their first goal after seven minutes' play. Occasionally the St. Barnabas' forwards broke away, but the Emmanuel defenders easily held the opposition in check. From an attack on the Old Boys' right a corner was conceded, from which Brooks scored a second goal, which lead they maintained at the interval.</p>
        <p>The game was more evenly contested, and even play ensued. At length the home forwards gained the upper hand, and, owing to slackness on the part of St. Barnabas' defenders, Wain-, wright increased the lead for the home team. Following this reverse, the visitors' forwards played more keenly, and on several occasions were very near to capturing the Old Boys' cita- del, hut the defence managed to hold out, and at the call of time the result- was: Emmanuel</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0253" n="art0253">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath v. Grappenhall St. Wilfrid's. The above teams met on Saturday in • a friendly encounter on the West-avenue ground. The home club could only field a,scratch team, while the visitors were well represented. The visitors won the toss, and following a very even and well-contested first half they were leading at the interval by one goal to nil. On resuming, the home team had the advan- tage of the wind and had most of the game, and although both teams had many chances of tak- ing the lead, When the final whistle was blown the game Was a draw, each side having scored two goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0254" n="art0254">
        <head>Greening's v. Monks, Hall and Co.</head>
        <p>Greening's v. Monks, Hall and Co.</p>
        <p>On Easter Monday morning, on the Thelwan. lane ground. Greening's had the best of thc exchanges during the first portion; and at half- time they were leading by one goal to nil. .In.j the.second half the Mooks,. Hall team Played .a. greatly improved , gaine, and they equalised:. This success stirred them- to *make better efforts and the game was keenly con- tested. • Result: Monks, Hall,' "three goals; Greening's, one goal.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath v. Orford St. John's.</p>
        <p>On Easter Monday morning, at West-avenue; before a good concourse of spectators. The Proceeds of the mat&amp; and the income from the sale of tickets were devoted to the Stockton Heath Nursing Association Funds. The follow- ing players lined up: Stockton Heath—Harri- son; Sankey and P. Gosling; West, Mortimer, and C. Gosling; Vernon, Smith, Jones, Gerrard. and Lloyd. • Orford St. John's—Horrocks; U Smith and Mißivard; Denny, Stretford, and Atherton; Tickle, Meachin, Scarisbrick, Han. kinson, and Smalley. Referee, Mr. S. C. Essen- high. The visitors were the first to become dangerous, but were driven back, owing to off. side against Smalley. The "Heathens" now retaliated, and their forwards failed in front of goal, andwplay continued even in mid-field The visitors gradually worked their way into the home half and Hankinson succeeded in netting the ball. The homesters now played more keenly, but were met with a stubborn defence, and following a series of even ex- changes both sets of forwards missed chances of scoring. Half-time arrived with the visitors lead- ing by one goal to nil.</p>
        <p>- _ Both teams put more vigour into the gams and a good exhibition was witnessed, St. John's being the first to attack, but the home defence was sound, and following good play by the home half-back line an attack was made on the St. John's citadel, and Horrocks made several smart saves in goal. End-to-end play followed, the defend on both sides proving too good for the attack and no further score was recorded. Re- suit: Orford St. John's, one goal; Stockton Heath, nil.</p>
        <p>Padgate United v. Pearson.and Knowles, D.O. This match, which is one of the most attrac- tive on the Padgate United card, wa-s played at Padgate, before a fair number of spectators. Padgate kicked off and made an early attack on the I'. 4.nd K. goal, but were repulsed. Good passing by the visitoraenabled them to get near to the home goal, but their final effort was weak. They came again, however, and almost caught the home defence napping, but Warburton cleared his lines well. The visitors were mak- ing full use of the wind and were exerting pres- sure on the Padgate defence, which, however, proved equal to all emergencies. Padgate now began to find their feet, and bombarded the P. and K. goal, but were unable to score. End- to-end play followed, but neither side could find the net, and after a spell of mid-field play half- time was called with the score sheet blank. Padgate, now having the advantage of the wind, made play on the left, but a splendid centre was made poor use of. Padgate con- tinued to attack, but did not trouble the P. and K. custodian much, as he was well protected by his hacks. The visitors now had a spell of attack and put two splendid shots in, hut Nork, in the home goal, was in good form. Pearson and Knowles' forwards wera showing splendid combination, and if their shooting had been More acdurate must have opened the score, al- though Partridge had hard lines with a good shot. The visitors were not finding the wind as great a handicap as the home team had done, and but for Sankey and Warburton, whc defended splendidly, must have penetrated the home defence. Padgate broke away, and filth right wing, which had been the most dangerous Part of the team, made good progress, and Hod- kinson's perfect centre was well met by Hard- man, who, taking the ball on the run, banged it into the net, thus opening the score. This suc- cess encouraged the homesters, and the P. and K. goalkeeper was fortunate to save, which he ! did at the expense of a corner, which proved abortive. The game, which had been played at a good pace, continued to be splendidly fought out, fouls being •very rare. The visitors had hard lines in not equalising, but were met by a good defence, and were attacking when the whistle blew for time, with Padgate United winners of a hard-fought game by one goal to nil.</p>
        <p>Padgate v. Latchford Baptists. On Easter Mohday Padgate entertained Latch- ford Baptists, and as holidays robbed them of several of the regular players they did well to make a draw. In doing this they were very for- tunate, as the Baptists were much the better team, and lead at one•time by two goals, but towards the end Padgate re-arranged their team and managed to equalise. The scorers for the home team were Buckley (2) and llodkinson.</p>
      </div>
