      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0256" n="art0256">
        <head>Opening of the Bowling Season Promises Well.</head>
        <p>Opening of the Bowling Season Promises Well.</p>
        <p>CROWN GREEN HANDICAP.</p>
        <p>By 'Thumb Bias."</p>
        <p>Easter has now passed and we can look for- ward with a degree of certainty to pleasant sunny days, with long, cool evenings. There is a diversity of ways as to how best the leisure time can be spent, but those whose minds turn to sport think of the bowling green, the cricket pitch, the tennis court. boating, &amp;c., and at this time of the year bring forth their various in- struments of "war" and prepare. In these parts bowling is a favourite pastime of the working men, and already the greens, which have opened in the town, and are look- ing at their • best, have been exceedingly well patronised, for the players in the various com- petitions are anxious to get " fit," so that they will be able to give a good account of them- selves.</p>
        <p>WELL CATERED FOR. Lovers of this sport in Warrington are catered for in a manner which workers in other towns, quite justly, envy. In a very short time the different 'cotupetitioru3, mains, &amp;c., will- be in full swing, and the greens will be swarmed with these good-humoured' " wood chasers," who are so whole-hearted in their sport as well as in their work. And the former has a. direct bear- ing on the Tatter. The men learn much from their sport. If they win they are exultant, and if they lose they are quite ready and willing to shake tip hands of their opponents and compli- ment them on their success, although " We hOld hard luck" is a favourite expression. So in their sport they learn tO overcome difficulties in their work, and are pleased with their success, whilst on the other hand rebuffs are incentives to go forward and try again. Then again the fresh air and exercise all tend to make the men more physically fit, and therefore the work they do is far better. And the majority of the em- ployers in Warrington do their best to encour- age the men in this and other directions of sport. Judging from the reports of the various clubs, everything augurs well for the present season, and every energetic secretary is anxious to be able to characterise the doings of his particular association or club as "highly successful" when the end of the season arrives.</p>
        <p>LIBERAL CLUB GREEN RE-OPENING. The advent of improved weather has so bene-</p>
        <p>fited the green at the Liberal Club that—it—Will be opened for the season on Saturday. Mr. Clay has given the turf much attention, and the season will be started on a green of superior quality to that experienced for some seasons. The turf has not only been improved. but the bare patches have been restored, and the green looks in the " pink " of condition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0257" n="art0257">
        <head>WARRINGTON MEN IN CROWN GREEN HANDICAP.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MEN IN CROWN GREEN HANDICAP.</p>
        <p>Several Warrington bowlers have taken part in the British Crown Green Association Easter handicap on the,Park.itel green at'Birkriale,: Southport, and. two well-known local pg.yers are in the finals, which will be played at Birk- dale to-day: Only 29 players nbiv. remain but of the 240 original entries, and these are as fol- lows :—Southport area, fi; Liverpool and dis- trict, 9; Wigan. 4;., Earle*town and Newton-le- -Willows, 4; Wirrington, 2; Manchester, 1; and Shrewsbury, 1. Play starts at eleven o'clock, and the draw is:—</p>
        <p>G. Walker, Wigan Ld. (4) v. J. H. Wainwright, Stanley (5). R. Wilson, Earlestown (4-) v. IL S. Bridge, Park (owes 5). E. Cropper, Coronation (1) v. A. Hornby, Earles- town (4). . 11. Baines, Stanley Arms (scratch), v. R. Brad- ley, Rainford Recs. (5). P. Mercer, Eccleston (3) v. E. Naylor, Rain- hill (3).</p>
        <p>E. Pryce, Thatto Heath (3) v. J. Talbot, Earles- town (6). J. Prestwich, Promenade (3) v. T. Ball, Wigan Subscription (4). J. Heath, Roebuck (4) v. A. P. Smith, Shrews- bury (5). N. Fielding, Warrington Cemetery (4) v. W. Ley- land, Wigan Subscription (5). F. Dale, Warrington Cemetery (4) v. J. Poulson, Newton-le-Willows (4). E. Morgan, Promenade (owes 1) v. J. W. Ward, Royal Excelsior (scratch). W. Lea, Park (1) v. J. T. Hilton, Wigan Sub. (1)• T. C. Jones, Promenade (owes 1) v. R. Sander- son, Park (owes 2). Byes.—B. Waddell, Park (owes 3); J. Roby. Rainhill (1); and T. Golbourn, Litherland (5). Players not present at 12.30 will be scratched.</p>
        <p>About £450 has been contributed to the local relief fund for the sufferers from the fire at the village of Little Chesterford (Essex) last week, when eleven houses were burned down. The fifty people whooccupied them said that they would rather not recgive help from other parts of the country.</p>
