      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0276" n="art0276">
        <head>FRE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 18, 1914.</head>
        <p>FRE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 18, 1914.</p>
        <p>trany, an you wou t, tor DIUW OUL kat, candle." _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Adams surrendered, and tottered to the door. They passed out, and through the vaulted kitchen, and along the slate-flagged corridor—very slowly here, for a draught fluttered the candle flame, and Mr. Jope had to shield it with a shaking palm. Once with a hoarse " What's that ?" Mr. Adams halted and cast himself into a posture of defence— against his own shadow, black and amor- phous, wavering on the wall. They came to the iron-studded door. 'Open, you," commanded Mr. Jope under his breath. " And not too fast, mind—there was a breeze o' wind blowin' this arteondon. Steady does it—look out for the step, an' then straight forw—" A howl drowned the last word, as Mr. Adams struck his shin against some obstacle and pitched headlong into darkness—a howl of pain blent with a dull jarring rumble. Silence followed, and out of the silence broke a faint groan. " Bill! 13511 Adams !—Oh, Bill, for the Lord's sake—!" Still mechanically shielding his candle, Mr. Jope staggered back a pace, and leaned against the stone door-jamb for support. Here !" sounded the voice of Bill, very faint in the darkness. " Here! fetch along the light, quick!" " Wot is it ?</p>
        <p>" Casks." " Casks ?" •• Kegs, thee. I ought to know," respond- ed Bill plaintively, " seem' as I pretty near broke my leg on one !" Mr. Jope peered forward holding the light high. In the middle of the cellar stood the quarter puncheon, and around it a whole regiment of small barrels. Half doubting his eyesight he stooped to examine them. Around each keg was bound a sling of rope. " Rope ? " muttered Mr. Jope stooping. "Foreign rope—left-handed rope—" And with that of a sudden he sat down on the nearest keg and began to laugh. "The old varmint! the darnel old sinful methodeerin' varmint !"</p>
        <p>Oh, stow it, Ben! 'Tien' manly." But still the unnatural laughter continued. "What in thunder—" Bill Adams came groping between the kegs. Step an' bar the outer door, ye nine-mil' ! Can't you see? There's been a run o' goods; an' while that Coyne sat stuffin' us up with his ghosts, his boys were down below here loadin' us up with net furrin' sperrits— loadia' us un, rnark yeti My blessed word, the fun we". :lave ST 1' hat Coyne to-mor- row!"</p>
        <p>Mr. _A a..D, in a m,,,r,,tal fog, groped his way t 4,3 r openitlg on the river stops, bolted r''t hL way back and etood scrat im, ni hc-vi A grin, grotesque in the —^".°*•!_„ ,•:;nt, contorted th 4 ong • low" hail of the face for a moment a.,1 He seldom smiled.</p>
        <p>"On the whole," said Mr. 4db,... eating the kegs, " I fancy tiles% .Jecter 'n the nak:d objects upstairs. Suppose we apend the rest o' the night here ? It's easier," he added, " than runnin' to and fro for the drink. But what, about -mu not accumy, 2"latin</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0277" n="art0277">
        <head>LOVERS' SUPERSTITIONS.</head>
        <p>LOVERS' SUPERSTITIONS.</p>
        <p>The maker of proverbs has left it on record that Love is blind, and as blindness and darkness are supposed to be particularly the breeding ground of superstition it is per- haps no wonder that lovers in all countries have developed quite a world of strange be- liefs and practices peculiar to themselves. The things that it is lucky or unlucky to do or not to do begin with the first dawn of love's young dream and end apparently, only with life itself. The first matter, if there be any reasonable doubt, is to discover the name of the lover. The Greeks consulted a kind of clock. They drew a circle on the floor and divided it into as many sectors as there were letters in the alphabet. On each letter they placed a grain of corn and the first hungry bird that hopped in, in search of nourishment, picked up the grains that took his fancy and in this way spelled out the name of the future bride or bridegroom as the case might be. In the Middle Ages, if a girl agitated the contents of a bucket of spring water with her hand, or threw broken eggs over the head of another person, she would see the image of the man she was going to marry. The latter method of divination must often have had serious and unexpected results in the case of maidens of uncertain aim. ST. JOHN'S EVE.</p>
        <p>St. John's Eve was a specially appropriate time on which to consult the oracles because, on that evening, the souls of all people left their bodies and wandered to the place where they should be finally liberated from their home of clay. Hence, in many cases, young men came to visit those who would be their partners through life. An unmarried woman could sometimes find out her fate by sitting fasting at midnight with some bread and cheese upon a table in front of her. She left the door of the house wide open and at the 'Witching hour' the spirit of her destined spouse entered and con- sumed the homely supper provided for him. Others gathered some of the hard black dead roots,often found under the living mug- wort and placed it under their pillows; they then dreamt of their lovers. In the middle of last century there was a paper called the "Connoisseur," one issue of which contained the following, "I and my sister tried the dumb cake together; you must know two must make it, two bake it, two break it, and the third put it under each of their pillows, (but you must not speak .a word all the time), and then you will dream of the man you are to have. This we did; and, to be sure. I did nothing all night but dream of Mr. Blossom. The same night, exactly at twelve o'clock, I sowed hemp-seed in our back-yard, and said to myself, "Hemp-seed I sow, hemp-seed I hoe, and he that is my true love come after me and mow." Will you believe me? I looked back and saw him as plain as eyes can see him Our maid Betty tells me, if I go backwards, without speaking a word, into the garden upon Midsummer Eve, and gather a rose, and keep it in a clean sheet of paper, without looking at it till Christmas Day, it will be as fresh as in June; and if I then stick it in my bosom, he that is to be my hus- band will come and take it mit." 'lO PROVE WHETHER LOVERS ARE TRUE OR FALSE.</p>
        <p>Having got your lover, either by the exer- cise of magic rites or natural - attractions the next thing that deserves attention is the possibility of discovering whether he is true or false. In this species of divination the plant known as St. John's Wort is invalu- able. You set it upright in a lump of clay and place the clay on a• piece of slate. In the morning you observe whether the plant has inclined to the right or the left; if to the right, there has been no falling away from the paths of fidelity, but if to the left, the lover is false. Our forefathers called these sprigs of St. John's Wort Midsummer Men. The primrose has also its uses in this di- rection. The flower must be plucked and the tops of the stamens cut off with a pair of scissbrs. The flower is next hidden and the young man has to think of his sweetheart all the next day and dream of her all the next night. If his passion in strong enough or IT evening meal disturbing enough to permit him to observe the conditions, he may again examine the delicate little blossom, and if the stamens have grown once more to their orig- inal height the lady of his choice is certainly true.</p>
        <p>To keep parted lovers constant the Irish split a sixpence in two and the halves are treasured, one by each of tlie separated pair. As long as these fragments of silver are kept safe and sound there will be no falling away on either side.</p>
        <p>The Siamese have a curious superstition as to the suitability to each other, of the per- sons who wish to become engaged. Each year is under the dominion of a certain ani- mal such as the Dog, the Cat, and the Rat. It is the business of the astrologers to decide whether ,a man born in a certain year may. with safety, wed a maiden born in some other year. It may be said that these astrolog-ers arc very obliging persons and for sufficient financial consideration will generally declare that there are no just causes or impedi- menta.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0278" n="art0278">
        <head>EXAMINER STORY.</head>
        <p>EXAMINER STORY.</p>
        <p>rA.LL RIGHTB RESERVED.]</p>
        <p>THE MULTIPLYING CELLAR.</p>
        <p>SIR A. T. QUILLER-COUCII (Author of "Dead Man's Rock," "Troy Town," " The Splendid Spur." etc.).</p>
        <p>St. Dilp by Tamar hides itself amid cherry- orchards in the windings of a creek above Hamoaze, where the war-ships lie. The war- ships have mightily altered in these hundred years; but St. Dilp has altered very little. As the village street is to-day, so—or pretty much so—it was on a certain warm afternoon in the early summer of 1807, when two wea- ther-tanned seamen of His Majesty's Fleet came along it with a hand-barrow and a huge cask very cunningly lashed thereto. On their way they eyed the cottages and gardens to rig.... and left with a lively curiosity; but "lord, Bill," said the shorter seaman, mis- quoting Wordsworth unawares, "the very houses look asleep!" At the Punch-Bowl Inn, kept by J. Coyne, they halted by silent consent. The taller sea- man, who had been trundling the barrow, set it down, and the shorter one—whose good- natured face would have recommended him anywhere—walked into the drinking-parlour and rapped on the table. This brought to him the innkeeper's daughter, Miss Elizabeth, twenty years old and comely. "What can I do for you, sir ?" she asked. " Two pots o' beer, first-along," said the seaman. "Two ?"</p>
        <p>"I got a shipmate outside." Miss Elizabeth fetched the two pots. "Here, Bill!" he called, carrying one to the door. Returning he blew at the froth on his own pot meditatively. "And the next thing is, I want a house." "A house ?" " 'Stonishing echo you keep here . 'Yes, o' the Vesuvius bomb, bos'un; but paid off inisg, a house. My rwiie's Jope—Ben Tope— at eight this mornin. friend outside goes by the name of Bill Adams; an' you'll find him livelier than he looks. Everyone does. But I forgot; you ha'a't seen him yet, and he can't come in, havin' to look after the cask." "The ca—" Miss Elizabeth had almost repeated the word, but managed to check herself. "you ought to consult someone about it, at your age," said Mr. Tope solicitously. "Yes, the cask. Rum it is, an' a quarter pun- cheon. Bill and me clubbed an' bought it off the purser las' night, the chaplain havin' ad- vised us not to waste good prize-money ashore but invest it in something we really wanted. But I don't know if you've ever noticed how often one thing leads to another. You can't go drink-in' out a quarter-puncheon 0? rum in the high road, not very well. So the flex' thing is, we want a house." said the girl, "who ever heard of a house to let hereabouts!" Mr. Tope's face fell. " Ain't there none ? An' it seemed so retired, too, an' handy near Plymouth." There's not a house to let in St. Dilp parish, unless it be the Rectory." Mr. Tope's face now suddenly brightened. "Then we'll take the Rectory," he said. " Where is it ?"</p>
        <p>"Down by the river . . . But 'tis nonsense you're telling'. The Rectory indeed! Why, its a seat!"</p>
        <p>Mr. Jope's face clouded. " Oh," he said, "is that all ?"</p>
        <p>" It's a bra' fine one, too." " It'd have to be, to ackomydate Bill an me an' the cask. I wanted a house, as I thought I told ye." "Oh, but I meant a country-seat," ex- plained Miss Elizabeth. " The Rectory is a house."</p>
        <p>- Again Mr. .Tope's face brightened. "An' so big," she went on, "that the Rector can't afford to live in it. That's why it's to let. The rent's forty pound." "Can I see him ?"</p>
        <p>"No; you can't; for he lives up to Lun- nou an' hires Parson Spettigew to do the work. But it's my father has the lettiu' o' the Rectory if a tenant comes along. He keeps the keys." "Then I'd like to talk with your father." " No you wouldn't," said the girl frankly; "cos he's asleep. Father drinks a quart o' cider at three o'clock every day of his life, an' no one don't dare disturb him before six."</p>
        <p>" Well, I like reggilar habits," said Mr. Jope, diving a hand into his breeches pocket and drawing forth a fistful of golden guin- eas. " But couldn't you risk it ?" Miss Elizabeth:a eyes wavered. "No, I couldn'," she sighed, shaking her head. "Father's very velent in his temper. But I tell you what," she added, " I might fetch the keys and you might go an' see the place for yourself." "Capital," said Mr. Jope. While she was fetching these he finished his beer. Then, baying insisted on paying down a guinea for earnest-money, he took the keys and her dir- ections for finding the house. She repeated them in the porch for the benefit of the taller seaman; who, as soon as she had concluded, gripped the handles of his barrow afresh and set off without a word. She gazed after the pair as they passed down the street. At the foot of it a bye-lane branched off towards the creek-side. It led them past a churchyard and a tiny church almost smoth- ered in cherry-trees—for the churchyard was half an orchard: past a tumbling stream, a mill and some wood stacks; anct so, still winding downwards, brought them to a pair of iron gates, rusty and weather-greened. The gates stood unlocked; and our two sea- men found themselves next in a carriage drive along which it was plain no carriage had passed for a very long while. It was overgrown with weeds, and straggling laur- els encroached upon it on either hand; and as it rounded one of these laurels Mr. Jope caught his breath sharply. "Lor' lumme!" he exclaimed. "It is a seat, as the gel said!" Mr. Adams, following close with the wheel- barrow, set it down, stared, and said," Then she's a liar. It's a house." " It's twice the size of a three-decker any- way," said his friend, and together they stood and contemplated the building. It was a handsome pile, set in a foundation of rock almost overhanging the river—on which, however, it turned its back; in de- sign an oblong of two storeys, with a square tower at each of the four corners and the towers connected by a parapet of freestone. The windows along the front were regular, and those on the ground-floor less handsome , than those of the upper floor, where (it ap- reared) were the state-rooms. For--strangest ature of all—the main entrance was in tliis upper storey, with a dozen broad steps lead- ing down to the unkempt carriage way and a lawn, across which a magnificent turkey aock threw dark masses of shadow. The masonry was of brickwork, ancient and licheped. Its surface, where the clamber- ing ivy lift it visible, seemed to drink in the afternoon sunshine as though it were pott able gold. A haze of gold, too, lay on the finargthue; tsohworaerdosrifvopeuTat; Tamar where the landscape tltuiratanßltlnepuerstge littered nrtathsseelite h ehs , unfolded its background in long slopes roll- sohsdupursetteitraselfblwo blocked e ad picturethe windows;ofdeeay. the main roof and edsfmroienmarthsit steps and parapet; dislodged slates dust crrn evices en- trance steps eoflac louvers were all in ruin, some hanging by a nail, others blown on edge by long-past gales. The very nails had rusted out of the walls and the creepers they should have sup- Ported hung down in ropy curtaicc. Mr. Adams like to scratched his head. " What I'd know," said h to get thee after a while, '9n how cask up them steps." ere 11 be a cellar -door f Jope assured him chee-rrr or sartin," Mr. suppose the gentry take "You don't front, hey ?'' s their beer in at the " This,' said Mr. Adams, "is rum; which is a totally different thing." But heniset d ' his barrow, albeit reluctantly and fo 11"eAn his shipmate up the entrance' steps. (73 front, door was massive and sheeted over with</p>
        <p>lead embossed in foliate and heraldic pat- terns. Mr. Jope inserted the key with some difficulty, turned it, and pushed . the door wide. It opened immediateiy upon the great hall, and after a glance within he removed his hat.</p>
        <p>The hall, some fifty feet long, ran right across the waist of the house, and was lit by tall windows at either end. Its floor was of black and white marble in lozenge pattern. Three immense chandeliers depended from its roaf. Along each of the two unpierced walls, against panels of peeling stucco, stood a line of statuary—heathen goddesses, fauns, ath- letes and gladiators, with here and there a vase or urn copied from the antique. The furniture was scanty, and consisted of half- a-dozen chairs, a settee, and an octagon table, all carved out of wood in pseudo-classi- cal patterns, and painted with a grey wash to resemble stone.</p>
        <p>" It's a fine room," said Mr. Jope, walking up to a statue of Diana: "but a man oouldn' hardly invite a mixed company to dinner</p>
        <p>" Symonds's f'r instance," suggested Mr. Adams. Symonds's being a somewhat notori- ous boarding house in a street of Plymouth which shall be nameless. " You ought to be ashamed o' yourself, Bill," said Mr. Jope sternly. " When you gave your word to his Reverence only yester- daY.l' ' I •wasn' hank.arin'," protested Bill. " I couldn't help Symonds's comin' into my head, that's all—wi' these figures about." " Pity you picked up so little eddication in your young days. They're anticks, that's whs., they are. Mr. Adams drew a long breath. ' I shouldn' wonder," he said. " Turnin"em wi' their faces to the wall 41 look too marked," mused Mr. Jope. "But a few tea' o' Scripture along the walls might ease things down a bit." . _</p>
        <p>" Wot about the hold ?" Mr. Adams sug- gested. " The cellar, you mean. Let's have a looK."</p>
        <p>They passed through the hall; thence down a stone stairway into an ample vaulted kitchen, and thence along a slate-flagged corridor flanked by sculleries, larders and other kit;chen offices. The two seamen searched the floors of air in hope of finding a cellar trap or hatchway, and Mr. Adams PvIS still searching when Mr. Jope called to 10. Lu from the end of the corridor:</p>
        <p>- Here we be, Bill !" He had found a flight of steps worthy of a cathedral crypt, leading down to a stone archway. The archway was closed by an iron-ttudded door. " It's like groin' to church," commented Mr. .Tope bating his voice. " Where's the candles, Hill ?" "In the barrer long wi' the bread an' bacon."</p>
        <p>"Then step back and fetch 'em." But from the foot of the stairs Mr. Jope present- ly called up that this was unnecessary, for the door had opened to his hand—smoothly, too, and without noise; but he failed to note this, being taken aback for the moment by a strong draught of air that met him, blowing full in his face. "There's daylight here, too, of a sort," he reported: and so there was. It pierced the darkness in a lon.g thin shaft, slanting across from a doorway of which the upper panel stood open to the sky. "Funny way o' leavin' a house," he mut- tered, as he stepped across the bare cellar floor and peered forth. " Why, hullo, here's water!" 1415 cellar, in fact, stood close by Tamar's edge, with a broad postern sill actually overhanging the tide, and a flight of steps, scarcely less broad, curving up and around the south-east angle of the house. While Mr. Jope studied these and the tran- quil river flowino., all grey and twilit, at his feet, Mr. Adams had joined him and had also taken bearings. "With a check-rope," said Mr. Adams, "—and I got one in the barrer—we can lower it down-here easy." He pointed to the</p>
        <p>steps. • " Hey said Mr. Jope. "Yes, the cask-- to be sure." " Wot else P " said Mr. Adams. " An' j reckon we'd best get to work, if we're to get it housed afore dark." . They did so: but by the time they had the cask bestowed and trigged up, and had spiled it and inserted a tap, darkness had fallen. If they wished to explore the house further, it would be necessary to carry gandles; and somehow neither Mr. Jope nor Mr. Adams felt eager for this adventure. They were hungry, moreover, and decided to make their waj back to the great hall and sup. supped by the light of a couple of candles. lhe repast consisted of bread and cold bacon washed down by cold rum-and- water. At Symonds's—they gave no utter ance to this reflection, but each knew it to be in the other's mind—at Symonds's just now there would be a boiled leg of mutton with turnips, and the rum would be hot, with a slice of lemon. " We shall get accustomed," said Mr. Jope with an air of cheerfulness equally forced and false.</p>
        <p>Mr. Adams glanced over his shoulder at the statuary, and answered " yes in a loud unfaltering voice. After a short silence " he arose, opened one of the windows, re- moved a quid from his cheek, laid it care- fully on the outer sill, closed the window, and resumed his seat. Mr. Jope had pulled out a cake of tobacco, and was slicing it into small pieces with his clasp knife. " Goin' to smoke ?" asked Mr. Adams, with another glance at the Diana. "-It don't hurt this 'ere marble pavement —not like the other thing." " No," Mr. Adams contemplated the pave- ment while he, too, drew forth and filled a pipe. "A man might play a go.nie of check- ers on it; that is o' course, when no-one was lookin'. "</p>
        <p>" I been thinking," announced Mr. Jope, " over what his Reverence said about bankin' our money." "How much d'ye reckon we've got ?" " Between us Hundred an twelve pound, fourteen and six. That's after paying for rum, barrer and oddments. We could live," said Mr. Jope, removing his pipe from his mouth and pointing the stein at his friend in expository fashion—" we could live in this here house for more'n three years." "Oh," said Mr. Adams, but without en- thusiasm. "Could we now ?" " That is, if we left out the Tittles." " But we're not goin' to." "0' course not. Vittlee for two'll run away with a heap of it. And then there'll be callers."</p>
        <p>" Callers ?" Mr. Adams's face brightened. " Not the sort you mean. County folk. It's the usual thing when strangers come an' set- t:a in a place o' this size... . . But, all the same, a hundred an' twelve pound, fourteen and six is a heap: an' as I say, we got to think over bankin' it. A. man teels solid, set- tin here with money under his belt: an' yet between you and me I wouldn't mind if it was less so, in a manner o' speakin'." " Me, either."</p>
        <p>" I was wonderin' what it would feel like to wake in the night an' tell yourself that someone was rollin' up money for you like a snowball."</p>
        <p>There might be a certain amount of fris- kiness in that. But contrariwise, if you waked an told yourself the fella was runnin' off with it, there wouldn'." "Shore living folka takes tint risk an' grows accuslomed to it. 117'y loak at the ielia in charge o' this house." Where ?' asked Mr. Adams nervously. " The landlord-fella, I mean, up in the village. His. daughter said he went to sleep every arternoon, an' wouldn' be waked. How c•Juld a man afford to do that if his money w;sn' rollin' up somewhere for him ? Au' the place fairly hued with barrels o' good li- quor." " Do liquor aecumylate in the same way P" asked Mr. Adams, with sudden and lively in- terest. " No, you nincom'," began Mr. Jops, when a loud knocking on the outer door in- irrupted him. hullo!" he sank his voice. "Callers!" - -</p>
        <p>Mr. Jope went to the door. unlocked it, and opened. A heavy-shouldered, bull-necked man stood outside ia the dusk. "Good eveLin'."</p>
        <p>" Evenin'," said the strafign. "My name is Coyne, an' you must get out o' this." " I cloal see as it follows," answered Air.</p>
        <p>,</p>
        <p>Jope meditatively. "But hadn't you Letter stepinside ?" "I don't want to bandy words—" began the publican, entering as though he shoul- dered his way. " That's iught, Bill, fetch an' fill a giass for the gentleman." " No thank you . . . Well, since you have it handy. But look here: I got nothin' particu- lar to say against you two men, only you can t stop here to-night. That's straight enough, I hope, and no bones broken." ' "Straight it is,Y Mr. jope agreed: " and we'll talk o' the bones by-an'-by. Wot name, sir ?—makin' so bold."</p>
        <p>" My name's Coyne." " An' mine's Cash," Mr. Jope fumbled with the fastening of a pouch underneath his broad waist-belt. "So were well met. How much ?" Eh ?" - - • -</p>
        <p>" How much ? Accordin' to your darter 'twas forty pound a year, an' money down : but whether monthly ,or quarterly she didn' say." "It's no question of money. It's a question of you two clearin'. out, and at once. House a-fire, perhaps, an' you're break- in' it gentle ?" " I'm breakin' what I have to say as gent- ly as I can. If you don't choose to understand plain language, I must go an' fetch the con- stable."</p>
        <p>" I seen him, up at the village this after- noon, an' you'd better not. Bill, why can't ye fill the gentleman's glass p" " Because the jug's empty," answered Mr. Adams.</p>
        <p>" Then slip down to the cellar again ale fill it."</p>
        <p>" No! Mr. Coyne almost screamed it, rising from his chair. Dropping back weakly he murmured, panting, " not for me: not on any account!" His face was pale and for the moment all the aggressiveness had gone out of him. Ile lifted a hand weakly to his heart. " A sudden faintness," he groaned., closing his eyes. "If you two men had any feelin's you'd offer to see me home." " The pair of us?" asked Mr. Jope suavely. "1 scale over fourteen stone," murmured Mr. Coyne, still with his eyes closed; " an' a weight like that is no joke." Mr. Jope nodded. " You're right there; so you'd best give it over. Sorry to seem heartless, sir, but 'tis for your good: an' to walk home in your state would be a 819: when we can fix you up a bed in the house. Mr. Coyne opened his eyes, and they were twinkling vindietivaly. .%.siieep in this house ?"he exclaimed. " I wouldn't do it, not for a thousand pound!" W'y not ?" " You'll find out why not,' safe enough, afore the mornin'! Why 'twas in kindness pure kindness-1 asked the pair of ye to see me home. I wouldn't be one to stay in this house alone arter nightfall—no, or I would- n't be one to leave a dog alone here, let be a friend. My daughter didn' tell, I reckon, as this place was ha'nted ?" " Ha'nted ?" It was Mr Coyne's turn to nod. " Ha'nted: an' by females. " Oh--oh!" Mr. Adams who had caught his breath, let it escape in a long sigh of relief. "Like Symonds's," he murmured. "Not a bit like Symonds's," his friend corrected snappishly. " lie's talkin' o' dead uns—ghosts—that is, if I take your mean- in', sir ?" Again Mr_ Coyne nodded. "That's it. Ghosts."</p>
        <p>- Ge ------ " t out with you," said Mr. Adams, in- credulous.</p>
        <p>" You must be a pair of very simple men, said landlord Coyne, half-closing his eyes again, "if you recky. that forty pound would rent a place like this without some drawbacks. Well, the drawbacks is ghosts; four of 'em; and all females." " Tell us about 'em, sir," requested Mr. JoPe, dropping into his seat. "An' if Bill don't believe in 'em, he can fill up his time by takin' the jug an' steppin' down to the cellar."</p>
        <p>Damned if I do," said Mr. Adams. stealing a glance over his shoulder at the statues. __</p>
        <p>It's a distressin' story," began Mx- 'Coyne with a very slight flutter of the eye- lids. "Maybe my daughter told you—an' if she didn't, you may have found out for your- selves—as how this here house is properly speakin' four houses—nothing in common but the roof, an' the cellar, an' this Teem we're sittin' in . . . Well then, back along there lived an old Rector here, with a man- servant called Oliver. One day he rode up to Exeter, spent a week there, an' brought home a wife. Oliver was ready at the door to receive 'em, an' the pair went upstairs to a fine set o' rooms he'd made ready in the sou' West tower, an' there for a whole month they lived together, as you might say, in wedded happiness. "At th' end o' the month th' old Rector discovered he had business takin' him to Bristol. He said his farewells very lovin'ly, promised to come back as soon as he could, but warned the poor lady against setting foot outside the doors. The gardens an' fields (he said) swarmed with field mice, an' he knew she had a terror of mice of all sorts. So off he rode an' by-an'-by came back by night With a second young lady: and Oliver showed 'em up to the nor' east tower for the honeymoon. " A week later my gentleman had a call to post down to Penzance. He warned his second wife that it was a terrible year for adders an' the ground swarmin' with 'em, for he knew she had a horror o' snakes. In- side of a fortnight he brought home a third—" "Bill," said Mr. Jope, sitting up sharply. "What noise was that ?" "I didn't hear it," answered Mr. Adams. "Adders maybe." " Seemed to me it sounded from some- wheres in the cellar. Maybe you wouldn't mind steppin' down, seem' as you don't take no interest in what Mr. Co'ne's tellin'." " I'm beginning to." The cellar's the worst place of all," said Mr. Coynes blinking. "It's there that the bodies were found." " Bodies ?" "Bodies. 'Pour of ,em. I was gob' to tell you how he brought home another, havin' kept the third poor lady to her rooms with some tale about a mad (log starvin' to death in his shrubberies—he didn't know where—" "If you don't mind," Mr Jope interposed. "I've a notion to hear the, rest o' the story some other eeeniee It's—it's agreeable enough to bear spiunin' out, an' I under- stand you're a fixture in this neighbour- hood." Certainly," said Mr. 'Coyne, rising. "But wot about you ?" "I'll-111 'tell von to-morrow." Mr. Jope gripped the arms Of his chair, having uttered the bravest speech of his life. He sat for a while, the sound of his own voice echoing strangely in his ears, even when Mr. Coyne rose to fake his leave. " Well, I can't help admirin' you," raid Mr- Coyne handsomely. "By the way the rent's by the quarter, an' in advance—fours into forty is ten; I mention it as a matter business and in case we don't meet again., ef Mr. Jope counted out the money. " When Mr. Coyne had taken his departure the, pair sat a long while in silence, their solitary candle flickering on the table be- tween them. You spoke out very bold," ams at length. said Mr. Ad- " Did I said Mr, Jope. " I didn't feel it. " What cuts me to the quick is the thought o' them adders outside." " Ye dolt! There ain't no real adders out- side. They're what the chap invented to frighten the women." " Sure ? Then," mused Mr. Adams, after is pause, " maybe there ain't no real ghosts nenher, but he invented the whole thing." " Maybe. What d'ye say to steppin down an' fetchin' up another mugful 0' liquor ?" ' I say," answered Mr. Adams slowly, "as how I won't." " Tess for it," suggested Mr. Jope. " Yott refuse ? Very well then I must go. On', I thought better of ye,Bill—ldid indeed.' "I can't help what ye thought," Mr. Adams began sulkily; and then, as his friend rose with the face of a man who goes to meet the worut he Reran up quaking. "Lord's sake, Ben Jope! You ain't a-goin' to take the candle an' leave me!" " Bill Adams," said 'Mr. Jope with fine so- lemnity, "if I was to put a name on your besettin' sin, it would be selfishness—an' you can just sit here in the dark an' think it over."</p>
        <p>"When I was on the p'int of offering to go with ye!" " Ho ! Wos you ? Very well, then, I accept the offer, an' you can walk first." "But I don't see—" " Another word" announced Mr. JoPo</p>
