        <head>ST. ANN'S EFFORT.</head>
        <p>ST. ANN'S EFFORT.</p>
        <p>Success Attends the Sale of</p>
        <p>Work.</p>
        <p>MRS. HYLANDS AND SUCH FUNCTIONS.</p>
        <p>Willing workers from St. Ann's Parish Church launched upon a two-days' sale of work on Wednesday, and happily their efforts were re- sponsible for reaching the zenith of their ex- pectations. The stalls groaned undor heavy burdens, which were considerably lightened before the first day of the enterprise came to a close. A peep through the door of the refresh- ment room forcibly reminded one of a first-class cafe, for here and there ladies and gentlemen assembled at small tables sipping tea and enjoy- ing light refreshments. Interest did not cease there, because at frequent intervals musical en- tertainments were given by Mr. W. 11. Rigby and his "Morrie Dandies."</p>
        <p>There was an encouraging attendance at the first day's opening ceremony, which was per- formed by Mrs. lands, of Thelwall, the Vicar, the Rev. Tames Davies, presiding. Amongst those present were the Revs. 11. V. Pigot and E. C. E. Carleton. THE OPENING.</p>
        <p>The Vim, said that last Easter they held a sale of work, which was the first for 18 years, and they were all very pleased with the result. ['hey had made very good use of the money, as the frequenters of the church would know, for in addition to the reredos, (to., in the chancel, the church had undergone a transformation in the hands of the decorators. Certain bills had yet to come in, and when they did it was be- lieved they would have sufficient money to pay them, and to leave a little Over to be carried to the curacy and lay readers' fm.,;s. (Applause.) It was absolutely necessary tnat they should have a sale of work, because they, as the parish- ioners knew, had lost a grant, so that they were compelled to seek outside help. There were about 10,000 people in the parish, and amongst these there were no multi-millionaires, semi- millionaires, of any other kind of millionaires— !laughter)—so that it was necessary to receive outside help. (Applause.) FAVOURABLE TO SUCH SALES.</p>
        <p>MRS. RYLANDS was cordially received. She said there were a good many people who did not look favourably upon sales of work, hit really there was a lot to be said in their favour. She always thought that when men and women worked together for sales i.f That kind, it must iacrease their interest and sympathy in the work of the parish when they felt they had done practical work. They could not p, ssibly raise sufficent money entirely in the parish, so there did not seem any other way than to solicit the help of others, so on that account sales of work were necessary things. Mr. T. S. JOLLEY proposed, and Mr. W. THOMAS seconded, a vote of thanks to Mrs. Rylands. The VICAR announced that he had received the following donations: Lady Greenall, .225; Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co., .25; the Rev. Canon Penrhyn, .22; the Mayor, .21 is.; the Deputy-Mayor, .21 is.; lion. Mrs. Wilson- Patten, .21 Is.; Lord Wolmer, Is.; Mrs. John Crosfield, .21; Mr. Smith, Liverpool, .21; Mr. 'Harold Smith, M.P., 10s. 6d.; Mrs. Earle, 10s.; Miss Burrow, ss. In addition to this there was the paper money, which Would considerably enhance the total. THE STALL-HOLDERS.</p>
        <p>The stall-holders were : Vicarage Stall: Miss Davies, Miss Bunch. and Mesdames H. Corbett and Tolley. Church Stall: Mesdames Glover, Savage, Har- rison and Thomas.</p>
        <p>Sunday School Stall: Misses S. Evans, L. Evans, M. Barton, M. J. Kenyon, G. Kenyon, H. Painter, N. Allender; Mesdames Ward, R. Smith, G. Parker, Hackett and Daniels. Crockery Stall: Mrs. Gee. Sweet Stall: Miss L. Glover. Flower and Fruit Stall: Miss Bradshaw and Aiss D. Jolley.</p>
        <p>General Stall: Messrs. W. Thomas, A. Brad- shaw and A. Catterall.</p>
        <p>Advertisement Stall: The Vicar and Mr. J. Savage.</p>
        <p>Refreshment Department and Pound Stall: Alesdames Rigby, Gorton, Laine, Downey and Ward, and Misses Cuppage, Green, Fryer, Broadhurst, Cooper and E. Blackburn. Bran Tub: Mrs. Roberts and Miss Ida Smith. Ice Cream Stand: Miss M. Johnson. "Curios and Competitions": Messrs. F. W. Bradshaw and Charles Glover.</p>
        <p>The stalls were erected by Messrs. Peter Walker and Sons, through the kindness of ‘fessrs. Hutton and Mercer.</p>
        <p>OVER HALF' A CENTURY AGO. Mrs. Melville, of Appleton, who shoul d p ,ot hp es enne td, tahned sat hl ee on VicarT 1 i u rp se dr af ey r, mwe was the a veoi ed have na 12 nl gy ieremony in her stead. us IfiaothVelrCAwßerseaidun3wlerlsl.. Mely mrsi.ll's husbandmeeivine,ai and a.the i.- n-law was the first vicar of St. Ann's, and Mrs. lelville had received se from t it.ein he be r following father-in-lawletter, which }le had vas greatly pleased to hear that you were going to open a. bazaar at St. Ann's, War- rington, enstito.n Antnhast damteys ~l'ainrglyton,assncwiahteinena co far back as January 11th, 1862, when I began 7ny ministrations in an old chapel used before that by a body of Methodists, who, having no further use for it, let it to me on a lease for six mars at an annual rent of -a. That was my 3hurch, licensed by the bishop, until the new cchools were built, and the bishop's licence ,ransferred from the old chapel to the new ')uildings. The school served for every purpose 'until the present fine church was built and uonsecrated in 1869. You may easily under- :lmnd bow I look upon St. Ann's as my first love. We lived most of the time in St. Ann's parish, ,re four children were born. You may well 'understand how severe a wrench it was to tear myself away even for so important: a sphere as that of Ashton Rectory. It seems a revival of ,he old clays that you should be doing something 4m help the parish with which I was connected for, I may say, nearly eight years of the most 'ventful period of my life, and I don't exagger- lte when I add the most interesting. lam zlad to say I am somewhat better, but not yet very vigorous."</p>
        <p>The Vicar said he was sure they all wished that both gentlemen would soon be restored to tealth, and he would send a letter to that effect m their behalf. The result of the previous 'lay's sale was ..278 15s. 10d., and with subscrip- ' ions there was a total sum of £llB ss. 4d. They lincerely thanked all those who had helped. c.ast year they received goods from Canada, Ceylon and U.S.A., and this year they had re- ceived articles from the United States. lfr. T. S. JOLLY submitted the detailed finan- -ial statement, and the Vicar opened the sale. The takings for Thursday amounted to .240 Os. Tid., and together with subscriptions since received, the total amount is £l6O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0281" n="art0281">
        <head>LAXATIVE FOR OLD PEOFLEKIMOC BLOOD TONIC.</head>
        <p>LAXATIVE FOR OLD PEOFLE- KIMOC BLOOD TONIC.</p>
        <p>Salts, Calomel and Pills act on Liver and Rowels like pepper acts in Nostrils—DanUerl</p>
        <p>Most old people must give the bowels some regular help, else they suffer from constipation. The condition is perfectly natural. It is lust as natural as it is for old people to walk slowly. , So all old people need Kimoe Blood Tonic. One might as well refuse to aid weak eyes with glasses as to neglect this gentle aid to weak bowels. The bowels must be kept active. This is important at all ages, but never so much as at fifty. Age is not a time for harsh physics. Youth may occasionally whip the bowels into activity. But a lash can't be used every day. What the bowels of the old need is a gentle and natural , tonic. One that can be constantly nsed with- out harm. The only such is Kimoe Blood Tonic at your chemist.</p>
      </div>
