        <p>slip to a minimum, have been beiore the public from time to time and then disappeared. How- ever, a Taunton alderman seems to have per- fected a' new type of motor wheel, which has proved to be as near perfection as possible. Its construction is quite unique, consisting of a series of rubber pads, arranged on the felloes, to absorb vibration, while the actual tread if of special blocks of wood cut out to enable the wheel to grip any surface no matter how smooth ' or greasy. A heavy vehicle equipped with one of the new wheels ws.9 subjected to a severe trial on one of the Somerset roads under the most exacting conditions and more than fulfilled the expcotations of its inventor. It held a particu- larly greasy surface, where the other three wheels skidded violently; then on the turf by the roadside it was the only one which gripped at all. Another great advantage is the facility for repair, and if early tests are any criterion for future performance the new wheel should have a prosperous future. It will most likely be limited to commercial vehicles owing to its lesser resiliency. than the ordinary tyre.</p>
