        <p>ROMAN PILLARS AT CHESTER. While workmen were excavating beneath a shop in Bridge-street Row, Chester, adjacent to the site of the Old Feathers Inn, they unearthed a number of pillars, which undoubtedly formed part of an old Roman hypocaust or bath. The columns, which are hewn out of old red sand- stone, have probably been in the position in which they were found since the Roman occupa- tion: In the rear of the shop beneath is an old Roman bath intact, which is an object of great interest to arclueologists, and the pillars which have been discovered have no doubt formed part of the same structure. The pillars will go to enrich the splendid collection of Roman re- mains in the Grosvenor Museum.</p>
